\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Falling, Weak Japan Stocks A Cue\b0\par\par\uc2 1,624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 08:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0610 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY, EUR/JPY falling as European banks selling, taking cue from Japanese share prices, which fell into negative territory after giving up all gains in morning session, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. USD/JPY last 83.36, says target at 82.90, where \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Adds, not expecting MOF action at that level as its money to conduct JPY-selling limited, MOF may act at psychological 82.50 level. Says attention on BOJ meeting ending tomorrow; many analysts expect additional easing measures. But dealer disagrees with mainstream view; says ahead of Friday's U.S. jobs data release, "tomorrow isn't the best timing for the BOJ to act." If that's the case, dealers say USD/JPY may fall sharply on disappointment. EUR/JPY last at 114.70, tipped support at 114.20. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0607 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares hit 8-month high of 8300.04 earlier but end flat at 8246.10 on profit-taking, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. Expects Taiex to condoliate in 8200-8300 range Tuesday. TWD strength "also weighed on electronics exporters such as TSMC (2330.TW) and HTC (2498.TW)." TSMC off 0.2% at NT$61.90, HTC down 1.3% at NT$698.00, \uc2 Nan Ya Plastics\uc2  (1303.TW) down 1.5% at NT$67.50 after fire hits company's plant Sunday, which will cause estimated NT$150 million in revenue losses, according to company's spokesman. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei down 0.3%, S&P/ASX 200 +1.0%, Kospi +0.1%, HSI +1.4%, STI +1.1%, KLCI +0.4%, SET +0.2%, NZX-50 +0.5%, Taiex flat, PSEi +1.9%, Sensex +0.6%. Markets in China closed for holiday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY weaker vs USD, on speculation BOJ will loosen monetary policy further at its Tuesday meeting; may expand programs to pump more cash into money market. USD/JPY at 83.40 vs 83.32 in late NY trade; EUR/JPY at 114.71 vs 114.83, EUR/USD at 1.3753 vs 1.3784. Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rebounded in September from previous month to hit highest level on record; reserves totaled $289.78 billion, topping previous high of $285.96 billion set in July; reserves rose by $4.42 billion from August, when reserves fell by $600 million on-month. Spot gold at $1,316.50/oz, down $2.10 cents vs NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 19 cents at $81.39/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0552 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 0.3% at 9360, intraday low, on likely selling by foreign investors, Japanese brokerage manager says. "The market is going to be volatile this month as funds' earnings (reports) are coming up in November," he says. Adds funds may choose to focus on short-term trading to secure profits quickly for October. Puts index support at 25-day moving average of 9300. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Speculation increasing BOJ will introduce additional easing measure by expanding its fund-provision programs at its 2-day meeting ending Tuesday. Such move has already been priced into current level, meaning if BOJ's action meets view, investors resume selling USD/JPY on "buy-on-rumor, sell-on-fact", dealers say. Pair last 83.51; possible for USD/JPY to fall below 83.00 in near term; focus now on whether Japan steps into market again when USD/JPY below 83.00. "If it doesn't act there, investors may start buying the yen massively," says Shinichi Hayashi, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.2% at 980.65 at midday pause in active trade. Rises dominated by firmer energy heavyweights, which drive benchmark to fresh 986.75 intrasession high in 14 years, though profit-taking trims gains. "There's no negative cue but just that baht is so strong (against the dollar). So, some investors are cashing in profit as they think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not hike (at Oct 20 meeting) as previouly expected," says SCB Securities Senior Vice President Sukit Udomsirikul. Adds approaching technical resistance of 990 also prompts some selling. Support put at 975. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +2.7% at THB305, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +3.2% at THB163 though off from almost 1-year-high of THB163.50, Thai Oil (TOP.TH) +4.6% at THB56.50 though off from almost 28-month-high of THB57, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.5% at THB137. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] India call rate under pressure as banking system likely past peak of cash tightness as government spending likely to pump cash into system, says trader with state-run bank; overnight call rate at 6.10% vs 6.60% late Friday. "We're not seeing banks borrowing more than INR400 billion at the repo window, which could pressure the call rate later in the session," says trader. CBLO, now 5.99% also likely to ease, with slip to 5.25% likely, adds trader. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 22.1% at HK$2.686 billion midday, 5.2% of total market volume vs 7.3% at Thursday's half-day, suggesting bears deterred by HSI's rally this morning, setting 2010 high. Hutchison (0013.HK) tops list with HK$300.06 billion, as stock appears in bad need of breather amid 6-session, 14.5% rally. Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) in second place with HK$106.95 million, then Cnooc (0883.HK) with HK$106.73 million, Henderson Land (0012.HK) with HK$70.38 million, \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$67.80 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil listless as bulls take break after pushing November contract to seven-week high last session; with fundamentals still showing weakness, current prices may be overvalued and selling pressure from profit taking may emerge in coming weeks, says Ben Westmore, minerals and energy economist at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 ; adds oil should be priced around mid- or high-$70s/barrel, judging from current supply-demand fundamentals, as demand for crude still "flat." November contract down 16 cents at $81.42/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  tips Bank of Korea to remain on hold at this month's rate setting meeting (October 14), as global uncertainty hasn't receded since last rate review, also amid governor's complete silence ahead of this month's meeting; "while U.S. PMI numbers were encouraging, data on consumer confidence, unemployment, and the housing market leaves much to be desired...there are few signs that the property measures (Korean government's relaxation last month of prudential property regulations) are working which will keep up the pressure on the BOK to remain accommodative," says RBS economist Erik Lueth. Maintains call for 25 bps hikes each in 4Q10, 1Q11, 3Q11, with immediate rate increase likely to happen in November as there has never been hike in month of December. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0536 GMT [Dow Jones] ECM Libra introduces 2011-end target of 1,650 for KLCI based on 15X mid-cycle valuation on CY12 earnings forecast; has Hold view on market; "Should the current liquidity-driven rally continue into 2011, end-2011 (KLCI) could potentially touch 1,870 based on 17X P/E." But says it doesn't expect that valuation to sustain, especially if it is reached in early part of 2011, amid expected slowdown of corporate earnings growth. House also says liquidity-driven rally could continue in 4Q 2010 with big-cap cyclical stocks likely to outperform small-cap, defensive yield stocks. Says Malaysia, other ASEAN countries will continue to attract net equity inflows driven by robust economic growth, while "advanced economies teeter on the brink of a double-dip recession." Expects Budget 2011 to have neutral impact on equity market "as market has largely priced in news flow from government's earlier announcements of subsidy cut, government transformation program, economic transformation program and 10th Malaysia Plan." Says budget expected to be follow-through on previous announcements with more concrete plans for implementation; better clarity on infrastructure spending programs. KLCI last +0.4% at 1,472.30.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +1.1% at 3587.047 midday in moderate volume, helped by steady IDR, traders say; tip resistance at 3600. "The index is likely to close at new highs today (Friday's close was new record high, +1.3% at 3,547.115) though gains for banking stocks will likely be limited in the afternoon session as market awaits Bank Indonesia's views tomorrow for further cues." \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hold monetary policy meeting tomorrow, where analysts economists bank to keep benchmark rate steady at record low 6.50%. Among leading gainers, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +2.9% at IDR7,050, Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK) +3.0% at IDR10,350, on hopes for strong 3Q earnings; \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) +0.7% at IDR60,000; Telkom (TLKM.JK) +0.5% at IDR9,450. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 0210ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a40003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picsc