\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: India Bonds Slip On Nov Rate Hike Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 05:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0357 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds slip as hawkish comments in speech from RBI executive director late Friday spark speculation on potential for another rate hike at next policy meeting on November 2, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.10 vs 99.29 last close. "The market is beginning to worry about the next set of rate hikes now, and it's beginning to look like a hike in November is very probable," says trader. Adds, upbeat local stocks also reinforce concerns on possibility that RBI may see economy as overheating, hike rates aggressively for while to come. Trader tips yield on benchmark to breach 7.95% within session, 8% within week, from 7.93% now. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] Scope for lower short-end interest rate swaps seems limited as no rate hikes priced in 2010, just 150 bp hike by June 2011, says ANZ; "But the long end is a different story, with further quantitative easing in the U.S. likely to see rates grind lower over the remainder of the quarter and remain somewhat anchored." Says not surprisingly, interest rates edged lower as domestic data last week added "to the long list of sub-par economic statistics" and have gone a long way to validate RBNZ's change of heart. Adds, NZ government bond demand continues to improve, should help swap spreads continue to normalise from recent negative extremes. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.9% as institutions buy, with gains in regional markets supporting sentiment. market breadth positive with 29 of 30 index stocks trading higher; 11 issues up more than 1.0%. Gains led by \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2 (500325.BY), last +1.2% at INR1,018, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY), +1.4% at INR1,135.50. "The opening is more or less on expected lines. Select auto shares continue to witness buying interest after strong September sales," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, liquidity driving share prices higher but technically, markets in overbought zone. Tips 20,680 as resistance. Mahindra (500520.BY) +3.4% at INR737.30, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1.6% at INR678.70. ACC (500410.BY) +1.3% at INR1,013.90, Wipro (507685.BY) +1.3% at INR467.10. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says cyclical factors suggest possible rebound in near-term share prices, noting 1) 2-year cycle of chip technology is nearing a bottom, and memory makers are starting to drive an upturn in output value index; 2) 4-year election cycle in U.S. is approaching a bottom; says over the past several decades, annual performance of Dow Jones Industrial Average has been in positive territory the year after mid-term elections, and market often bottoms through October even in down years when mid-term elections are held; 3) Japanese market appears to have 3-year cycles for its long-term developments. On point 3, adds acceleration in the industrial production index is likely to see an end to downtrend, and market often rallies in second half of fiscal years when upper house elections are held. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] Regardless how RBA moves Tuesday, and not hiking is a harder case to make than tightening, there will be plenty of bond trading opportunities, note NAB Chief of Research Peter Jolly. Says if RBA hikes, market will wrongly price in more tightening in November and December. "That would be wrong in my view as in the pre-emptive game I reckon the RBA are on a 25 bps a quarter pace - "a stitch in time 