\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZ Short-End IRS Unlikely To Go Much Lower - ANZ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,644 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 05:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] Scope for lower short-end interest rate swaps seems limited as no rate hikes priced in 2010, just 150 bp hike by June 2011, says ANZ; "But the long end is a different story, with further quantitative easing in the U.S. likely to see rates grind lower over the remainder of the quarter and remain somewhat anchored." Says not surprisingly, interest rates edged lower as domestic data last week added "to the long list of sub-par economic statistics" and have gone a long way to validate RBNZ's change of heart. Adds, NZ government bond demand continues to improve, should help swap spreads continue to normalise from recent negative extremes. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.9% as institutions buy, with gains in regional markets supporting sentiment. market breadth positive with 29 of 30 index stocks trading higher; 11 issues up more than 1.0%. Gains led by \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2 (500325.BY), last +1.2% at INR1,018, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY), +1.4% at INR1,135.50. "The opening is more or less on expected lines. Select auto shares continue to witness buying interest after strong September sales," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, liquidity driving share prices higher but technically, markets in overbought zone. Tips 20,680 as resistance. Mahindra (500520.BY) +3.4% at INR737.30, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1.6% at INR678.70. ACC (500410.BY) +1.3% at INR1,013.90, Wipro (507685.BY) +1.3% at INR467.10. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says cyclical factors suggest possible rebound in near-term share prices, noting 1) 2-year cycle of chip technology is nearing a bottom, and memory makers are starting to drive an upturn in output value index; 2) 4-year election cycle in U.S. is approaching a bottom; says over the past several decades, annual performance of Dow Jones Industrial Average has been in positive territory the year after mid-term elections, and market often bottoms through October even in down years when mid-term elections are held; 3) Japanese market appears to have 3-year cycles for its long-term developments. On point 3, adds acceleration in the industrial production index is likely to see an end to downtrend, and market often rallies in second half of fiscal years when upper house elections are held. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] Regardless how RBA moves Tuesday, and not hiking is a harder case to make than tightening, there will be plenty of bond trading opportunities, note NAB Chief of Research Peter Jolly. Says if RBA hikes, market will wrongly price in more tightening in November and December. "That would be wrong in my view as in the pre-emptive game I reckon the RBA are on a 25 bps a quarter pace - "a stitch in time saves nine" for one and also because of higher funding costs there is plenty of speculation banks will add a bit extra to mortgage rates." If they don't hike, market will downgrade risk of any tightening; "Also wrong." Says sell any rally on this outcome. December 3-year spot contract last 95.09, 2 ticks weaker. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR slips to over 5-month low as upbeat local stocks pressure USD, says trader; pair at 44.31 vs 44.48 late Friday, lowest level since April 26. "The pair is approaching a major support zone at around 44.15-44.20, so some caution is in order," says trader. Notes, some traders waiting for sign of bounce, standing ready to reverse short positions very quickly on signs of stickiness. Tips pair to trade in 44.25-44.40 band in session. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.4% at 982.56 on sustained portfolio inflows, robust trade and gains in regional bourses, but off day's high of 986.75 on profit-taking. Big cap energy plays buoyant on higher crude oil price Friday, while banking plays also surge on optimism over strong 3Q earnings, expected third week of October. Arpaporn Sawaengpak, senior analyst with DBS Vickers Securities says "the Thai stock market is likely to test the important 1,000-mark level within this week." Also adds market now eyeing BOT to see if it tries to stem THB's rapid rise after USD/THB slipped to 30.16-30.18 early morning vs 30.20 late Friday; notes capital inflows may decline if capital control measures introduced. Resistance for SET index seen at 990, support at 980. Among top actives, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) last +2.5% at THB162, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.0% at THB300, IRPC (IRPC.TH) off 0.5% at THB4.38, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +2.2% at THB138, Thai Oil (TOP.TH) +2.8% at THB55.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY's gains earlier due to buying by European banks who triggered stop-loss buying orders at 83.50, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Adds, pair also bought amid Nikkei Stock Average's rise on back of speculation BOJ may ease policy at two-day meeting ending Tuesday. But says upside heavy due to selling by Japanese exporters, attention now on whether Japan will step into market if pair falls below 83.00, like it did last month. "If (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) doesn't (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), then speculators may start buying the yen massively," he says; expects USD/JPY to fall longer-term; last 83.54 vs 83.88 hit earlier. EUR/JPY falling due to profit-taking orders by speculators; last 114.94 vs 115.07 earlier, support at 114.50. EUR/USD last at 1.3759, may trade in 1.3720-1.3780 band. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0335 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options higher in Tokyo at 10.85%/11.55% vs 10.50%/11.20% Friday in New York, as players seek hedges against downside in spot amid lingering speculation Fed may ease more aggressively than BOJ, which concludes two-day policy-setting meeting Tuesday. Rise in vols comes after \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President Dudley said Friday Fed is almost certain to take further easing measures ahead as current economic conditions are "wholly unsatisfactory." Vols may trend up further this week if Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not meet or exceed market expectations for it to expand liquidity-provision measures, dealers say.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 1.6% at 444.45, with investors selling after brokerage firms said companies received permission to issue new equity valued at VND34.6 trillion in 3Q vs VND18.6 trillion in 1H, causing concerns that large volumes of new shares will result in price dilution. "In addition to weak inflows, the glut of new shares will certainly pull down prices," says brokerage trader. Volume thin with 36 million shares valued at VND840 billion traded so far. Among decliners, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 4.1% at VND30,300, Itaco (ITA.VH) down 4.2% at VND16,300, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) down 2.4% at VND16,500. Tips immediate support at 442. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed; medium-term notes quoted lower in lackluster trade as market seeks fresh cues. However, most deals executed so far in quiet morning are in long-dated tenors due to continued fund inflows, says local dealer. Adds yields of long-term bonds may inch lower on fund flow demand but price rise likely limited due to recent sharp gains. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.47%/2.44% vs 2.45%/2.42% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.70%/no offer vs 2.63%/2.58%, June 2019 at 2.73%/2.70% vs 2.78%/2.75%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting off slightly as activity subdued as parts of Australia, including Sydney, on holiday, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  senior trader Stuart Ive. Pair last 0.7434 vs 0.7443 early. Adds trigger for NZD to move higher will be if RBA decides to raise rates tomorrow: "I believe the market has a 70% chance priced in (for hike), and I think the Kiwi will be taken higher on the basis of that (a rate hike)." Adds ongoing speculation of quantitative easing in U.S. likely to continue to weigh broadly on USD; "That will stem any downward moves for any \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (vs USD) at the moment." Tips support to hold at 0.7315 with resistance at 0.7520. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] If RBA does hike Tuesday -- market 60% priced for 25 bps move -- then AUD/USD could get serious lift, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  Strategists. "Barring a scenario in which the RBA does not raise the rate and sounds dovish, a combination we do not expect, a rate hike would be a substantial boost for the AUD, in our opinion. The RBA would not begin raising rates again after being on hold for four months to fine-tune policy. So, the bills market would likely quickly move to price in a second rate hike this year, which is currently priced at only about a 20% chance." AUD/USD last 0.9704 in thin trade, Sydney shut for public holiday. (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 2356ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a40002k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
