se in vols comes after \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President Dudley said Friday Fed is almost certain to take further easing measures ahead as current economic conditions are "wholly unsatisfactory." Vols may trend up further this week if Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not meet or exceed market expectations for it to expand liquidity-provision measures, dealers say.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 1.6% at 444.45, with investors selling after brokerage firms said companies received permission to issue new equity valued at VND34.6 trillion in 3Q vs VND18.6 trillion in 1H, causing concerns that large volumes of new shares will result in price dilution. "In addition to weak inflows, the glut of new shares will certainly pull down prices," says brokerage trader. Volume thin with 36 million shares valued at VND840 billion traded so far. Among decliners, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 4.1% at VND30,300, Itaco (ITA.VH) down 4.2% at VND16,300, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) down 2.4% at VND16,500. Tips immediate support at 442. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed; medium-term notes quoted lower in lackluster trade as market seeks fresh cues. However, most deals executed so far in quiet morning are in long-dated tenors due to continued fund inflows, says local dealer. Adds yields of long-term bonds may inch lower on fund flow demand but price rise likely limited due to recent sharp gains. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.47%/2.44% vs 2.45%/2.42% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.70%/no offer vs 2.63%/2.58%, June 2019 at 2.73%/2.70% vs 2.78%/2.75%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting off slightly as activity subdued as parts of Australia, including Sydney, on holiday, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  senior trader Stuart Ive. Pair last 0.7434 vs 0.7443 early. Adds trigger for NZD to move higher will be if RBA decides to raise rates tomorrow: "I believe the market has a 70% chance priced in (for hike), and I think the Kiwi will be taken higher on the basis of that (a rate hike)." Adds ongoing speculation of quantitative easing in U.S. likely to continue to weigh broadly on USD; "That will stem any downward moves for any \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (vs USD) at the moment." Tips support to hold at 0.7315 with resistance at 0.7520. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] If RBA does hike Tuesday -- market 60% priced for 25 bps move -- then AUD/USD could get serious lift, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  Strategists. "Barring a scenario in which the RBA does not raise the rate and sounds dovish, a combination we do not expect, a rate hike would be a substantial boost for the AUD, in our opinion. The RBA would not begin raising rates again after being on hold for four months to fine-tune policy. So, the bills market would likely quickly move to price in a second rate hike this year, which is currently priced at only about a 20% chance." AUD/USD last 0.9704 in thin trade, Sydney shut for public holiday. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0320 GMT [Dow Jones] UOB KayHian pegs end-2011 target for KCLI at 1,635 based on 14.5X forward P/E; says applying conservative P/E multiple (below post-Asian financial crisis average of 15X) due to risks to corporate earnings from likely fall-off in external demand, execution risks to economic transformation programme. "The rolling-out of more mega construction projects amid lower development expenditure suggests a strong reliance and 'buy-in' from private funding." Adds banks still remain "most effective" proxies to rising investment cycle in Malaysia. Also says potential winners from Budget 2011 are Gamuda (5398.KU), WCT (9679.KU), \uc2 IJM Corp\uc2 . (3336.KU) from construction sector, \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU) from property sector; \uc2 British American Tobacco (Malaysia) (\uc2 4162.KU) key loser. PM Najib to make budget announcement on October 15. KLCI last +0.5% at 1,474.01. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered tips USD/THB to fall to 30.00 by year-end as Thailand's resilient economic fundamentals, renewed investor interest have strongly boosted THB in recent months. USD/THB last at 30.18; it hit 13-year low of 30.20 on Oct 1. House says, "amid exporters' protests that THB strength is undermining competitiveness, there are concerns that capital controls could be reintroduced to contain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation." Adds, THB strength has benefit of dampening imported inflation, subsequently allows more moderate interest rate tightening; "we remain constructive on the THB in the near term, owing to strong growth momentum as well as capital inflows; hence our short-term Overweight \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating." But notes, "a narrowing trade surplus and moderating economic growth in 2011 could stem THB gains, and we therefore maintain our medium-term Neutral \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." House tips USD/THB at 31.50 by end 2Q11. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to hit fresh 5-month low in early session trade, as upbeat EUR vs USD and likely higher local stocks set to boost local unit, says trader with foreign bank; tips pair to open in 44.35-44.40 band, vs 44.48 late Friday. But pair unlikely to slip below strong support at 44.20 as importers likely to step in to buy USD heavily at that level. Foreign funds were net sellers of a provisional INR18.26 billion worth of Indian stocks on Friday. 1-month NDF at 44.51/44.58. (bijou.george@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 2346ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a40002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip