\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Gain Earlier Due To Stop-Loss Buy Orders\b0\par\par\uc2 1,561 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 05:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0342 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY's gains earlier due to buying by European banks who triggered stop-loss buying orders at 83.50, says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Adds, pair also bought amid Nikkei Stock Average's rise on back of speculation BOJ may ease policy at two-day meeting ending Tuesday. But says upside heavy due to selling by Japanese exporters, attention now on whether Japan will step into market if pair falls below 83.00, like it did last month. "If (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) doesn't (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ), then speculators may start buying the yen massively," he says; expects USD/JPY to fall longer-term; last 83.54 vs 83.88 hit earlier. EUR/JPY falling due to profit-taking orders by speculators; last 114.94 vs 115.07 earlier, support at 114.50. EUR/USD last at 1.3759, may trade in 1.3720-1.3780 band. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0335 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options higher in Tokyo at 10.85%/11.55% vs 10.50%/11.20% Friday in New York, as players seek hedges against downside in spot amid lingering speculation Fed may ease more aggressively than BOJ, which concludes two-day policy-setting meeting Tuesday. Rise in vols comes after \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President Dudley said Friday Fed is almost certain to take further easing measures ahead as current economic conditions are "wholly unsatisfactory." Vols may trend up further this week if Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not meet or exceed market expectations for it to expand liquidity-provision measures, dealers say.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 1.6% at 444.45, with investors selling after brokerage firms said companies received permission to issue new equity valued at VND34.6 trillion in 3Q vs VND18.6 trillion in 1H, causing concerns that large volumes of new shares will result in price dilution. "In addition to weak inflows, the glut of new shares will certainly pull down prices," says brokerage trader. Volume thin with 36 million shares valued at VND840 billion traded so far. Among decliners, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 4.1% at VND30,300, Itaco (ITA.VH) down 4.2% at VND16,300, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) down 2.4% at VND16,500. Tips immediate support at 442. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0331 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed; medium-term notes quoted lower in lackluster trade as market seeks fresh cues. However, most deals executed so far in quiet morning are in long-dated tenors due to continued fund inflows, says local dealer. Adds yields of long-term bonds may inch lower on fund flow demand but price rise likely limited due to recent sharp gains. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.47%/2.44% vs 2.45%/2.42% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.70%/no offer vs 2.63%/2.58%, June 2019 at 2.73%/2.70% vs 2.78%/2.75%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD drifting off slightly as activity subdued as parts of Australia, including Sydney, on holiday, says \uc2 HIFX\uc2  senior trader Stuart Ive. Pair last 0.7434 vs 0.7443 early. Adds trigger for NZD to move higher will be if RBA decides to raise rates tomorrow: "I believe the market has a 70% chance priced in (for hike), and I think the Kiwi will be taken higher on the basis of that (a rate hike)." Adds ongoing speculation of quantitative easing in U.S. likely to continue to weigh broadly on USD; "That will stem any downward moves for any \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  (vs USD) at the moment." Tips support to hold at 0.7315 with resistance at 0.7520. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] If RBA does hike Tuesday -- market 60% priced for 25 bps move -- then AUD/USD could get serious lift, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  Strategists. "Barring a scenario in which the RBA does not raise the rate and sounds dovish, a combination we do not expect, a rate hike would be a substantial boost for the AUD, in our opinion. The RBA would not begin raising rates again after being on hold for four months to fine-tune policy. So, the bills market would likely quickly move to price in a second rate hike this year, which is currently priced at only about a 20% chance." AUD/USD last 0.9704 in thin trade, Sydney shut for public holiday. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0320 GMT [Dow Jones] UOB KayHian pegs end-2011 target for KCLI at 1,635 based on 14.5X forward P/E; says applying conservative P/E multiple (below post-Asian financial crisis average of 15X) due to risks to corporate earnings from likely fall-off in external demand, execution risks to economic transformation programme. "The rolling-out of more mega construction projects amid lower development expenditure suggests a strong reliance and 'buy-in' from private funding." Adds banks still remain "most effective" proxies to rising investment cycle in Malaysia. Also says potential winners from Budget 2011 are Gamuda (5398.KU), WCT (9679.KU), \uc2 IJM Corp\uc2 . (3336.KU) from construction sector, \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU) from property sector; \uc2 British American Tobacco (Malaysia) (\uc2 4162.KU) key loser. PM Najib to make budget announcement on October 15. KLCI last +0.5% at 1,474.01. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered tips USD/THB to fall to 30.00 by year-end as Thailand's resilient economic fu