\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/PHP Down On Inflows For Stks; Support 43.60\b0\par\par\uc2 1,538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 04:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving lower, tracking USD weakness vs some Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and weighed down by inflows to stock market; pair last at 43.73 on moderate volume after settling at 43.88 late Friday. Support pegged at 43.60, resistance at 44.00. "We're still seeing (dollar) inflows and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is so far allowing the peso to appreciate," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY falls vs USD, EUR as investors turn attention toward 2-day BOJ policy-setting meeting starting today, amid expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce further easing measures at conclusion of meeting tomorrow, possibly weighing on Japan interest rates to detriment of JPY, says senior dealer at major Japan trust bank. USD/JPY rises to fresh intraday high at 83.88, from earlier low at 83.22. Tips resistance at 83.90, as investors may hesitate to push pair higher due to Japan exporter sell orders lined up above 84.00. Pair last 83.82. EUR/JPY last 115.24, up from intraday low 114.61. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0159 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea government bonds mixed in early trade, taking breather following recent sharp gains, with 3-year bonds underperforming ahead of government auction of those bonds; results due 0230 GMT. 3-year yield quoted +2 bps at 3.28%, 5-year +1 bp at 3.58%, 10-year down 1 bp at 3.99%, 20-year down 3 bps at 4.21%, December futures down 3 ticks at 112.82, as foreigners net sell 5,233 contracts so far today. With 3-year yield already hovering near lowest closing levels for 2010, "investors are staying sidelined till after the auction results are out, though the issuance volume isn't that big," says bond manager at local securities firm. Notes, rising KRW remains favorable for foreign investors investing in Korean bonds. Pegs support for futures at 112.70. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0155 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.3% at 4638.2, extends recovery from Friday's 3-week low of 4559.0 in extremely thin trading due to NSW state holiday. Trading volume is about half normal levels, so today's rally could prove slightly exaggerated. U.S. stock index futures are down 0.2%, giving a slightly bearish lead for Wall Street ahead of U.S. home sales and factory orders data tonight. However, while remaining cautious on the economy, traders say expectations of further quantitative easing in the U.S. could drive equity market strength in the short term. "Everyone seems to be driven by expectations of QE2 at the moment," says senior institutional trader at major broker. "The U.S. dollar remains weak and the commodity complex is up. That's the way the market has been trading, on that Bernanke put theory, that bad economic data promotes QE2, which is bad for the U.S. dollar, but good for equities and commodities," says trader. Another trader says QE2 could well be factored in by the time it occurs but that, in the short-term, equities could remain firm in the face of mediocre U.S. payrolls data this week if they serve to promote expectations of imminent QE. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares moving higher, with gains on Wall Street spurring further accumulation by investors; benchmark PSEi at new record high 4157.43, up 1.1%. Resistance pegged at 4200, while support at 4100. Volume moderate as gainers swamp losers 56 to 12. Leading advancers are Banco de Oro (BDO.PH) up 0.4% at PHP60.30, Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH) up 1.3% to PHP3.86. Grace Cerdenia, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia, says investors starting to reposition on certain stocks for 4Q, with industrial, utilities, mining stocks among the favorities. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong buying of yen-crosses by Dutch bank in past 20 minutes propelled EUR/JPY from 114.90 to as high as 115.07, USD/JPY from 83.40 to 83.65, say Singapore-based dealers; reckon rise was not due to any official \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Add, Japanese and UK names also grabbing USD/JPY offers. USD/JPY last at 83.57, EUR/JPY at 114.96. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0146 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures +0.7% at 22,486, as cash market expected to open higher on Wall Street cue. Still, contract ended Thursday at recently rare 30-point discount to cash, suggesting view that profit-taking may set in for HSI in near term, which unsurprising given HSI's 9% rise in September. October open interest increases 316 lots to 108,779. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares +0.6% at 8289.36, hitting 8-month high 8300.04 earlier, led by HTC's (2498.TW) 1.0% rise in hops of strong September revenues, says Mega International Investment Service manager Stanley Chou. Notes profit-taking likely to cap Taiex's gains, though any correction likely mild; "The Taiex is not likely to fall sharply" as people still buying large-cap stocks, he says. Tips Taiex in 8200-8300 band for rest of session. Yulon Motor (2201.TW) +5.6% at NT$60.20. \uc2 Nan Ya Plastics\uc2  (1303.TW) down 3.5% at NT$66.10 after fire hits company's plant Sunday, which will cause estimated NT$150 million in revenue losses, according to company's spokesman. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.6% at 1474.75, supported by strong gains in regional markets, but dealers say upside likely limited in near term due to lack of positive domestic drivers. Tip strong resistance at 1480. Still, analysts say market's long-term uptrend remains intact, underpinned by positive chart patterns, expected roll-out of large government projects. HwangDBS Vickers Research says KLCI may reach 1525-1565 before 2010 is over. "This means there is a potential for the bellwether to match or exceed its all-time record high of 1524.69," HwangDBS says. Among gainers, \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2  (2194.KU) +5.4% at MYR3.29, Top Glove (7113.KU) +4.3% at MYR5.59, Kencana (5122.KU) +4.8% at MYR1.73. Among decliners, \uc2 JT International\uc2  (2615.KU) down 0.9% at MYR5.63. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper, aluminum, zinc and nickel all edge higher in early trade; tin and lead so far untraded, reflecting Chinese holiday-affected trading conditions. LME 3-month copper most active at $8,145/ton, up $50 since Friday's kerb with 86 lots done. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  says in report that 4Q could intially see more gains. "Considering that market participants seem to slowly factor in higher emerging market growth next year and considering that some investors are still underweight copper, we believe that prices may rise further at the outset of 4Q10." But adds copper could correct later in quarter with inventory trend possibly reversing if seasonal pattern follows traditional trajectory. Also notes Chinese imports could fall; "weak incentives for copper imports from China are at present also reflected in differentials between the Shanghai Futures Exchange and the London Metals Exchange prices, which have been firmly against importers since August." (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to rise, but profit taking likely amid volatile trade as key 1,000 level approached. "Nothing has changed fundamentally. Fund inflows are still the key to this upward sentiment. However, while the market seems to be confident that the index will head to the 1,000 mark, investors are aware that several stocks are in overbought zones," says KT Zmico Securities First VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. Adds energy heavyweights like PTT (PTT.TH), \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) likely to dominate while gains in "overbought" banks may be slower after recent rises. Initial resistance pegged at 987, support at 963. SET Index ends +0.3% at fresh 14-year high close of 978.58 Friday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY, EUR/JPY rise as Japanese importers buy, amid lingering caution over possible Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major European bank. Says USD/JPY may test 84.00 vs last 83.56 (up from earlier low 83.22). But says absent any Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "the dollar-yen should stay pretty heavily offered this week," due to broad-based USD weakness amid mounting expectations for further Fed easing; that should curb any sharp rises in pair for week, dealer says. EUR/JPY rises to 115.07 (from earlier low 114.61), highest since May 18; last 114.96. But says profit-taking by short-term investors may drag on any further rises in cross, likely keeping it centered around 115.00 for now.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 2205ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a40001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
