\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/USD, EUR/JPY Down On Profit-Taking - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 02:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones]EUR/USD, EUR/JPY down as short-term investors in Asia take profits, says senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at major brokerage in Japan. EUR/USD earlier hit 1.3809, highest level since March 17, while EUR/JPY rose to 114.98, highest since May 18. "Those rises put the euro at an attractive level for profit-taking, which should put the brakes on further gains for the rest of today." Says EUR/USD could fall to 1.3710 vs last at 1.3755, EUR/JPY to 114.20 vs last 114.66. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.2% at 9385.02, after opening in positive territory, as investors stay sidelined ahead of BOJ policy board meeting (Monday-Tuesday). Investors widely anticipate additional monetary easing measures to be announced; today's Nikkei range seen at 9350-9500, analysts say. "It's a chance for the BOJ to take steps before the U.S.," says Cosmo Securities equity strategist Toshikazu Horiuchi, referring to upcoming November FOMC meeting. "But market players are not quite sure about what, specifically, the BOJ can do when it comes to 'stepped-up' measures." 25/33 Topix subindexes lower; banks off 0.9%, consumer finance firms off 0.9%. Among heavyweight shares, \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) off 1.1%. Inpex (1605.TO) up 2.2% at Y412,000 on crude gains. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  (8801.TO) up 2.1% at Y1,490 on continued BOJ easing hopes after sharp Friday gains. \uc2 Shinsei Bank\uc2  (8303.TO) up 3.4% at Y60 on \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  rating hike. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, bolstered by positive cues from U.S. stocks Friday; but gains expected modest, with last week's rangebound trading pattern likely to persist in absence of fresh fundamental leads. KLCI ends +0.2% at 1466.32 Friday; dealers tip index to remain in 1455-1480 range near term. "Interest may only pick up in a more significant manner when we get closer to the release of the budget," dealer says, referring to government's 2011 budget due to be unveiled October 15. Cigarette makers such as \uc2 BAT Malaysia\uc2  (4162.KU) may be in focus after government raised retail prices by 7.5%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR unchanged from late Friday at 3.0850 but biased down, dealers say. "With sentiment on the dollar still somewhat bearish, we could see new lows being reached this week," dealer says. USD/MYR tipped to test 3.0800 support near-term, then 3.0750, though sharp decline unlikely due to possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers say Bank Negara suspected of buying in recent sessions to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0021 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,130 support in early trade, falling to near 5-month low on expectations foreign funds will continue to flow into local stock market following gains on Wall Street Friday. Pair last at 1,124.4 vs 1,130.4 late Friday in Seoul. Local bank trader pegs next support at 1,122. Still, players likely to remain wary over possible USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels, traders say; note pair now falling for 7th straight session, headed for fresh lows. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities seem to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  (almost) on a daily basis recently to moderate the pace of the pair's fall," which will likely be the case again today, the local bank trader adds. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely steady amid absence of fresh economic data; dealers say MGS, which barely budged last week in tepid trade, may remain confined to narrow ranges again this week as investors continue to bide time until release of government's 2011 budget on October 15. "For the near term, one factor to watch out for is the ringgit as any further appreciation may lend some support to the bond market," dealer says. On Friday, yields on benchmark MGS end mixed, with May 2013 unchanged at 3.15%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.23%, September 2017 up 5 bps at 3.54%, November 2019 unchanged at 3.62%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0017 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes Nikkei gave up 15.5% in 1H FY2010, its seventh worst performance since 1950. Says following such sharp losses, the market has tended to follow one of two patterns from around the start of 2H: 1) reversed course or 2) continued on a downtrend through half-end. "We think the former scenario is the more likely this time around. The rise in M&A points to renewed appetite for growth in the corporate sector, while solid performances from South Korean stocks and U.S. stocks in the capital goods sector are encouraging signs for markets with considerable exposure to capital goods." Adds, Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also equity friendly (more so than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates per se) medium to long-term. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures briefly climb to session high of 143.46, highest level since June 2003, may push higher to 143.70 as expectations grow that BOJ will ease monetary policy further early this week, say