\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/MYR Steady But Biased Down;May Test 3.0800 N/T\b0\par\par\uc2 1,682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 02:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0028 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR unchanged from late Friday at 3.0850 but biased down, dealers say. "With sentiment on the dollar still somewhat bearish, we could see new lows being reached this week," dealer says. USD/MYR tipped to test 3.0800 support near-term, then 3.0750, though sharp decline unlikely due to possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Dealers say Bank Negara suspected of buying in recent sessions to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0021 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW takes out 1,130 support in early trade, falling to near 5-month low on expectations foreign funds will continue to flow into local stock market following gains on Wall Street Friday. Pair last at 1,124.4 vs 1,130.4 late Friday in Seoul. Local bank trader pegs next support at 1,122. Still, players likely to remain wary over possible USD-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at current levels, traders say; note pair now falling for 7th straight session, headed for fresh lows. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities seem to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  (almost) on a daily basis recently to moderate the pace of the pair's fall," which will likely be the case again today, the local bank trader adds. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely steady amid absence of fresh economic data; dealers say MGS, which barely budged last week in tepid trade, may remain confined to narrow ranges again this week as investors continue to bide time until release of government's 2011 budget on October 15. "For the near term, one factor to watch out for is the ringgit as any further appreciation may lend some support to the bond market," dealer says. On Friday, yields on benchmark MGS end mixed, with May 2013 unchanged at 3.15%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.23%, September 2017 up 5 bps at 3.54%, November 2019 unchanged at 3.62%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0017 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes Nikkei gave up 15.5% in 1H FY2010, its seventh worst performance since 1950. Says following such sharp losses, the market has tended to follow one of two patterns from around the start of 2H: 1) reversed course or 2) continued on a downtrend through half-end. "We think the former scenario is the more likely this time around. The rise in M&A points to renewed appetite for growth in the corporate sector, while solid performances from South Korean stocks and U.S. stocks in the capital goods sector are encouraging signs for markets with considerable exposure to capital goods." Adds, Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets also equity friendly (more so than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates per se) medium to long-term. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures briefly climb to session high of 143.46, highest level since June 2003, may push higher to 143.70 as expectations grow that BOJ will ease monetary policy further early this week, says Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. Adds growing concerns over impact of JPY's rise on nation's exports, broader economy boosting demand for safe-haven assets, which could lift JGB prices higher further. JGB futures now at 143.45, +0.13; 10-year cash bonds not yet traded; yield at 0.955% on Friday. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold, silver likely to go "much higher still" in medium term with monetary policy outlook clear, says David Rosenberg, chief economist at Gluskin Sheff. Notes Fed signaling its intent on embarking on QE2, Bank of England doing likewise, while fiscal pressures building in euro zone, so ECB likely to extend its accommodative posture. Adds industrial production in Japan has now declined 3 months in a row and JPY at similar level to where BOJ made unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort, and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  planning more monetary easing efforts. "We may not have a whole lot of conviction over the corporate profit outlook, but we do have conviction over the looming growth rate of fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and we do have conviction over the supply curve for gold, which is perfectly inelastic on a near- and intermediate-term basis." Spot gold at $1,316.30/oz, down $2.30 since Friday's NY close; spot silver at $22.13/oz, up 4 cents. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may edge higher on Wall Street's gains Friday, enabling STI to attempt another new year-to-date high. China's offer to continue buying Greek bonds, create US$5 billion fund to help Greek shipping companies buy Chinese ships may also help ease investors' concerns over euro zone's debt problems, generate interest in shipbuilders like Cosco (F83.SG), Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), JES International (EG0.SG). STI +1.1% at 3130.90 Friday, highest close so far this year; resistance expected at 3184 (peak at June 6, 2008). "China's announcements over the weekend is a welcome change from the slew of mixed economic data from the U.S. all these months," says trader at local brokerage. Cosco +2.3% at S$1.81 Friday, Yangzij