\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI May Attempt New 2010 High; Shipbuilders Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 02:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may edge higher on Wall Street's gains Friday, enabling STI to attempt another new year-to-date high. China's offer to continue buying Greek bonds, create US$5 billion fund to help Greek shipping companies buy Chinese ships may also help ease investors' concerns over euro zone's debt problems, generate interest in shipbuilders like Cosco (F83.SG), Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), JES International (EG0.SG). STI +1.1% at 3130.90 Friday, highest close so far this year; resistance expected at 3184 (peak at June 6, 2008). "China's announcements over the weekend is a welcome change from the slew of mixed economic data from the U.S. all these months," says trader at local brokerage. Cosco +2.3% at S$1.81 Friday, Yangzijiang +1.1% at S$1.78, JES +4.5% at S$0.35. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.7% at 4611.3, hits 4613.0 after Friday's U.S. gains on positive manufacturing data from China and the U.S. last week. Consolidation now expected amid holidays in NSW state and China today. Materials and financials sectors leading gains, with BHP (BHP.AU) up 1.3%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 0.7% and major banks up 0.6%-1.1%. IG Markets Institutional Trader Chris Weston says equities bull market back on track after recent weakness, with the outlook for materials stocks particularly strong. "I'm still quite positive," says Weston. "It was good to see the manufacturing data holding up in China and the U.S. I think equities could break convincingly higher early this month because we have so much tier 1 economic data from around the world. Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls data will really underscore what's going to happen in regard to QE (quantitative easing)." Cautions that Australian retail sector and stocks that are sensitive to Australian dollar strength are delicately poised before tomorrow's RBA meeting, which could see another rate hike. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY downside could accelerate this week if pair breaches 82.87, 15-year low marked Sept. 15 just before Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly dragging pair down to 80.00 if government this time refrains from further action, says Osao Iizuka, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Sumitomo Trust & Banking\uc2 . That would bring into sight post World War II-low of 79.75 marked in April 1995. Pair last at 83.35, trading in narrow 83.22-83.41 band so far in Asian trade as investors remain cautious \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF may conduct USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prevent such slides after doing so Sept. 15 for first time in more than 6 years. Absence of Sydney-based players due to holiday also "may keep the market a bit thinner than usual today," Iizuka says. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals picture looking positive with tin approaching 2008 record high of $25,500/ton after LME 3-month contract added $700 to close at $24,900 Friday. Copper also well bid with 2006 high at $8,800 and record of $8,940 hit in 2008 likely to be challenged later in year based on inventory trend, expectations of renewed China demand. LME 3-month copper closes at 2-year high of $8,095 +$85 after good China PMI readings last week. LME Options declaration this Wedneday likely supportive. "The main open strikes suggest that aluminium is likely to hold above the $2,300 level and copper above $8,000," says Sucden Financial. LME 3-month aluminum added $8 to $2,358/ton Friday. However trade likely to be subdued in Asia this week with China closed until Friday for national day holidays. Data focus likely to be on U.S. September non-farm payrolls Friday. Later today, euro-zone August PPI due at 0900 GMT, U.S. August pending home sales index at 1400 GMT, only data of note. Culmination of Ryder cup golf competition in European session, early U.S. could hurt trading volumes. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to trade in range with yields moving 2-3 bps either way as investors look for further cues on outlook for global economy, says trader with foreign bank. "For today, we'll see how equities perform. If stocks turn high, we might see some selling in bonds, but the moves won't be big." On Friday, 2-year bond yield up 1 bp to close at 0.42%, while 10-year up 2 bps to end at 2.04%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +37.7% at HK$7.145 billion Thursday, as percentage of total market volume increased to 8.3% vs Wednesday's 6.1%, suggesting bears stepping up bet, expecting short-term correction with HSI +9.0% in September. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) topped list with HK$874.87 million, followed by Hutchison (0013.HK) with HK$632.60 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$586.70 million. Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK), \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$342.93 million, HK$303.52 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. 10-year Treasury yield may fall to 2.3% as expectations grow that Fed will loosen monetary policy in near term, Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Akihiro Nishida. But adds declines in long-term yields should be limited ahead of U.S. September jobs data due Friday. Tokyo traders say if data indicate that pace of possible U.S. economic slowdown won't become as fast as investors expect, that could make some players willing to start selling Treasurys to lock in profits. 10-year yield at 2.519% in NY Friday, 5-year yield at 1.263%, 2-year yield at 0.422%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold hits new record high of $1,321.80/oz in New York Friday before record close at $1,318.60/oz. Spot gold last at $1,319.90/oz, up $1.30 early in Asia. Yellow metal was again driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after EUR/USD broke through 1.3700 level and reached 6-month high as another top Federal Reserve official hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will launch second monetary stimulus program. Poor U.S. ISM manufacturing data also added to the mix. "We're focused on economic data because it could prompt the Fed into that second round of QE that would potentially pressure the dollar and further support gold prices," says David Meger, director of metals trading at Vision Financial Markets in Chicago. Silver also hit new 3-year high of $22.17/oz before settling at $22.09/oz; last at $22.14/oz, up 5 cents. LME base metals closed higher Friday, propelled by weaker USD, fresh buying from macro funds, brightened demand outlook in China after well-received PMI data. LME 3-month copper rose $85 vs Thursday's kerb to close at $8,095, highest close since August 2008. Lead was up $18 at $2,295, zinc up $34 at $2,229, tin was closing in on its 2008 record high, up $700 to $24,900; nickel up $450 to $23,845 while aluminum $8 higher at $2,358. Platinum, palladium also rallied, up 1.5%, 1.8% respectively. In early Asia, spot platinum at $1,676/oz, up $1, while spot palladium at $572/oz, down $1. Nymex November crude futures down 5 cents early in Asia at $81.53/bbl after 2% leap Friday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,130.4 late Friday in Seoul, extending decline to 7th straight session as downward momentum builds after successive breach of key support levels including 1,150 and 1,140 last week. U.S. stocks end up again on Friday, boding well for local stocks, KRW, traders say. "Dollar selling by offshore players is just so strong...while the greenback is weakening globally," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,122-1,133 range. Traders note depth of pair's fall will likely depend on extent of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem KRW gains recently amid concerns a stronger KRW may sap an export-led recovery. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely open higher with gains for U.S. market Friday likely to inspire further foreign buying; hopes surrounding upcoming 3Q corporate earnings this month could help index to test 1900 level near term, analysts say. But Kospi's recent rally, strengthening KRW may cap gains. "\uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' third-quarter earnings guidance due later this week will likely set a positive tone for the market," says \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  in report; adds, KRW strength, bearish sentiment on tech sector recently may limit gains. Kospi, ends +0.2% at 1876.73 Friday, targets 1900. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  says some automakers, shipbuilders, refinery, chemical, machinery issues that rallied in recent weeks on hopes of strong 3Q earnings may face profit-taking pressure while techs limited by recent falls of chip, panel prices, still-sluggish demand. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.930% vs Friday's 0.955% amid growing expectations that BOJ will take additional monetary easing steps on Tuesday, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. But Tokyo traders say falls in long-term yields would be limited as many players will likely shy away from active trading due to persisting uncertainty over what kind of measures BOJ will decide to implement. Lead December JGB futures may move in 143.30-143.50 vs Friday's 143.32. BOJ scheduled to hold 2-day policy board meeting set to start today; 9 out of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to expand liquidity-provision measures, including potential increase in supply of 6-month loans or more purchases of short-term government bills. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 4.8 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling in 6 days may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 14.6 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.8 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 2008ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a400003\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
