\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Kospi Likely To Open Lower; Support Tipped At 1870\b0\par\par\uc2 1,873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2010\par 01:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2352 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi likely to open lower, tracking slump in U.S. market overnight and as market takes breather after recent sustained rise, analysts say; but expect market still on track toward 1900 mark, on back of ample liquidity, foreign interest in local shares. "Foreign buying in Korean shares is expected to be sustained for a while because global liquidity is likely to increase amid recent market expectations the Fed will ease further, while investor preference on riskier assets will also stay strong following the continued weakening in the dollar," says \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  in note. Although KRW continues to rise sharply, relatively stronger JPY will also buffer any negative impact on Korean exporters, says Meritz Securities. Kospi ended +0.1% at 1879.29 Monday, initial support tipped at 1870. Analysts favor banks, insurers this week as investors have been showing strong interest for those stocks on low-valuation. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2350 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate after setting 2-month high of $82.38/bbl but settling 11 cents lower at $81.47/bbl yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD bullish, 5-, 15-day moving averages rising; but stochastics turned bearish, while spinning-top candlestick pattern reflecting indecision of traders completed yesterday. Support at $80.77 (yesterday's low); breach would target $80.45 (hourly chart swing low), then $79.70 (Friday's low), $78.04 (previous cap set Sept. 13), $77.55 (Thursday's low, near 200-day moving average), $76.77 (55-day moving average) and $75.52 (Sept. 27 low, near 100-day moving average). But rise above $82.38 resistance would reinstate near-term positive outlook, exposing upside to $82.97 (Aug. 4 top), then psychological levels from $84.00 up to $87.00, then $87.15 (May 3 peak). November crude last down 15 cents at $81.32/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to 84.00 if BOJ decides at policy board meeting to buy more long-term JGBs or asset-backed securities, says Akira Hoshino, senior dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. Adds move would send message that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  taking steps in quantitative easing. Nikkei reports today that BOJ may take bolder-than-anticipated steps, such as buying ABSs. Hoshino adds that if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, coordinating with BOJ's moves, pair may rise above 85.00. On the other hand, says that with expectations for some more modest steps already factored in pair may fall to below 83.00, approaching level that triggered \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mid-September, if BOJ meeting only comes up with easing measures such as expanding 3-month, 6-month lending facilities introduced last December, August respectively. Pair last at 83.57 on small rising trend during early Asia morning. Says EUR/USD may fall to 1.3550 as correction comes in recent USD weakness. EUR/USD falls Monday after hitting highest level since March at 1.3809 as extent of Ireland's budget deficit revealed; pair last 1.3679. Says EUR/JPY may trade in 113.00-115.00 range vs last 114.33. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 1.2% at 4569.1, hit 2-day low of 4559.2 in thin trading after Wall Street's S&P 500 fell 0.8%. Materials underperforming after U.S. peer declines, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) down 1.5%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 2.1%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) down 1.5%. Financials, energy and consumer discretionary also weak, with major banks down 1.0%-1.5%, Woodside (WPL.AU) down 1.4%, \uc2 News Corp\uc2  (NWS.AU) (owner of Dow Jones Newswires) down 1.9% and \uc2 David Jones (DJS.AU) \uc2 down 5.1% ex-div. Index is near critical short-term support from last week's low at 4559.0. But the early fall could be exaggerated by light volumes and nerves before today's RBA meeting. Traders say support could emerge after RBA decision, with 25 bps rate hike widely expected. "Yesterday we outperformed on thin volume and today we're underperforming on light volume," says RBS Morgans investment adviser Danny Dreyfus. "Most people think the RBA will hike today, so the market is vulnerable beforehand. There's also been some profit-taking on Wall Street after strong gains on light volume, but there's a lot of cash on the sidelines." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.5% at 3211.78 on offshore weakness, sharply lower business confidence data eyed in NZ Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "it won't move into positive ground but the question is whether it will be mildly weak or if the sells off will gain strength." NZIER survey shows a net 6.0% of surveyed firms expect general conditions to improve over next 6 months vs 18% in 2Q; Schaper says probably hadn't surprised people but gave another reason for people to sit on sidelines. Adds Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) down 4.2% to NZ$1.85 as stock went ex-dividend earlier. Says Warehouse (WHS.NZ) down 1.3% to NZ$3.92 following 3.7% move higher yesterday; "the company came out this morning and said it was not aware of any information (moving the stock)." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to rise vs 1,122.3 late yesterday in Seoul, finally taking breather following 7 straight sessions of losses after Wall Street ends down. But other than taking profits on USD shorts, buying unlikely to be aggressive as majority in market remain biased towards weaker pair, foreign-equity fund inflows likely to cap upside, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,120-1,130 range. "Dominant views for a broadly weaker dollar and high expectations for a BOK rate hike will make it difficult for the market to anticipate any strong rebound in the pair," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 19.4% at HK$5.76 billion yesterday, 6.0% of total market volume vs Thursday's 8.3%, suggesting bears remained hesitant to step up bets, with HSI's rise yesterday marking 18th session of gains out of 22. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) grabbed lion's shares with HK$643.97 million as shares appeared very overbought, in 6-day, 11.5% rally. Cnooc (0883.HK) in second place with HK$251.81 million, then Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$249.89 million. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$200.50 million, HK$200.25 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 6.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 2nd day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 20.4 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 13.7 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.900% vs Monday's 0.935% given upturn in U.S. Treasurys overnight, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. But adds declines in yields should be limited as players will likely shy away from active trading ahead of BOJ's policy decision later in day; if \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to boost size of long-term JGB buying from market, long-term yields could extend falls. Lead December JGB futures may hover in 143.40-143.80 vs Monday's 143.45. BOJ expected to announce policy decision around 0330 GMT. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 3Q Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey of Business Opinion suggest inflation pressures muted at moment as businesses find it difficult to pass on rising costs, says ASB economist Christina Leung; "This has hampered profitability and is the key driver behind the fall in business confidence." Says further declines in profitability over the coming year may lead businesses to put expansion plans on hold. "Overall, we expect the RBNZ will remain cautious about the pace of the recovery and not increase the OCR until the March 2011 meeting." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall following overnight decline in U.S. stocks; index expected to trade between 9250-9500 vs 9381.06 Monday close (down 0.3%), depending on what kind of additional monetary easing measures BOJ will likely announce following 2-day policy board meeting through Tuesday, analysts say. "The Nikkei could top 9500 if, given drastic BOJ steps, combined with (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar nears 85 yen," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. "But such expectations are very low." Adds positive stock reaction likely if BOJ exceeds consensus by announcing increase in buying of long-term JGBs or asset-backed securities. Among individual shares, \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) likely to fall after logging its biggest monthly sales drop in 7 years for September. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9325 vs Osaka close at 9350.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 1952ET ]\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] Technical, fundamental factors seem to be aligning in a positive way for copper, with major market participant who requests anonymity noting that LME 3-month copper losses limited overnight by forecast by International Copper Study Group that it had widened its deficit forecast for 2011 for global refined copper to 400,000 metric tons, swinging from an April estimate of a 240,000 tons surplus, which will support prices going forward. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  notes copper pulled back a touch overnight from Friday highs, with old April highs and resistance at $8,043/$7,995 zone turning to support. While allowing for this zone to be probed the focus remains higher, with $8,210 and $8,468 the next two levels to aim for, says BarCap in technical analysis, with a move below $7,950 suggesting only a pause in trend, and in absence of a move back below $7,750, "we would continue to expect further gains in 4Q". Historic highs established between 2006 and 2008 still represent a formidable obstacle, so upside progress is likely to be choppy but a slow steady erosion of those highs now seems likely, it says. Overnight, LME 3-month copper fell $36 on day at $8,064/metric ton, lead lost $18 at $2,295, zinc added $1 at $2,229, tin jumped $300 to $25,200, nickel climbed $294 at $24,140 while aluminum added $4 at $2,363. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 1952ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a4000eo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
