ash on the sidelines." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.5% at 3211.78 on offshore weakness, sharply lower business confidence data eyed in NZ Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Dave Schaper; "it won't move into positive ground but the question is whether it will be mildly weak or if the sells off will gain strength." NZIER survey shows a net 6.0% of surveyed firms expect general conditions to improve over next 6 months vs 18% in 2Q; Schaper says probably hadn't surprised people but gave another reason for people to sit on sidelines. Adds Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) down 4.2% to NZ$1.85 as stock went ex-dividend earlier. Says Warehouse (WHS.NZ) down 1.3% to NZ$3.92 following 3.7% move higher yesterday; "the company came out this morning and said it was not aware of any information (moving the stock)." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to rise vs 1,122.3 late yesterday in Seoul, finally taking breather following 7 straight sessions of losses after Wall Street ends down. But other than taking profits on USD shorts, buying unlikely to be aggressive as majority in market remain biased towards weaker pair, foreign-equity fund inflows likely to cap upside, traders say. Local bank trader tips pair in 1,120-1,130 range. "Dominant views for a broadly weaker dollar and high expectations for a BOK rate hike will make it difficult for the market to anticipate any strong rebound in the pair," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 19.4% at HK$5.76 billion yesterday, 6.0% of total market volume vs Thursday's 8.3%, suggesting bears remained hesitant to step up bets, with HSI's rise yesterday marking 18th session of gains out of 22. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) grabbed lion's shares with HK$643.97 million as shares appeared very overbought, in 6-day, 11.5% rally. Cnooc (0883.HK) in second place with HK$251.81 million, then Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$249.89 million. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$200.50 million, HK$200.25 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 6.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 2nd day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 20.4 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 13.7 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.900% vs Monday's 0.935% given upturn in U.S. Treasurys overnight, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. But adds declines in yields should be limited as players will likely shy away from active trading ahead of BOJ's policy decision later in day; if \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to boost size of long-term JGB buying from market, long-term yields could extend falls. Lead December JGB futures may hover in 143.40-143.80 vs Monday's 143.45. BOJ expected to announce policy decision around 0330 GMT. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 3Q Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey of Business Opinion suggest inflation pressures muted at moment as businesses find it difficult to pass on rising costs, says ASB economist Christina Leung; "This has hampered profitability and is the key driver behind the fall in business confidence." Says further declines in profitability over the coming year may lead businesses to put expansion plans on hold. "Overall, we expect the RBNZ will remain cautious about the pace of the recovery and not increase the OCR until the March 2011 meeting." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall following overnight decline in U.S. stocks; index expected to trade between 9250-9500 vs 9381.06 Monday close (down 0.3%), depending on what kind of additional monetary easing measures BOJ will likely announce following 2-day policy board meeting through Tuesday, analysts say. "The Nikkei could top 9500 if, given drastic BOJ steps, combined with (\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar nears 85 yen," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. "But such expectations are very low." Adds positive stock reaction likely if BOJ exceeds consensus by announcing increase in buying of long-term JGBs or asset-backed securities. Among individual shares, \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  (9983.TO) likely to fall after logging its biggest monthly sales drop in 7 years for September. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9325 vs Osaka close at 9350.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] Technical, fundamental factors seem to be aligning in a positive way for copper, with major market participant who requests anonymity noting that LME 3-month copper losses limited overnight by forecast by International Copper Study Group that it had widened its deficit forecast for 2011 for global refined copper to 400,000 metric tons, swinging from an April estimate of a 240,000 tons surplus, which will support prices going forward. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  notes copper pulled back a touch overnight from Friday highs, with old April highs and resistance at $8,043/$7,995 zone turning to support. While allowing for this zone to be probed the focus remains higher, with $8,210 and $8,468 the next two levels to aim for, says BarCap in technical analysis, with a move below $7,950 suggesting only a pause in trend, and in absence of a move back below $7,750, "we would continue to expect further gains in 4Q". Historic highs established between 2006 and 2008 still represent a formidable obstacle, so upside progress is likely to be choppy but a slow steady erosion of those highs now seems likely, it says. Overnight, LME 3-month copper fell $36 on day at $8,064/metric ton, lead lost $18 at $2,295, zinc added $1 at $2,229, tin jumped $300 to $25,200, nickel climbed $294 at $24,140 while aluminum added $4 at $2,363. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 1950ET ]\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 3Q Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey of Business Opinion was unequivocally weak across the board, confidence fell in all the regions and across all sectors surveyed, says ANZ chief economist Khoon Goh; "The QSBO was weaker than what the National Bank Business Outlook was flagging, and points to a soggy second-half GDP performance, following a subdued first half." Survey showed net 6.0% of surveyed firms expect general conditions to improve over next 6 months vs 18% in 2Q. Khoon says deleveraging continues to act as headwind for near term activity but still sees better prospects next year on back of increased rural incomes, unleashed pent up demand, earthquake reconstruction work, activity from Rugby World Cup. Adds data reinforce RBNZ's on-hold message with "OCR set to stay where they are until first quarter next year." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 10-04-10 1950ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a4000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
