\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Investors Ponder How Much, Not When, On Fed Stimulus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 21:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors are placing more bets on the anti-dollar table, increasingly convinced the Federal Reserve will inject the U.S. economy with another shot of growth-stimulating cash.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the Fed undertaking another round of quantitative easing, it has become "not will they or won't they, but how much will it be?" said Camilla Sutton, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 As investors have solidified their views on the Fed's action to prop a sagging economy, they have driven the dollar down more than 7% against the euro since early September. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dropped more than 5% in the same time period, trading near its recent nine-month low.\par \par \uc2 Speculative bets against the dollar reached their deepest level since December 2009 as of the week ended Tuesday, according to a \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysis of the weekly Commitments of Traders report released by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission.\par \par \uc2 Anti-dollar bets are likely to mount even if the Fed doesn't engage in another round of asset purchases, said Brian Kim, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Stamford, Conn., with just the talk of a stagnating U.S. economy leading investors to believe key rates will remain ultralow for an extended period.\par \par \uc2 Investors use cheap dollars to place bets on higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 If investor sentiment proves correct and the Fed does initiate asset purchases--some target the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nov. 3 rate-setting committee meeting as a key date to watch for a decision on so-called QEII--"the more it is, the more it weighs on the dollar," Sutton said of the amount of asset purchases.\par \par \uc2 Even if the Fed's injections are delivered in bits and pieces, as opposed to the first round's massive shock-and-awe campaign, the dollar will remain under pressure, and bets against it are likely to escalate, she said.\par \par \uc2 "As long as the potential for [quantitative easing] is on the table, it speaks to a weaker U.S. dollar," Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 Aside from a Fed that is seen as near to injecting fresh stimulus into a sagging U.S. economy, uncertainty surrounding November's U.S. elections also could keep bets against the dollar piling up, Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 "The fear is that we end up in a somewhat deadlocked political state," Sutton said. "That would really hamper the recovery," especially as lawmakers are expected to come up with fiscal policies to match the monetary policy aimed at more quickly digging the U.S. economy out of its hole.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the yen's speculative positioning should hold relatively steady, analysts said, with investors keeping a pro-yen stance in the most recent CFTC data, and likely to continue holding in as they test the patience of the government of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which initiated a massive, Y2 trillion yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid September.\par \par \uc2 Since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has traded in a tight range against the yen. But with concerns over the pace of the U.S. recovery taking center stage in world markets, investors are wont to press the dollar lower against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 The speculative investors that remain positive on the yen "are betting more that there will be limited success in stopping the appreciation" of the yen, Sutton said.\par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon, the dollar traded at Y83.36 from Y83.32 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 (Bradley Davis covers \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires from New York. He can be reached at 212-416-2654 or bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 10-04-10 1515ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101004e6a4000co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
