\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei falls 0.3 pct; awaits BOJ, U.S. data\b0\par\par\uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 01:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * BOJ easing expected, market wants to see details -analyst\par \par \uc2 * Expected easing, yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limit falls -analysts\par \par \uc2 * Support near 9,360, 13-wk moving average, retracement level\par \par \uc2 By Aiko Hayashi\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Japan's Nikkei fell 0.3 percent on Monday, taking its cues from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets ahead of a Bank of Japan policy decision the next day with the market expecting further easing, albeit a minor move.\par \par \uc2 Former BOJ Deputy Governor Toshiro Muto said on Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may ease policy as inaction would run the risk of spurring further yen gains, given the prospects for easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 The policy move expected most by economists polled by Reuters was yet another expansion of a cheap fund-supply tool that the BOJ set up in December and expanded in March and August.\par \par \uc2 "It is difficult to move actively before seeing the outcome of the BOJ's monetary policy, although the market is not expecting to see any big surprises," said Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.\par \par \uc2 "Reflecting the mood in the afternoon in which the Nikkei drifted down, it's more likely the market will test the downside after confirming the outcome of the BOJ meeting."\par \par \uc2 Trade is likely to remain nervous throughout the week, traders said.\par \par \uc2 "Nervous trade will likely continue this week, even after tomorrow's event, as U.S. jobs data is also set to be released later in the week," said Hiroaki Kuramochi, chief equity marketing officer at \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Nikkei eased 23.17 points to 9,381.06, while the broader Topix fell 7.23 points, or 0.9 percent, to 822.74.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average briefly ventured into positive territory in the morning, reaching a session high of 9,508.36 as the dollar rebounded against the yen on short-covering.\par \par \uc2 But the Nikkei ran out of steam in the afternoon as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  erased early gains, limiting active follow-through moves in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 In Asian trade, the dollar traded at 83.40 yen, having risen as high as 83.88 yen. Yet the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 0.3 percent higher against the yen after it found support at 83.15/16 last week, above its 15-year low of 82.87 yen set just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years on Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 "The market has largely factored in the possibility of further easing at the (BOJ) meeting, but at this point it needs to see the actual content of the decision," said Masayuki Otani, chief market analyst at Securities Japan, Inc.\par \par \uc2 "With the yen staying on the strong side, investors are also waiting to see if there will be another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the BOJ eases and this is combined with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that could have a bigger impact."\par \par \uc2 Market players see solid support for the Nikkei around 9,360, near its 13-week moving average. The 38.2 percent retracement of the Nikkei's move from 8,796 to the peak of its September rally at 9,704 also falls near that level.\par \par \uc2 Further support is expected around 9,344 yen, the Nikkei's 55-day moving average. One analyst said the 25-day moving average, which has been steadily moving upward and is now at 9,307, is another point of support for the index.\par \par \uc2 Resistance likely looms around 9,530, the upper level of the Nikkei's daily Ichimoku cloud on charts.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street rose on Friday, led by gains in resource stocks after data in China showed a pick-up in manufacturing activity.\par \par \uc2 SUMITOMO METAL JUMPS, DAIKIN FALLS\par \par \uc2 Exporters rose, with \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2  up 1.1 percent at 2,565 yen and \uc2 Kyocera Corp\uc2  advancing 1.4 percent to 8,110 yen.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Ricoh\uc2  gained 2.9 percent to 1,218 yen after \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  upgraded the copier and printer maker's rating to "outperform" from "neutral" and raised its target share price to 1,450 yen from 1,300 yen.\par \par \uc2 Sumitomo Metal Mining\uc2  shares climbed 3.3 percent to 1,317 yen after a jump in metals prices, with gold near a record high hit on Friday and copper prices at a two-year peak.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said it had raised its forecasts to reflect Shinsei's amended medium-term business plan released last week, which included steps to cut expenses and lower credit costs.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Daikin Industries\uc2  slid 1.3 percent to 3,075 yen after the air conditioner maker said its first-half net profit would likely be 74 percent lower than its previous forecast due to losses on its stocking holdings.\par \par \uc2 Shares of Eisai dropped 3 percent to 2,853 yen after \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  MUFG on Friday downgraded the drugmaker's stock rating to 'underweight' from 'equal-weight' and lowered its target stock price to 2,500 yen from 3,000 yen. (Additional reporting by Chikafumi Hodo in Tokyo and Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Krishna Kumar in Sydney; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-STOCKS (UPDATE 5)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101003e6a3000kr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
