\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Start Month On Positive Note\b0\par\par\uc2 2,681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 00:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China's markets are closed for the National Day holiday, while in Australia, Canberra, Sydney and Adelaide celebrate Labour Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10829.68  +41.63  +0.39% \par Nasdaq     2370.75   +2.13  +0.09% \par S&P 500    1146.24   +5.04  +0.44% \par FTSE 100   5592.90  +44.28  +0.80% \par Xetra DAX  6211.34  -17.68  -0.28% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 29/32 flat  0.422% flat \par 5Y Tsy   99 30/32 +1/32 1.263% -1.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 29/32 -2/32 2.519% +0.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.26-28     +0.04% \par Range         83.27-83.23 \par EUR/USD       1.3801-03    +0.09% \par Range         1.3807-1.3787 \par AUD/USD       0.9730-33    +0.02% \par Range         0.9735-0.9718 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.60-10.90% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    11.70-12.10% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    13.40-13.70% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,317.80  +$8.20 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,307.30-$1,322.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $81.58   +$1.61 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks kicked off the fourth quarter on a positive note, rising modestly even as a string of economic reports, including key manufacturing data, yielded conflicting signals.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 42 points, or 0.4%, at 10830. But the blue-chip index fell 0.3% for the week and snapped its four-week winning streak. \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  was the Dow's top gainer, rising 2%. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  gained 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  fell 3.1% after the company appointed Leo Apotheker as its new chief executive. Apotheker, former SAP CEO, replaces \uc2 Mark Hurd\uc2 , who resigned last month amid a sexual-harassment investigation.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite edged up 0.1% to 2371. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rose 0.4% to 1146, led by the energy and materials sectors. The index earlier climbed to as high as 1150. Traders noted that the S&P 500 in recent days has risen above that level but not decisively, which has added to the market's volatility.\par \par \uc2 "The fact that we got through a number of data points today without losing steam is a positive sign for the bullish camp," said Roger Volz, director at BGC Financial.\par \par \uc2 Friday's gains came after the Dow surged 7.7% last month--its best September performance since 1939--and rallied 10.4% in the third quarter. It was the Dow's best quarterly performance in a year.\par \par \uc2 Volz noted that there are still two weeks before earnings season ramps up. "Then we'll see if the bulls go into a 'lets take some profits' mood or if they push this issue and make the sideline money pay the price," he said.\par \par \uc2 Trading was jittery after the Institute for Supply Management said the U.S. manufacturing sector expanded in September, but at a slower pace than in August.\par \par \uc2 The ISM's manufacturing purchasing managers index fell to 54.4 from 56.3 in August. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires had expected a reading of 54.0. Readings above 50 indicate expanding activity, and September was the 14th consecutive month of growth. But investors said certain details in the report triggered alarm about the economy's health.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the data points had a lot of weakness in the layers," said Seth Setrakian, partner and co-head of trading at \uc2 First New York Securities\uc2 , a proprietary trading firm. "Backlogs and new orders were down and inventories were way up, which portends declining economic growth in the next few quarters."\par \par \uc2 Investors also continued to stew over the possibility of the Federal Reserve taking further action to stimulate the economy.\par \par \uc2 Better-than-expected U.S. personal income and spending data as well as positive Chinese manufacturing figures also fueled Friday's moves. The Reuters/University of Michigan consumer sentiment index ticked up a bit in September.\par \par \uc2 Brian Lazorishak, portfolio manager and quantitative analyst at Chase Investment Counsel, said the coming week's nonfarm payrolls report and the start to earnings season are the two major themes investors will watch closely to determine the market's future direction.\par \par \uc2 "A lot of people are just holding onto positions until some of that data starts coming out," Lazorishak said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Friday, as solid China economic data and hopes for monetary easing by the Bank of Japan lifted the broader indexes, while higher crude prices helped oil-related shares.\par \par \uc2 "We can expect a surprise impact (on stocks) if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government carries out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with BOJ monetary easing (expected at this week's policy board meeting)," said Toshihiko Matsuno, senior strategist at SMBC Friend Securities. But he said stocks aren't likely to respond if the BOJ's steps are merely in line with media reports.\par \par \uc2 Matsuno put the Nikkei's range for the week at 9100-9700, citing U.S. September jobs data due Friday as a downside risk.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets were generally mostly higher Friday, with South Korea's Kospi up 0.2% and Taiwan's Taiex up 0.1%. Hong Kong and China markets were closed for the National Day holiday, but the former will reopen Monday.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures rose to a seven-week high Friday, extending the week's rally on the back of continued dollar weakness and positive economic data.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures ended the week at all-time highs as the weaker dollar continued to feed gold's historic rally.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, base metals traded higher on the London Metal Exchange Friday, propelled by the weaker dollar, fresh buying from macro funds and a brighter demand outlook in China.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell Friday against all major rivals, with the focus on potential Federal Reserve stimulus diverting attention from encouraging U.S. economic data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A top Federal Reserve official said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is almost certain to have to offer fresh support to ensure that already-tepid economic growth doesn't falter further.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The current situation is wholly unsatisfactory," and "both the current levels of unemployment and inflation and the time frame over which they are likely to return to levels consistent with our mandate are unacceptable," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Dudley\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a speech in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dudley stopped short of saying what type of stimulus he prefers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chicago Fed President Charles Evans, who currently isn't a voting member of the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee, said further steps to stimulate the economy are desirable, but this requires "unconventional policy tools" due to the possibility that the U.S. may be in a "liquidity trap."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He stopped short of specifically endorsing a second round of quantitative easing in remarks at a Bank of France conference in Rome.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. data, including slightly higher Institute for Supply Management manufacturing data, failed to lift the greenback, with the euro touching a six-month peak above $1.37 and reaching its intraday high shortly after the ISM data were released mid-morning.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That ISM data was basically flat and didn't really give market participants direction either way, but the Fed speakers exacerbated the situation" and helped cement the dollar's extended losing streak, said Amelia Bourdeau, senior G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Stamford, Conn. "Dudley was dovish, and Evans while not a voter this year, is a voter in 2011 and was closely listened to."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traded at its lowest level since January. It also reached an intraday low point shortly after the ISM data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.3784, up from $1.3632 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.32, off from Y83.44, while the euro was at Y114.81, above Y113.72. The U.K. pound strengthened to $1.5841 from $1.5706. The dollar was at CHF0.9736, down from CHF0.9821. The ICE Dollar Index slipped to 78.076 from 78.762.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar dropped back a little against the yen, hovering close to Y83.15, its lowest level since \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Sept. 15 to hinder the yen's appreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors ramped up their bets against the dollar, according to data released Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Net speculative bets long the euro were at 35,300 contracts in the week ended Tuesday, increasing the position by $5.1 billion to a total $6 billion, according to a Scotia Capital analysis of the weekly Commitments of Traders report released by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission late Friday afternoon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro/dollar is "really pushing the top end of the bands. It does look over-extended but it is a U.S. dollar story," ANZ Senior Dealer Alex Sinton said Monday, tipping initial resistance at $1.3820 and support at $1.3762.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pair could take a breather but given the current environment, and "with QE II looming," may well head toward resistance, Sinton said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen should continue to push lower on the back of the "QE II" trade, with a push to Y83.00 likely, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Unless the Japanese authorities do something drastic, such as rinban purchases...and another round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the grind lower towards the 83.00 mark should continue." Good support should be met around that level on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears, they said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys maturing in seven years or less rebounded Friday while long-dated maturities recouped most of their earlier losses after a key gauge of the U.S. manufacturing sector fell.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The report from the Institute for Supply Management contrasted with other data showing a rise in U.S. consumer spending and expansion in China's manufacturing industry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ISM data prompted some investors to bet that the Federal Reserve needs to step up government-debt purchases, known as quantitative easing, to prevent a relapse of the economic slowdown. Fed Bank of New York President \f28 \uc2 \f2 William Dudley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , who votes on monetary policy, said Friday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is almost certain to offer fresh monetary stimulus. [ 03-10-10 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Treasurys today seem to have grasped that not only is additional QE likely in some form in the not-too-distant future as argued by Dudley but also that manufacturing activity in the U.S. is also now more than likely to shift downward," said Christopher Sullivan, who oversees $1.6 billion as chief investment officer at the United Nations Federal Credit Union in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1935 GMT Friday, the benchmark 10-year note was 2/32 lower to yield 2.519% and the 30-year bond was down 17/32 to yield 3.716%. The two-year note was flat at 0.422% and the seven-year note was up 2/32 to yield 1.899%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note posted a third straight weekly price advance, boosted by speculation about more Treasury purchases from the Fed. It yielded 2.612% at the end of the previous week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note's yield, a benchmark for consumer and corporate borrowings, touched 2.448% Tuesday, the weakest level since this year's trough of 2.418% set on Aug. 25, which was the lowest level since January 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policy makers are still split on the timing and form of additional monetary stimulus. Some even opposed the idea, arguing that such policy is unlikely to give a major boost to the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-term Japanese government bonds were lower Friday as Tokyo shares rallied with upward pressure on the yen easing, chipping away at demand for safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants will focus on whether the Bank of Japan eases monetary policy further at its policy board meeting Monday and Tuesday. If it does so, long-term JGB yields--which are already at multi-year lows--could fall even more.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The prevailing market view is that the BOJ will continue to take measures to help cushion the impact of the strong yen, so investors are likely to snap up bonds now to sell again when the yields show signs of dropping, said Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some traders also say long-term JGB yields may extend declines if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , albeit unlikely, decides to implement such drastic steps as increasing the amount of long-dated JGB purchases from the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Should the BOJ take such a radical measure, investors would become very eager to buy longer-term JGBs, pushing the yields sharply lower," a dealer at a Tokyo securities house said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ officials have long opposed the idea of increasing the size of the bank's JGB buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because it could fan market concern that the BOJ is handing the government a blank check for public spending projects, possibly undermining the credibility of Japanese sovereign debt in the long term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nine of the 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to expand liquidity-provision measures--including increases in the supply of six-month loans or purchases of more short-term government bills. The BOJ is also expected to keep its overnight call loan rate at 0.1%, unchanged since December 2008.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data on offer, Hong Kong's retail sales likely continued to post double-digit growth in August, bolstered by an increase in the number of tourists visiting the city and an improving labor market, though economists expect the growth rate slowed from July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of Hong Kong's retail sales likely rose 16.0% in August from the year-earlier month, smaller than July's 18.9% increase, according to the median forecast of five economists. In price-adjusted volume terms, retail sales likely rose 13.2%, down from 16.0% in July, according to the survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par BOJ Likely To Take Added Monetary-Easing Step Tues: Sources \par IMF Urges Broader Financial System Restructuring Beyond Basel \par IMF Urges Cooperation To Prevent '\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  War' \par S Korea's SK Energy Discovers More Crude In Vietnam Block \par China Pledges To Back Greece In Bond Issues: Greek Source \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  Expands Sakai Plant To Meet Asia Demand \par China Encourages More Foreign Investments In Coming Years - Report \par =DJ EU's Offer To Give Up IMF Board Seats Likely To Be Denied \par Australia Warns China, Europe On Trade \par China Premier Wen: China Will Not Reduce Euro-Bond Holdings - Xinhua \par China Premier Wen: Applauds Greek Efforts To Tackle Debt Crisis -Xinhua \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xinjiang Goldwind\f28 \uc2 \f2  Prices HK IPO At Top End Of Range At HK$17.98 Per Share - Source \par AIG Forced To Lower Valuation Of AIA IPO - Report \par Report Says Algorithm Set Off Flash Crash \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans 5 For 1 Stock Split \par US Consumer Spending, Incomes Rise In August \par US Auto Sales Indicate Strong Rise In September \par Obama Pledges To Support Russia's WTO Bid \par Geithner Says Oversight Council Needs To Move Quickly \par Fisher Says Need, Effectiveness Of Further Aid Unclear \par =Honda September US Sales Grew 26% On Strong Truck Demand \par =Nissan Reports 34% Jump In September US Auto Sales \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2350  Japan        Sep Monetary Base \par 0830  Hong Kong    Aug Retail Sales \par 0830  UK           Sep CIPS Construction PMI \par 0900  EU           Aug PPI \par 1400  US           Aug Pending Home Sales Index \par 1400  US           Aug Manufacturers' Shipments, Inventories & Orders (M3) \par 1900  US           Fed Chmn Bernanke participates in a discussion with college \par                    students in Rhode Island \par 2330  US           Fed Chmn Bernanke speaks on fiscal sustainability and rules \par                    in Rhode Island \par N/A   Japan        Aug Provisional Labour Survey - Earnings, Employment \par                    & Hours Worked \par N/A   Japan        Oct Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting \par N/A   UK           Bank of England Deputy Governor Paul \par                    Tucker speech at Mansion House Conference \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-10-10 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101003e6a30004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
