 "The main open strikes suggest that aluminium is likely to hold above the $2,300 level and copper above $8,000," says Sucden Financial. LME 3-month aluminum added $8 to $2,358/ton Friday. However trade likely to be subdued in Asia this week with China closed until Friday for national day holidays. Data focus likely to be on U.S. September non-farm payrolls Friday. Later today, euro-zone August PPI due at 0900 GMT, U.S. August pending home sales index at 1400 GMT, only data of note. Culmination of Ryder cup golf competition in European session, early U.S. could hurt trading volumes. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to trade in range with yields moving 2-3 bps either way as investors look for further cues on outlook for global economy, says trader with foreign bank. "For today, we'll see how equities perform. If stocks turn high, we might see some selling in bonds, but the moves won't be big." On Friday, 2-year bond yield up 1 bp to close at 0.42%, while 10-year up 2 bps to end at 2.04%. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +37.7% at HK$7.145 billion Thursday, as percentage of total market volume increased to 8.3% vs Wednesday's 6.1%, suggesting bears stepping up bet, expecting short-term correction with HSI +9.0% in September. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) topped list with HK$874.87 million, followed by Hutchison (0013.HK) with HK$632.60 million, \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$586.70 million. Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK), \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) rounded out top 5, with HK$342.93 million, HK$303.52 million respectively. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. 10-year Treasury yield may fall to 2.3% as expectations grow that Fed will loosen monetary policy in near term, Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Akihiro Nishida. But adds declines in long-term yields should be limited ahead of U.S. September jobs data due Friday. Tokyo traders say if data indicate that pace of possible U.S. economic slowdown won't become as fast as investors expect, that could make some players willing to start selling Treasurys to lock in profits. 10-year yield at 2.519% in NY Friday, 5-year yield at 1.263%, 2-year yield at 0.422%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold hits new record high of $1,321.80/oz in New York Friday before record close at $1,318.60/oz. Spot gold last at $1,319.90/oz, up $1.30 early in Asia. Yellow metal was again driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after EUR/USD broke through 1.3700 level and reached 6-month high as another top Federal Reserve official hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will launch second monetary stimulus program. Poor U.S. ISM manufacturing data also added to the mix. "We're focused on economic data because it could prompt the Fed into that second round of QE that would potentially pressure the dollar and further support gold prices," says David Meger, director of metals trading at Vision Financial Markets in Chicago. Silver also hit new 3-year high of $22.17/oz before settling at $22.09/oz; last at $22.14/oz, up 5 cents. LME base metals closed higher Friday, propelled by weaker USD, fresh buying from macro funds, brightened demand outlook in China after well-received PMI data. LME 3-month copper rose $85 vs Thursday's kerb to close at $8,095, highest close since August 2008. Lead was up $18 at $2,295, zinc up $34 at $2,229, tin was closing in on its 2008 record high, up $700 to $24,900; nickel up $450 to $23,845 while aluminum $8 higher at $2,358. Platinum, palladium also rallied, up 1.5%, 1.8% respectively. In early Asia, spot platinum at $1,676/oz, up $1, while spot palladium at $572/oz, down $1. Nymex November crude futures down 5 cents early in Asia at $81.53/bbl after 2% leap Friday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,130.4 late Friday in Seoul, extending decline to 7th straight session as downward momentum builds after successive breach of key support levels including 1,150 and 1,140 last week. U.S. stocks end up again on Friday, boding well for local stocks, KRW, traders say. "Dollar selling by offshore players is just so strong...while the greenback is weakening globally," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,122-1,133 range. Traders note depth of pair's fall will likely depend on extent of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem KRW gains recently amid concerns a stronger KRW may sap an export-led recovery. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely open higher with gains for U.S. market Friday likely to inspire further foreign buying; hopes surrounding upcoming 3Q corporate earnings this month could help index to test 1900 level near term, analysts say. But Kospi's recent rally, strengthening KRW may cap gains. "\uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' third-quarter earnings guidance due later this week will likely set a positive tone for the market," says \uc2 Daewoo Securities\uc2  in report; adds, KRW strength, bearish sentiment on tech sector recently may limit gains. Kospi, ends +0.2% at 1876.73 Friday, targets 1900. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  says some automakers, shipbuilders, refinery, chemical, machinery issues that rallied in recent weeks on hopes of strong 3Q earnings may face profit-taking pressure while techs limited by recent falls of chip, panel prices, still-sluggish demand. (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.930% vs Friday's 0.955% amid growing expectations that BOJ will take additional monetary easing steps on Tuesday, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. But Tokyo traders say falls in long-term yields would be limited as many players will likely shy away from active trading due to persisting uncertainty over what kind of measures BOJ will decide to implement. Lead December JGB futures may move in 143.30-143.50 vs Friday's 143.32. BOJ scheduled to hold 2-day policy board meeting set to start today; 9 out of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to expand liquidity-provision measures, including potential increase in supply of 6-month loans or more purchases of short-term government bills. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 4.8 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling in 6 days may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 14.6 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.8 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to be weighed by broad-based USD weakness in Asia, with players closely focused on 82.87, 15-year low marked just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 15, says Osao Iizuka, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Sumitomo Trust\uc2  and Banking. Following comments Friday by \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President Dudley that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost certain to opt for further easing, Bank of Japan could further pressure USD/JPY down this week if it fails to impress markets with any additional easing measures of its own at end of regular 2-day policy-setting meeting Tuesday, Iizuka says. "While concern over further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep the dollar supported above Y82.87 for today, the BOJ meeting tomorrow could bring that level closer in sight, depending on the outcome." Pair last 83.33. Says USD weakness means more rises may be in store for EUR/USD. After pair earlier tops 1.3800, its highest since March 17, says could rise to 1.3850 later in global day. Last 1.3778. Meanwhile EUR/JPY may trade in band around 115.00, last 114.82.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise on rebound in U.S. stocks even after ISM manufacturing data showed sector expanded at slower pace in September than previous month. "Investors are likely to take a wait-and-see attitude since they want to see policy developments from the BOJ (policy board) meeting," says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. Adds market players cautious USD/JPY (now 83.35) may still weaken even if BOJ announces additional monetary easing on Tuesday unless surprise steps come out. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9350-9500 vs 9404.23 (+0.4%) Friday close. Euro-linked shares like \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) may rise on firmer EUR/JPY (now 114.85); commodity-related stocks expected to advance on rise in crude and gold prices Friday. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9435 vs Osaka close at 9390 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 1958ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101003e6a30004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
