ersisting uncertainty over what kind of measures BOJ will decide to implement. Lead December JGB futures may move in 143.30-143.50 vs Friday's 143.32. BOJ scheduled to hold 2-day policy board meeting set to start today; 9 out of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to expand liquidity-provision measures, including potential increase in supply of 6-month loans or more purchases of short-term government bills. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 4.8 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling in 6 days may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 14.6 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.8 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to be weighed by broad-based USD weakness in Asia, with players closely focused on 82.87, 15-year low marked just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 15, says Osao Iizuka, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Sumitomo Trust\uc2  and Banking. Following comments Friday by \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President Dudley that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost certain to opt for further easing, Bank of Japan could further pressure USD/JPY down this week if it fails to impress markets with any additional easing measures of its own at end of regular 2-day policy-setting meeting Tuesday, Iizuka says. "While concern over further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep the dollar supported above Y82.87 for today, the BOJ meeting tomorrow could bring that level closer in sight, depending on the outcome." Pair last 83.33. Says USD weakness means more rises may be in store for EUR/USD. After pair earlier tops 1.3800, its highest since March 17, says could rise to 1.3850 later in global day. Last 1.3778. Meanwhile EUR/JPY may trade in band around 115.00, last 114.82.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise on rebound in U.S. stocks even after ISM manufacturing data showed sector expanded at slower pace in September than previous month. "Investors are likely to take a wait-and-see attitude since they want to see policy developments from the BOJ (policy board) meeting," says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. Adds market players cautious USD/JPY (now 83.35) may still weaken even if BOJ announces additional monetary easing on Tuesday unless surprise steps come out. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9350-9500 vs 9404.23 (+0.4%) Friday close. Euro-linked shares like \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) may rise on firmer EUR/JPY (now 114.85); commodity-related stocks expected to advance on rise in crude and gold prices Friday. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9435 vs Osaka close at 9390 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD hits new low at 1.3108 in New York trade Friday, tracking USD's slide vs EUR, majors given possibility of U.S. Federal Reserve's quantitative measures to support economy. Investors chasing higher-yielding assets, as worries remain that low interest rate regime in U.S. may continue for extended period. Immediate psychological support at 1.3000. Pair now 1.3117 vs 1.3159 late Asia trade Friday. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong August retail sales growth likely remained in double digits, but likely slowed vs July on fading base factor. By value, tipped +16.0% on-year in August vs July's +18.9%; by volume +13.2% in August vs July's +16.0%, according to Dow Jones poll of 5 economists. "Domestic demand remains buoyant with the fall in the jobless rate and tourist arrivals continue to be resilient, but the higher base of comparison last year also weighs on the headline figure," Core Pacific-Yamaichi analyst Keith Lo says. \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  economist Paul Tang says retail sales growth likely fell to "high single digits" in 4Q due to higher base of comparison, as city's retail sales returned to growth since September last year. Data due 0830 GMT. (chester.yung@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 1958ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101003e6a30004d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
