\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: 10-Yr Tsy Yield May Fall To 2.3% - Strategist\b0\par\par\uc2 1,879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 01:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. 10-year Treasury yield may fall to 2.3% as expectations grow that Fed will loosen monetary policy in near term, Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Akihiro Nishida. But adds declines in long-term yields should be limited ahead of U.S. September jobs data due Friday. Tokyo traders say if data indicate that pace of possible U.S. economic slowdown won't become as fast as investors expect, that could make some players willing to start selling Treasurys to lock in profits. 10-year yield at 2.519% in NY Friday, 5-year yield at 1.263%, 2-year yield at 0.422%. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold hits new record high of $1,321.80/oz in New York Friday before record close at $1,318.60/oz. Spot gold last at $1,319.90/oz, up $1.30 early in Asia. Yellow metal was again driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after EUR/USD broke through 1.3700 level and reached 6-month high as another top Federal Reserve official hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will launch second monetary stimulus program. Poor U.S. ISM manufacturing data also added to the mix. "We're focused on economic data because it could prompt the Fed into that second round of QE that would potentially pressure the dollar and further support gold prices," says David Meger, director of metals trading at Vision Financial Markets in Chicago. Silver also hit new 3-year high of $22.17/oz before settling at $22.09/oz; last at $22.14/oz, up 5 cents. LME base metals closed higher Friday, propelled by weaker USD, fresh buying from macro funds, brightened demand outlook in China after well-received PMI data. LME 3-month copper rose $85 vs Thursday's kerb to close at $8,095, highest close since August 2008. Lead was up $18 at $2,295, zinc up $34 at $2,229, tin was closing in on its 2008 record high, up $700 to $24,900; nickel up $450 to $23,845 while aluminum $8 higher at $2,358. Platinum, palladium also rallied, up 1.5%, 1.8% respectively. In early Asia, spot platinum at $1,676/oz, up $1, while spot palladium at $572/oz, down $1. Nymex November crude futures down 5 cents early in Asia at $81.53/bbl after 2% leap Friday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2341 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,130.4 late Friday in Seoul, extending decline to 7th straight session as downward momentum builds after successive breach of key support levels including 1,150 and 1,140 last week. U.S. stocks end up again on Friday, boding well for local stocks, KRW, traders say. "Dollar selling by offshore players is just so strong...while the greenback is weakening globally," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,122-1,133 range. Traders note depth of pair's fall will likely depend on extent of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support USD. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities suspected of frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem KRW gains recently amid concerns a stronger KRW may sap an export-led recovery. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely open higher with gains for U.S. market Friday likely to inspire further foreign buying; hopes surrounding upcoming 3Q corporate earnings this month could help index to test 1900 level near term, analysts say. But Kospi's recent rally, strengthening KRW may cap gains. "\uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2 ' third-quarter earnings guidance due later this week will likely set a positive tone for 