\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Foreign Net Sell Orders On 4.8M Japan Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2010\par 01:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 4.8 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling in 6 days may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 14.6 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 9.8 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY likely to be weighed by broad-based USD weakness in Asia, with players closely focused on 82.87, 15-year low marked just before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 15, says Osao Iizuka, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 Sumitomo Trust\uc2  and Banking. Following comments Friday by \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President Dudley that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost certain to opt for further easing, Bank of Japan could further pressure USD/JPY down this week if it fails to impress markets with any additional easing measures of its own at end of regular 2-day policy-setting meeting Tuesday, Iizuka says. "While concern over further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep the dollar supported above Y82.87 for today, the BOJ meeting tomorrow could bring that level closer in sight, depending on the outcome." Pair last 83.33. Says USD weakness means more rises may be in store for EUR/USD. After pair earlier tops 1.3800, its highest since March 17, says could rise to 1.3850 later in global day. Last 1.3778. Meanwhile EUR/JPY may trade in band around 115.00, last 114.82.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2311 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise on rebound in U.S. stocks even after ISM manufacturing data showed sector expanded at slower pace in September than previous month. "Investors are likely to take a wait-and-see attitude since they want to see policy developments from the BOJ (policy board) meeting," says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities. Adds market players cautious USD/JPY (now 83.35) may still weaken even if BOJ announces additional monetary easing on Tuesday unless surprise steps come out. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9350-9500 vs 9404.23 (+0.4%) Friday close. Euro-linked shares like \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) may rise on firmer EUR/JPY (now 114.85); commodity-related stocks expected to advance on rise in crude and gold prices Friday. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9435 vs Osaka close at 9390 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD hits new low at 1.3108 in New York trade Friday, tracking USD's slide vs EUR, majors given possibility of U.S. Federal Reserve's quantitative measures to support economy. Investors chasing higher-yielding assets, as worries remain that low interest rate regime in U.S. may continue for extended period. Immediate psychological support at 1.3000. Pair now 1.3117 vs 1.3159 late Asia trade Friday. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong August retail sales growth likely remained in double digits, but likely slowed vs July on fading base factor. By value, tipped +16.0% on-year in August vs July's +18.9%; by volume +13.2% in August vs July's +16.0%, according to Dow Jones poll of 5 economists. "Domestic demand remains buoyant with the fall in the jobless rate and tourist arrivals continue to be resilient, but the higher base of comparison last year also weighs on the headline figure," Core Pacific-Yamaichi analyst Keith Lo says. \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  economist Paul Tang says retail sales growth likely fell to "high single digits" in 4Q due to higher base of comparison, as city's retail sales returned to growth since September last year. Data due 0830 GMT. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely biased higher on positive steer from Wall Street gains Friday; trade likely in relatively tight ranges, no key data today, markets looking to U.S. payrolls data later in week for clues about what Fed's likely to do next. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, broad USD weakness on expectations Fed will take on more quantitative easing measures likely to mean EUR still pressured higher, JPY traders remain on the watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY from Japanese authorities with USD/JPY pair near key 83.00 level. USD/JPY at 83.35 vs 83.32 in late NY trade Friday, EUR/JPY at 114.83 vs 114.81, EUR/USD at 1.3778 vs 1.3784. China markets shut. On data slate: Hong Kong retail sales due at 0830 GMT, BOJ's 2-day policy meeting begins. Later there's EU PPI, U.S. pending home sales, manufacturers' shipments, inventories & orders. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2251 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 should follow Wall Street's bullish lead today, albeit in extremely quiet trading because of public holidays in NSW state and China. IG Markets tips 0.3% opening rise to 4593.0, with financials, materials and energy stocks expected to generate most of the strength after their U.S. peers and commodity prices rallied on better-than-expected Chinese and U.S. economic data. On the charts, Friday's bounce on Wall Street should reassure the bulls, because Thursday's bearish key reversal patterns weren't validated by further weakness. Domestically, S&P/ASX 200 closed Friday above neckline support from the inverse head & shoulders pattern, suggesting it could soon test last week's high at 4698, which is close to the 61.8% retracement of the April to May decline. A break of that level could trigger a surge toward the April peak at 5025, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. But the market will be cautious before Tuesday's RBA meeting, U.S. third quarter earnings season and U.S. economic data, particularly Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls. Index last 4579.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares +0.3% at 3221.40 in light volume trading. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  broker Humphrey Sherratt says typical Monday trading compounded by domestic school holidays, holiday in parts of Australia, including Sydney; "It's just a very quiet start to the week." \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  (FPA.NZ) +1.7% at NZ$0.59 as investors cheered by news company sold its Cleveland, Australia site for A$21.5 million; "They are going to use those funds to retire debt. Without a doubt that is a positive." High NZD against USD weighing on exporters with Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) down 1.0% at NZ$3.04 but again volumes extremely tepid. Says given dearth of local data, market will now be looking ahead to tomorrow's NZ Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey of Business Opinion, RBA rate decision. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil market perched on sizable amount of bullish momentum that could easily push crude futures up another $2-$3 to $83-84 area before buying frenzy of recent days begins to subside, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Near-zero interest rate environment has created environment very conducive toward speculative purchases, signaled by USD hitting 6-month lows vs EUR. "Although we still have difficulty constructing a bullish fundamental case purely on fundamental merits, we are also cautioning against any attempts to pick a top to this sharp price up-spike that will continue to be driven more by economic perceptions than by supply-demand reality," Ritterbusch says. Nymex crude for November delivery now up 25 cents at $81.83/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2235 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.6% at 4611.0 after better-than-expected U.S. economic data and Chinese manufacturing data saw Wall Street recover most of the previous days' declines, while commodity prices rose as the U.S. dollar fell. DJIA up 0.4% at 10829.68, S&P 500 up 0.4% at 1146.24, Nasdaq flat at 2370.75. U.S. August ISM manufacturing index fell to 54.4 vs 54.0 expected. U.S. consumer spending rose 0.4% vs 0.3% expected, incomes rose 0.5% vs 0.3% expected. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  led the DJIA, rising 2%, while \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  rose 1.7%. \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  fell 3.1% on CEO appointment. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.1%. S&P 500 Materials rose 1.2%. BHP ADRs at A$39.94 vs local close at A$39.46. Spot gold up 0.6% at US$1,317.90. Nymex crude oil up 2.0% at US$81.58. LME copper up 1.1%, zinc up 1.5%, aluminum up 0.3%, nickel up 1.9%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD up 1.2% at 1.3791. AUD/USD up 0.5% at 0.9725. S&P/ASX 200 last 4579.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2143 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia Treasurer Wayne Swan will chew the fat with investors in New York this week, spreading the good word on Australia's miracle economy and no doubt his adroit handling of it. Swan set to attend IMF and World Bank meetings in Washington where he says discussion of global regulation will be on the agenda. "I'll also be talking to investors in New York about the strength of our economy, our strong fiscal position, and our commitment to responsible economic management," Treasurer writes in his weekly missive. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2137 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD will rally hard if RBA hikes on Tuesday, possibly having another run at parity, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Senior Strategist Annette Beacher. "The AUD has already had a significant boost on rising RBA expectations but one cannot rule out another rally towards parity within a risk-on trading session." Pair last 0.9727, traded 0.9745 in New York. Expects spread between 3-10 year bond futures implied yields to flatten towards zero on any hike and U.S.-Australia 10-year spread will travel towards 300 bps. Last 254 bps. (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 1922ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101003e6a300046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
