teer from Wall Street gains Friday; trade likely in relatively tight ranges, no key data today, markets looking to U.S. payrolls data later in week for clues about what Fed's likely to do next. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, broad USD weakness on expectations Fed will take on more quantitative easing measures likely to mean EUR still pressured higher, JPY traders remain on the watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY from Japanese authorities with USD/JPY pair near key 83.00 level. USD/JPY at 83.35 vs 83.32 in late NY trade Friday, EUR/JPY at 114.83 vs 114.81, EUR/USD at 1.3778 vs 1.3784. China markets shut. On data slate: Hong Kong retail sales due at 0830 GMT, BOJ's 2-day policy meeting begins. Later there's EU PPI, U.S. pending home sales, manufacturers' shipments, inventories & orders. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2251 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 should follow Wall Street's bullish lead today, albeit in extremely quiet trading because of public holidays in NSW state and China. IG Markets tips 0.3% opening rise to 4593.0, with financials, materials and energy stocks expected to generate most of the strength after their U.S. peers and commodity prices rallied on better-than-expected Chinese and U.S. economic data. On the charts, Friday's bounce on Wall Street should reassure the bulls, because Thursday's bearish key reversal patterns weren't validated by further weakness. Domestically, S&P/ASX 200 closed Friday above neckline support from the inverse head & shoulders pattern, suggesting it could soon test last week's high at 4698, which is close to the 61.8% retracement of the April to May decline. A break of that level could trigger a surge toward the April peak at 5025, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. But the market will be cautious before Tuesday's RBA meeting, U.S. third quarter earnings season and U.S. economic data, particularly Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls. Index last 4579.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares +0.3% at 3221.40 in light volume trading. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  broker Humphrey Sherratt says typical Monday trading compounded by domestic school holidays, holiday in parts of Australia, including Sydney; "It's just a very quiet start to the week." \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  (FPA.NZ) +1.7% at NZ$0.59 as investors cheered by news company sold its Cleveland, Australia site for A$21.5 million; "They are going to use those funds to retire debt. Without a doubt that is a positive." High NZD against USD weighing on exporters with Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) down 1.0% at NZ$3.04 but again volumes extremely tepid. Says given dearth of local data, market will now be looking ahead to tomorrow's NZ Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey of Business Opinion, RBA rate decision. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] Oil market perched on sizable amount of bullish momentum that could easily push crude futures up another $2-$3 to $83-84 area before buying frenzy of recent days begins to subside, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Near-zero interest rate environment has created environment very conducive toward speculative purchases, signaled by USD hitting 6-month lows vs EUR. "Although we still have difficulty constructing a bullish fundamental case purely on fundamental merits, we are also cautioning against any attempts to pick a top to this sharp price up-spike that will continue to be driven more by economic perceptions than by supply-demand reality," Ritterbusch says. Nymex crude for November delivery now up 25 cents at $81.83/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2235 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 0.6% at 4611.0 after better-than-expected U.S. economic data and Chinese manufacturing data saw Wall Street recover most of the previous days' declines, while commodity prices rose as the U.S. dollar fell. DJIA up 0.4% at 10829.68, S&P 500 up 0.4% at 1146.24, Nasdaq flat at 2370.75. U.S. August ISM manufacturing index fell to 54.4 vs 54.0 expected. U.S. consumer spending rose 0.4% vs 0.3% expected, incomes rose 0.5% vs 0.3% expected. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  led the DJIA, rising 2%, while \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  rose 1.7%. \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  fell 3.1% on CEO appointment. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.1%. S&P 500 Materials rose 1.2%. BHP ADRs at A$39.94 vs local close at A$39.46. Spot gold up 0.6% at US$1,317.90. Nymex crude oil up 2.0% at US$81.58. LME copper up 1.1%, zinc up 1.5%, aluminum up 0.3%, nickel up 1.9%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD up 1.2% at 1.3791. AUD/USD up 0.5% at 0.9725. S&P/ASX 200 last 4579.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2143 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia Treasurer Wayne Swan will chew the fat with investors in New York this week, spreading the good word on Australia's miracle economy and no doubt his adroit handling of it. Swan set to attend IMF and World Bank meetings in Washington where he says discussion of global regulation will be on the agenda. "I'll also be talking to investors in New York about the strength of our economy, our strong fiscal position, and our commitment to responsible economic management," Treasurer writes in his weekly missive. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2137 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD will rally hard if RBA hikes on Tuesday, possibly having another run at parity, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Senior Strategist Annette Beacher. "The AUD has already had a significant boost on rising RBA expectations but one cannot rule out another rally towards parity within a risk-on trading session." Pair last 0.9727, traded 0.9745 in New York. Expects spread between 3-10 year bond futures implied yields to flatten towards zero on any hike and U.S.-Australia 10-year spread will travel towards 300 bps. Last 254 bps. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2132 GMT [Dow Jones] RBA won't hike on Tuesday or for some time ahead either and one only has to look at sickly credit growth numbers to draw that conclusion, says \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2  Chief Economist \uc2 Carl Weinberg\uc2 . Notes in particular 1.4% year-on-year drop in non-mortgage lending and 6.3% drop in year-on-year business lending point to very weak demand, which in turn translates into business struggling to access credit. "We expect the downward trend in retail sales and consumer spending growth to extend." (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 10-03-10 1920ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101003e6a300045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
