\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-BOJ may ease next week ahead of Fed - Muto\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 06:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 * Says more JGB buying would boost BOJ coordination with govt\par \par \uc2 * Yen gains cannot be stopped with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone -Muto\par \par \uc2 * Govt must follow up to make \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a success -Muto (Adds quotes, background from paragraph 6)\par \par \uc2 By Rie Ishiguro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 1 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan may ease policy next week as inaction would run the risk of spurring further yen gains, given the prospects for easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve, former BOJ Deputy Governor Toshiro Muto said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The yen has appreciated in lockstep with narrowing interest rate differentials between Japan and the United States. So if the Federal Reserve moves to ease, this runs the risk of prompting further yen gains unless the Bank of Japan acts," Muto told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "It is possible that the BOJ will act at its policy meeting at the beginning of October."\par \par \uc2 The BOJ will hold its next monetary policy meeting on Oct. 4-5, as worries mount about the fragile economic recovery due to the yen's rise, slowing overseas demand and stalling output growth.\par \par \uc2 Muto said another possibility was for the BOJ to wait to ease until the following policy meeting on Oct. 28, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to downgrade its growth forecasts in a twice-yearly outlook report.\par \par \uc2 Muto said it would be difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop yen gains with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone so policy coordination between the government and the BOJ would be desirable.\par \par \uc2 Data released on Thursday showed that Japanese authorities spent 2.12 trillion yen ($25.39 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the month to Sept. 28, roughly in line with market estimates for Sept. 15 alone, the only confirmed instance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nTOE68T08I]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at a critical juncture as its credibility would be affected if it does not succeed in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ... \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to act to achieve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and should not stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with just a single shot," he said.\par \par \uc2 Muto said an increase in the BOJ's government bond buying from the current annual pace of 21.6 trillion yen was one way of boosting cooperation with the government, which is now considering another stimulus budget.\par \par \uc2 He also said there was room for the bank to make better use of quantitative easing, a policy of flooding markets with excess cash that is generally believed to have had limited effect in stimulating the economy when it was implemented from 2001 to 2006.\par \par \uc2 He said he could not single out one policy option that was likely to be effective in countering downside economic risks. But by showing its resolve to act, the BOJ could change the market's perception that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has limited policy options.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ needs to explore new policy options," he said.\par \par \uc2 Muto was deputy BOJ governor from 2003 until 2008. He is now chairman of \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He spent nearly 37 years in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was vice finance minister before leaving to join the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Muto said in the interview that Japan faces a difficult task of simultaneously ensuring economic growth and pursuing fiscal reform, with its budget for the fiscal year from next April likely to be a good indicator of whether it would be able to achieve debt reduction targets.\par \par \uc2 He expected that, for the 2011/12 fiscal year, the Japanese government would put on hold plans to cut the corporate income tax rate, now around 40 percent and the highest among G7 countries. ($1=83.49 Yen) (Additional reporting by Izumi Nakagawa; Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (INTERVIEW, UPDATE 1, TV)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101004e6a10006z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
