\par\par \b \uc2 RPT- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro, Aussie edge up as China data supports risk\b0\par\par\uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 02:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Euro eyes 5-month high of $1.3684\par \par \uc2 * Dollar index edges lower, eyes latest 8-month low\par \par \uc2 * Aussie dollar buoyed after China PMI at 53.8\par \par \uc2 By Charlotte Cooper\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 1 (Reuters) - The euro edged up towards a five-month high on the U.S. dollar on Friday and the Australian dollar gained after upbeat Chinese data encouraged a little risk-taking in the higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of U.S. indicators.\par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped 0.1 percent to 83.47 yen, but stayed above the previous day's low at 83.16 yen and last month's 15-year trough below 83.00 that had prompted Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years.\par \par \uc2 Chinese manufacturing gathered momentum last month, with the official purchasing managers' index rising to 53.8 from 51.7 in August, beating forecasts and providing further evidence the economy is pulling smoothly out of a second-quarter swoon.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the data provided a sharp contrast to the situation in the United States, with the market speculating the Federal Reserve will take more quantitative easing steps even though economic numbers the previous day injected a little caution into that expectation.\par \par \uc2 "It's a perfect storm for risk and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to perform well. The U.S. is struggling with low inflation and high unemployment so there's a lot of focus on QE2 which should drive the dollar down further," said Gerrard Katz, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading for Hong Kong at Standard Chartered.\par \par \uc2 "And you have reasonable growth elsewhere ... Asia, as with that China PMI number, is performing strongly."\par \par \uc2 The euro rose 0.2 percent to $1.3663 after hitting a five-month peak of $1.3684 on Thursday helped by data showing euro zone banks are relying less on funds from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro has rallied 15 percent against the dollar since hitting a low in June. It has pushed up through its 55- and 100-week moving averages this week but not yet taken out resistance at its April high of $1.3692.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index eased 0.1 percent to 78.627 after dropping as far as 78.414 on Thursday, its weakest in eight months.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rose 0.1 percent to $0.9668, after hitting a two-year high of $0.9734 on Thursday. Higher interest rates and demand for Australia's resources, especially from China, have fuelled its rally.\par \par \uc2 EYES ON JAPAN'S STANCE\par \par \uc2 Data on Thursday showed new U.S. jobless claims fell last week and regional manufacturing grew faster than expected. Analysts and traders say the market will be watching U.S. indicators closely to gauge whether the Fed will act.\par \par \uc2 "If we see some good U.S. data it would not be a surprise to see market speculation about the possibility of the Fed embarking on quantitative easing in November fade a bit," said Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2 The dollar could get a lift against the euro on position unwinding if that happens, Shioiri said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was supported against the yen by market nervousness that Japanese authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts say one potential trigger point could be a drop to the 82.87 yen 15-year low.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said on Friday he would continue to take decisive steps on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves when necessary.\par \par \uc2 Despite such rhetoric, some traders said they had doubts about how willing Japanese authorities were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I think the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was intended to buy time before (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's half-year end) the end of September," said a trader for a major Japanese bank.\par \par \uc2 Japan's recent feud with China has underscored the importance of U.S.-Japan ties, and such diplomatic considerations may prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be cautious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the trader said.\par \par \uc2 "With the half-year end behind us and the United States becoming more politically important for Japan, you have to wonder how much action they can take that may meet U.S. opposition, especially with a G20 meeting coming up," he said.\par \par \uc2 Data late on Thursday showed Japanese authorities spent 2.12 trillion yen, or $25.4 billion, on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the month to Sept. 28.\par \par \uc2 This was roughly in line with market estimates of 2 trillion for Sept. 15 alone, the only confirmed instance of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during that period. (Additional reporting by Reuters \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Krishna Kumar in Sydney and Masayuki Kitano in Tokyo; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|GNN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020101001e6a1000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
