\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,223 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 12:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE MANUFACTURING SLOWED IN SEPTEMBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The growth of manufacturing activity in the 16 countries that share the euro slowed in September, while activity declined in Ireland, Spain and Greece, according to a monthly survey of purchasing managers released Friday. (published 0814 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU'S REHN: IN FAVOR OF EXPLORING FINANCIAL TRANSACTION TAX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European Commission is backing a proposal to impose a tax on cross-border \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trades and other financial transactions, the European Union's Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs Olli Rehn said Friday. (published 0731 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN RETAIL SALES DECLINE UNEXPECTEDLY BY 0.2% IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail sales in Germany fell unexpectedly in August, casting doubt on a strong recovery of the country's weak private consumption. (published 0600 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA'S OFFICIAL SEPTEMBER PMI UP, EASING SLOWDOWN WORRIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing activity expanded in September and accelerated from the previous month, an official gauge showed Friday, adding to evidence that Chinese growth remains robust and that its slowdown from extraordinary growth rates earlier this year will be mild. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PM KAN: EXPECT BOJ TO TAKE FURTHER NECESSARY STEPS TO BEAT DEFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan Friday renewed his hopes for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take more policy steps to beat deflation and reminded the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market that he won't tolerate sharp moves in the yen. (published 0503 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN CPI FALLS FOR 18TH MONTH; DEFLATION PERSISTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's consumer prices fell in August for the 18th consecutive month, government data showed Friday, suggesting no end in sight for a deflationary trend that has been undermining the broader economy, and providing further impetus for the Bank of Japan to take additional monetary easing measures soon. (published 0458 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ: CONSUMER SENTIMENT WORSENS FOR FIRST TIME IN SIX QUARTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment among Japanese consumers deteriorated for the first time in six quarters in July-September, a Bank of Japan survey showed Friday, suggesting that an uncertain economic outlook is starting to weigh more heavily on the household sector. (published 0650 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER RENEWS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREAT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister renewed his pledge Friday to take "decisive" steps to fight the yen's rise, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edges back toward last month's 15-year high against the dollar that triggered Tokyo's first market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in more than six years. (published 0249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE UNEMPLOYMENT RATE 10.1% IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's unemployment rate was unchanged at 10.1% in August, although the number of people without work fell by 20,000 to 15.9 million. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THREE-MONTH EURIBOR AT OVER 14-MO HIGH AS EXCESS CASH FALLS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of borrowing three-month euros in the interbank market Friday rose to its highest level in over 14 months as surplus cash in the financial system fell after banks repaid EUR225 billion of loans to the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0948 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PORTUGAL UNION GROUP PROPOSES GENERAL STRIKE FOR NOVEMBER 24 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The General Confederation of Portuguese Workers, known as CGTP, proposed late Thursday a general strike on Nov. 24 to protest a series of budget deficit-cutting measures. (published 0927 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN PM CONFIRMS PLEDGE TO CARRY OUT FURTHER ECONOMIC REFORMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish Prime Minister Jose Luis Rodriguez Zapatero Friday said his government remains fully committed to carrying out additional structural reforms with the aim of stimulating more economic growth, including reform of the country's industry, energy sector and pensions. (published 0821 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA AUG EXPORTS UP 22.5% ON YEAR AT $16.64 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's merchandise exports rose 22.5% to $16.64 billion in August from a year earlier, provisional data issued Friday by the Ministry of Commerce showed. (published 0551 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA SEPTEMBER PERFORMANCE OF MANUFACTURING INDEX -4.4 POINTS TO 47.3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector contracted in September for the first time this year as factory production was hit hard by a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rising costs, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Friday shows. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED'S PIANALTO POINTS TO SIGNS ECONOMY IS STARTING TO MEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there are signs the U.S. is starting to recover from the deepest downturn since the Great Depression, the Federal Reserve has options if the economy ends up needing more help to heal, Cleveland Federal Reserve President Sandra Pianalto said Thursday. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURY TO SELL CITIGROUP TRUST SECURITIES FOR $2.25B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Treasury Department Thursday moved to sell down its \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . holdings, the latest government effort to unwind the hefty ownership stakes it took in financial institutions at the height of the financial crisis. (published 2320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par INTERVIEW: S&P ANALYST: BOJ, GOV SHOULD DO MORE VS DEFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "are not doing their job," and should take stronger steps to put an end to the deflationary pressures facing the economy, according to a senior analyst at the ratings agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 0929 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : EURO BOUNCES OVER $1.37; DOLLAR EYEING DATA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro rose to a new six-month high of $1.3733 Friday, carried on a fresh wave of positive risk sentiment. (published 0949 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: '\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WAR' ONLY LIKELY TO ESCALATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The competitive devaluations that are becoming common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  across the globe are likely to intensify in coming weeks as the dollar, as well as most of other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , remain out of favor. (published 0720 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA, INDIA PMI, KOREAN EXPORTS SHOW ASIA STILL ON SOLID FOOTING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An expansion of manufacturing in China and India, and strong Korean exports in September suggest Asia's economies will remain on a solid footing in coming months, even as they contend with a pickup in food inflation. (published 0731 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOST ECONOMISTS EXPECT BOJ TO EXPAND LIQUIDITY NEXT WEEK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is expected to take more monetary easing steps at its two-day policy board meeting starting Monday, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's own business sentiment survey reveals deepening concerns about the outlook for the corporate sector and the yen's uptrend shows no signs of abating, a Dow Jones Newswires poll showed. (published 0539 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS HEADING LOWER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Support at 130.97 is set to face renewed pressure following Thursday's bearish outside day setback off 132.20. 132.20 has become a near-term bull failure, and a break below 130.97 would bring the Sep. 24 higher low at 130.68 back into the picture. Corrective gains need to force a break above 131.89 in order to lift the tone, opening 132.20. (published 0727 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: CHF WEAKNESS TO EXTEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The strong upward bias continues, and is set to challenge the April 12 lower high at 1.3692. Tuesday's sustained break above 1.3507 keeps bulls in control of the near-term, setting their sights on projected resistance at 1.3702. A fresh wave of bull pressure is required to force a break above 1.3702, creating scope for 1.3896. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 1.3495, and only below there would concern bulls. (published 0611 GMT) [ 01-10-10 1015GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 D