\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 940 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 08:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA'S OFFICIAL SEPTEMBER PMI UP, EASING SLOWDOWN WORRIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's manufacturing activity expanded in September and accelerated from the previous month, an official gauge showed Friday, adding to evidence that Chinese growth remains robust and that its slowdown from extraordinary growth rates earlier this year will be mild. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN PM KAN: EXPECT BOJ TO TAKE FURTHER NECESSARY STEPS TO BEAT DEFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan Friday renewed his hopes for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take more policy steps to beat deflation and reminded the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market that he won't tolerate sharp moves in the yen. (published 0503 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN CPI FALLS FOR 18TH MONTH; DEFLATION PERSISTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's consumer prices fell in August for the 18th consecutive month, government data showed Friday, suggesting no end in sight for a deflationary trend that has been undermining the broader economy, and providing further impetus for the Bank of Japan to take additional monetary easing measures soon. (published 0458 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ: CONSUMER SENTIMENT WORSENS FOR FIRST TIME IN SIX QUARTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sentiment among Japanese consumers deteriorated for the first time in six quarters in July-September, a Bank of Japan survey showed Friday, suggesting that an uncertain economic outlook is starting to weigh more heavily on the household sector. (published 0650 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN RETAIL SALES DECLINE UNEXPECTEDLY BY 0.2% IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retail sales in Germany fell unexpectedly in August, casting doubt on a strong recovery of the country's weak private consumption. (published 0600 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par *EU'S REHN: IN FAVOR OF EXPLORING FINANCIAL TRANSACTION TAX (published 0656 GMT)  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FINANCE MINISTER RENEWS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  THREAT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister renewed his pledge Friday to take "decisive" steps to fight the yen's rise, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  edges back toward last month's 15-year high against the dollar that triggered Tokyo's first market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in more than six years. (published 0249 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA AUG EXPORTS UP 22.5% ON YEAR AT $16.64 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's merchandise exports rose 22.5% to $16.64 billion in August from a year earlier, provisional data issued Friday by the Ministry of Commerce showed. (published 0551 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: CHINA ISSUES JAPAN TRAVEL WARNING CITING ALLEGED ATTACK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China has issued a travel warning for Japan, reflecting continued strains between the two neighbors despite efforts to ratchet down tensions. (published 0411 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA SEPTEMBER \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  MANUFACTURING PMI 48.8 VS 50.9 IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in South Korea's manufacturing sector slowed for a fifth straight month in September, with a key gauge measuring activity falling below the neutral level for the first time in 19 months, amid escalating concerns over the strength of the global economic recovery. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA: GOVERNMENT DEBT ESTIMATED TO RISE TO KRW400.4 TLN AT END 2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's government debt load is expected to rise to KRW400.4 trillion, or 34.7% of gross domestic product, by the end of this year, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Friday. (published 0600 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA TO REMOVE TARIFFS ON CABBAGE, RADISH IMPORTS BY END-2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea will temporarily remove its tariffs on cabbage and radish imports and buy 150 tons of the fresh vegetables from China in an effort to help curb soaring fresh vegetable prices, the Ministry of Food, Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries said Friday. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA SEPTEMBER PERFORMANCE OF MANUFACTURING INDEX -4.4 POINTS TO 47.3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector contracted in September for the first time this year as factory production was hit hard by a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rising costs, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Friday shows. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED'S PIANALTO POINTS TO SIGNS ECONOMY IS STARTING TO MEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there are signs the U.S. is starting to recover from the deepest downturn since the Great Depression, the Federal Reserve has options if the economy ends up needing more help to heal, Cleveland Federal Reserve President Sandra Pianalto said Thursday. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURY TO SELL CITIGROUP TRUST SECURITIES FOR $2.25B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Treasury Department Thursday moved to sell down its \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . holdings, the latest government effort to unwind the hefty ownership stakes it took in financial institutions at the height of the financial crisis. (published 2320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par MOST ECONOMISTS EXPECT BOJ TO EXPAND LIQUIDITY NEXT WEEK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is expected to take more monetary easing steps at its two-day policy board meeting starting Monday, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's own business sentiment survey reveals deepening concerns about the outlook for the corporate sector and the yen's uptrend shows no signs of abating, a Dow Jones Newswires poll showed. (published 0539 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: CHF WEAKNESS TO EXTEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: The strong upward bias continues, and is set to challenge the April 12 lower high at 1.3692. Tuesday's sustained break above 1.3507 keeps bulls in control of the near-term, setting their sights on projected resistance at 1.3702. A fresh wave of bull pressure is required to force a break above 1.3702, creating scope for 1.3896. Corrective weakness will attract support while above 1.3495, and only below there would concern bulls. (published 0611 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS: DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS DECLINE ON SLIGHT SPOT MOVES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen options edged down Friday, as the underlying exchange rate stayed in a narrow range, but further falls appear unlikely as bargain-hunting may emerge below current low levels. (published 0420 GMT) [ 01-10-10 0659GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101001e6a1000fo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
