\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen's Dollar Strength, Euro Weakness Creates Japan Policy Quandry\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kosaku Narioka   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 08:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen's continuing rebound against the dollar, even as it weakens against the euro, is creating a policy dilemma for the Japanese government over whether it can justify additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push the yen lower in order to help the export-dependent economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan undertook large-scale yen selling against the dollar on Sept. 15 as it became increasingly concerned about the damage being done to the economy by the stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Figures released Thursday said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y2.125 trillion for the month, although it did not break out details on whether all of the selling was in the initial day or over a longer period. Analysts had estimated that sales totaled Y1.8 trillion to Y2.0 trillion in the first day's wave of selling in Asia, Europe and North America.\par \par \uc2 The selling program was an initial success, pushing the dollar up to over Y85.60 yen from its low of Y82.87, which in turn sent the euro up to around Y111.50 from around Y107.70.\par \par \uc2 With the euro discovering some new-found popularity, it has continued to rise against the yen. At 0450 GMT on Friday it was quoted at Y114.03, compared with Y113.72 in late New York trading Thursday, according to data from EBS.\par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, it has been a different story. Amid weakening U.S. economic figures, there is a growing view that the U.S. Federal Reserve will undertake an aggressive program of quantitative easing. This is expected to further push down U.S. interest rates and make the dollar less attractive.\par \par \uc2 At 0450 GMT the dollar was quoted at Y83.43, compared with Y83.44 in late New York trading Thursday.\par \par \uc2 This has left traders wary about taking a position and the dollar/yen in a narrow range. Through the Asian day, the dollar wandered from Y83.38 to Y83.58.\par \par \uc2 Because of the split trend, it will be more difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to a senior trader at a major Japanese trust bank. "It is not likely that Japan will get the green light from the United States and Europe. If Japan acts, it will have to act alone," he said. "It is hard (for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again), but you never know when they may move. That makes our job more difficult," he said, adding that "we can't short at this level. And that's why the dollar isn't moving against the yen that much," he added.\par \par \uc2 Traders in Asia also said that strong U.S. data overnight had not changed the overall mood. Second-quarter gross domestic product data, weekly jobless claims and a closely watched survey of Chicago-area purchasing managers were generally stronger than expected.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. figures have not changed the broader trend of dollar weakness," said Satoshi Tate, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the euro was up against the dollar, rising to $1.3664 at 0450 GMT from $1.3632 in late New York trade.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the euro and yen, was at 78.622 from 78.762 late Thursday in New York.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:5