midpoint of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2% to 4% inflation target band for the first time since January. (published 2300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =DATA SNAP: S KOREA EXPORTS UP 17.2% ON YEAR IN SEPTEMBER VS +13.5% FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's exports grew faster than expected in September despite widespread concerns over the health of the global economic recovery, boosting the nation's trade surplus to $5.01 billion from $1.72 billion previously, according to the preliminary trade data from the Ministry of Knowledge Economy Friday. (published 0002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA GOVERNMENT OFFICIAL: ANNUAL INFLATION LIKELY LOW-3% IN 4Q \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unexpected jump in South Korea's inflation rate in September stems mainly from a temporary lack of supply of agricultural products due to heavier than usual rain in summer, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Friday. (published 0103 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA SEPTEMBER \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  MANUFACTURING PMI 48.8 VS 50.9 IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Growth in South Korea's manufacturing sector slowed for a fifth straight month in September, with a key gauge measuring activity falling below the neutral level for the first time in 19 months, amid escalating concerns over the strength of the global economic recovery. (published 0050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA SEPTEMBER PERFORMANCE OF MANUFACTURING INDEX -4.4 POINTS TO 47.3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing sector contracted in September for the first time this year as factory production was hit hard by a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rising costs, a performance gauge produced by an industry group published Friday shows. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: FED'S PIANALTO POINTS TO SIGNS ECONOMY IS STARTING TO MEND \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there are signs the U.S. is starting to recover from the deepest downturn since the Great Depression, the Federal Reserve has options if the economy ends up needing more help to heal, Cleveland Federal Reserve President Sandra Pianalto said Thursday. (published 0004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: TREASURY TO SELL CITIGROUP TRUST SECURITIES FOR $2.25B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Treasury Department Thursday moved to sell down its \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . holdings, the latest government effort to unwind the hefty ownership stakes it took in financial institutions at the height of the financial crisis. (published 2320 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par  \par =AL'S EMPORIUM: SEC'S ENFORCER HAS SOME UNFINISHED BUSINESS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More than 14,000 Cuban children were shipped to Miami between 1960 and 1962. One of them was Luis Aguilar, who at 56 is one of the five commissioners of the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission. (published 1300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-10-10 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101001e6a10004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
