\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Renews  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threat\b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 04:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from Noda, background on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister renewed his pledge Friday to take "decisive" steps to fight the yen's rise, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edges back toward last month's 15-year high against the dollar that triggered Tokyo's first market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 "We will take decisive steps when necessary going forward," Yoshihiko Noda said at a news conference, the day after the dollar touched a two-week low of Y83.16. "Our fundamental view is that the Japanese economy is gradually picking up, but...we are still in a severe situation" as far as the job market is concerned, he said.\par \par \uc2 Noda's comments come as the Japanese government ratchets up its verbal warnings against the rebounding yen, which economists say could combine with a slowdown in overseas economies to stuff out the country's fragile export-led growth. On Thursday, Mitsuru Sakurai, one of Noda's two senior deputies, reminded investors that the government "will take decisive steps to correct the yen's strength."\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa has also hardened his rhetoric against the rising yen, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to loosen its already easy monetary policy as early as a two-day policy meeting next week.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Ministry sold Y2.125 trillion last month in its first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than six years, according to data released Thursday. The effort helped the dollar recover to Y85.94 around the middle of September, compared with a 15-year low of Y82.87 posted before Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 There is also speculation, though unconfirmed by the government, that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have conducted a small-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 24.\par \par \uc2 But analysts say the impact of last month's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has mostly faded. Expectations for further U.S. monetary easing, coupled with concerns over decelerating U.S. and European economies, have rendered the dollar and the euro relatively less attractive, causing investors to once again buy the yen.\par \par \uc2 At around 0200 GMT, the dollar was changing hands at Y83.45.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 01-10-10 0249GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101001e6a10004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
