\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan's CPI Data Mark 18th Month Of Continuous Decline\b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 04:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details of data, analyst comments and background.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tomoyuki Tachikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's consumer prices fell in August for the 18th consecutive month, government data showed Friday, suggesting no end in sight for a deflationary trend that has been undermining the broader economy, and providing further impetus for the Bank of Japan to take additional monetary easing measures soon.\par \par \uc2 The country's core consumer price index, which excludes volatile fresh food prices, fell 1.0% from the same month a year earlier, the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications said. The result was in line with the median forecast in a poll of private economists.\par \par \uc2 The latest data show that the pace of decline in the core CPI is easing up, with the figure having fallen 1.1% in July and 1.0% in June compared with a recent low of 1.5% in April. This suggests that a mild recovery in the economy is propping up domestic demand and preventing prices from falling further.\par \par \uc2 Separate data released by the government also showed that the jobless rate improved slightly to 5.1% in August from 5.2% a month earlier, and that overall household spending rose 1.7% on year in real terms during the same period.\par \par \uc2 The spending figure has received a boost of late thanks to scorching summer temperatures increasing demand for cool beverages, air conditioning and the like.\par \par \uc2 But analysts warn that deflation may accelerate as the yen stays strong with the outlook for the U.S. and European economies uncertain, dragging on Japan's export-reliant economy and reducing the prices of oil, food, metals and other commodities the country buys from overseas.\par \par \uc2 Persistent price declines usually eat into corporate profits, which could prompt firms to scale down \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cut payrolls, increasing the risk that worsening income and employment conditions will make consumers reluctant to spend. That in turn should raise the possibility of Japan's economy entering a lull in the near future.\par \par \uc2 "As long as deflation continues and the yen rises, falling corporate profits could keep putting downward pressure on the economy," said Takeshi Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute. "It's very likely for the Japanese economy to stall down the road."\par \par \uc2 With pessimism about the outlook for the nation's economy intensifying, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be forced to implement drastic monetary easing steps to deter the economy from deteriorating further.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, political leaders have grown increasingly worried that a global slowdown and a 10% rise in the value of the yen this year will again send Japan's economy into a decline. According to the latest GDP figures, Japan's GDP grew at an annualized 1.5% in the three months ended June 30.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa also stepped up his remarks over the recent yen rises, saying that the strong yen can "significantly affect business sentiment." He pledged to take "appropriate policy steps" if the surging yen seriously hits \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.\par \par \uc2 Economists added that the Japanese government--which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Sept. 15 for the first time in more than six years--may face increasing pressure to do so again.\par \par \uc2 According to the BOJ's latest tankan survey, Japanese firms' profit forecasts for this fiscal year are based on an average dollar value of Y89.66. A dollar trading more than six yen below the expected rate could deal a sharp blow to corporate profits. As of 0155 GMT Friday, the U.S. dollar stood at Y83.44.\par \par \uc2 -By Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2787; tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Takashi Nakamichi contributed to this article.) [ 01-10-10 0208GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020101001e6a10003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
