\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls As Fed Officials' Comments Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 20:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell Friday against all major rivals, with the focus on potential Federal Reserve stimulus diverting attention from encouraging U.S. economic data.\par \par \uc2 A top Federal Reserve official said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is almost certain to have to offer fresh support to ensure that already-tepid economic growth does not further falter.\par \par \uc2 "The current situation is wholly unsatisfactory" and "both the current levels of unemployment and inflation and the timeframe over which they are likely to return to levels consistent with our mandate are unacceptable," \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  President \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  said in a prepared text for a speech in New York.\par \par \uc2 Dudley stopped short of saying what type of stimulus he prefers.\par \par \uc2 Charles Evans, president of the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2 --who is not a voting member this year of the policy-setting Federal Open Market Committee -- said further steps to stimulate the economy are desirable, but this requires "unconventional policy tools" due to the possibility that the U.S. may be in a "liquidity trap."\par \par \uc2 He also stopped short of specifically endorsing a second round of quantitative easing in prepared remarks delivered at a Bank of France conference in Rome.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, positive U.S. data failed to support the greenback, with the euro touching a six-month peak above $1.37 during choppy morning trading that stabilized later in the North American session.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traded at its lowest level since January.\par \par \uc2 Friday afternoon, the euro was at $1.3765 from $1.3632 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.40 from Y83.44, while the euro was at Y114.81 from Y113.72. The U.K. pound was at $1.5829 from $1.5706. The dollar was at CHF0.9770 from CHF0.9821. The ICE Dollar Index was at 78.174 from 78.762.\par \par \uc2 Better-than-expected Reuter's/University of Michigan confidence index, consumer spending and Institute for Supply Management manufacturing data did little to sway investors.\par \par \uc2 Dudley's comments Friday generated buzz comparable to the day's dose of U.S. data, said Matthew Strauss, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 "His is clearly another vote in favor of quantitative easing," he said. "It does seem that any quantitative easing would be a gradual process, rather than any 'shock and awe'-type effort."\par \par \uc2 The dollar didn't fall more than it did on Dudley's comments because investors have already priced in such measures, Strauss said.\par \par \uc2 Specifically, Dudley's reference to potentially "$500 billion of purchases" is only "slightly bigger" than the last round of Fed purchases, said Strauss.\par \par \uc2 "The Fed will still need to turn on the spigots but will probably only [need] to let a few drips out," said Kathy Lien, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at GFT \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Before the data, the euro already benefited from a run-up in risk due to better-than-expected Chinese manufacturing data overnight.\par \par \uc2 An expansion of manufacturing in China and India and strong South Korean exports in September suggested Asia's economies will remain on a solid footing in coming months. That favors \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to global growth.\par \par \uc2 China's purchasing managers' index rose to 53.8 from 51.7 in August, the China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped back a little against the yen, hovering close to Y83.15, its lowest level since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15 to hinder the yen's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 With Fed concerns as a tailwind, the Canadian dollar was trading particularly strong Friday, supported by gains in commodity and equity prices, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.0217, compared with C$1.0290 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Michael Derby in New York, Jacob Bunge in Rome and Brent Harder in Winnipeg contributed to this article.) [ 10-01-10 1435ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101001e6a1000ae\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
