\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Fall Slowed By  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 09:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0714 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY trending down in recent days, but pair only declining gradually as traders can't short pair as they fear further JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from Tokyo may emerge, says senior dealer at major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trust bank. Says Japan will have to act alone as assistance from U.S., Europe unlikely. "Even if they do it alone, the dollar will likely surge by Y2-Y3. A person like me in the interbank is scared about that. That's why we can't short at this level. And that's why the dollar isn't moving against the yen that much." Says chance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again may increase if pair goes below Thursday's new post-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  low at 83.16. Stop-loss orders accumulated below 82.80, slightly below level before Sept. 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 82.87. Says if pair breaks that level, players may sell heavily as stops executed. Pair last 83.48. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0710 GMT [Dow Jones] Short end European government bond yields are set to rise after banks voluntarily reduced their euro-denominated borrowings from the ECB at this week's ECB liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a London-based trader. "The drop in surplus money in the system is one of the most significant pieces of incremental news to hit the EU market in a long time," he says. This will exacerbate this month's index adjustments as the September 2011 maturity bonds fall out of the index basket. The lower demand for ECB liquidity support is a sign of the deleveraging process that has been taking place in the banking sector, with stronger banks able to stand alone with more confidence. However, "this 'freed up' leverage represents 'potential' demand for risk, but I don't see it being re-applied anytime soon," he says. "It's good we need less of the repo 'drug' but that doesn't make it easier for distressed institutions to issue." (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1,465.80 but off 1,469.77 intraday high as profit-taking hits blue chips. Still, gains in regional markets offering support, keeping benchmark index in positive territory. Market breadth negative with 326 losers vs 300 gainers; Dealer tips 1,460-1,470 range for rest of day. Construction shares higher on hopes of winning big government contracts; Gamuda (5398.KU) +0.3% at MYR3.89, Zelan (2283.KU) +4.4% at MYR0.715; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +4.3% at MYR3.13 after \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  upgrades company to Outperform, citing potential gains from MYR50 billion mass rail transit project, improving Singapore-Malaysia relations. Among losers, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 3.1% at MYR2.18 after having gained 34% in September; UEM Land Holdings (5148.KU) down 1.3% at MYR2.31 following 40% rise last month.(ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] This will now be war, war...not jaw, jaw, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. The competitive devaluations that are becoming common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the globe are likely to intensify in coming weeks as the USD, as well as most of other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , remain out of favor. The conflict is no doubt under discussion by leading finance minister and central bankers and will continue to be so as they bump into each other at various meetings over the next few weeks. But, that doesn't mean anything will be done about it. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. Congress is doing its part to put the U.S. back in rare earth production game--in competition with China. House of Representatives approved legislation earlier this week aimed at restoring country as a leading producer of elements known as rare earth. The 17 elements, or metals, are critical to production of computer disc drives and advanced windmills, as well as defense items such as aircraft controls and navigation systems. China produces almost all rare earth elements today. House passed bill which would support activities in Energy Department to find and develop rare earth sources, in part through loan guarantees; similar measure moving through Senate. Both measures supported by some in U.S. rare earth sector. China's ability to pull global trade levers in rare earth--despite its denials in trying to do so--has governments around world concerned their tech and defense industries could be starved of vital ingredients. While Beijing hasn't conceded to steering supplies, it has signaled dissatisfaction with too-low pricing of some elements. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time: U.S. Congress Spurs Rare Earth Race (james.areddy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sharply lower at 1,130.4 late vs 1,140.2 yesterday despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support, traders say; offshore players continue to sell as rising expectations of further US monetary easing expected to keep USD under pressure. BOK was suspected of buying in morning, afternoon trade; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  (buying USD) near 1,138-1,139, then once again near 1,130-1,131, say Seoul based traders. 2 traders estimate BOK probably bought around $1.5 billion-$2 billion. "I think we should now expect the pair to slip to as low as the year's low (at KRW1,102.6). There is no obvious support level," says local bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0652 GMT [Dow Jones] Clarity on the Anglo Irish bailout plan and the government's decision to shelve the remaining bond auctions planned for this year helped bring down yield spreads on Irish government bonds vs their German peers on Thursday. However Ireland's temporary exit from the market may make it harder to gauge investor demand once they return, says Nomura. "This may lead to a rise in price volatility and may require larger concessions prior to future issuance," Nomura says. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] China's IRS have tumbled in recent months as hedging from two insurers distorts fledgling market, mispricing inflation risk, strong economic growth, meaning IRS market can't function as sensitive barometer of economic, monetary-policy expectations, as it does in developed countries. Speculators not causing the severe pricing anomaly, market participants say: it's PICC Life Insurance and \uc2 Taikang Life Insurance\uc2 . Since April, the two repeatedly received fixed swap rates to hedge risk on floating-rate bond holdings, with orders big enough to drive market rates down, keep them there, say traders who have done transactions with one or both companies or seen the trades. Officials at PICC, Taikang didn't respond to repeated attempts to seek comment on IRS deals. Traders say they can't position for rates to rebound, because the companies come in frequently with huge orders whenever rates tick up. There aren't any big players with genuine demand to protect against higher rates, so PICC and Taikang dominate market. See: =FOCUS: China IRS Weighed By Insurer Hedges; Misprice Inflation (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0639 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are down Friday as European equities are poised to open slightly higher following an increase in China's official PMI. A set of better than expected US data and clarity on the Anglo Irish bailout plan weighed on safe haven bunds Thursday. Meanwhile, a lower than expected take-up at the ECB's 6-day liquidity facility brought down surplus cash in the banking system, hurting the front end of the curve. "Window dressing and index-buying is behind us while anxiety about rising funding rates is on the rise," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . The final release of euro zone manufacturing PMI is out 0800 GMT, followed by unemployment data at 0900 GMT. In the US, the ISM manufacturing report is due at 1400 GMT. December bunds are down 0.09 at 131.34 and the 10-year bund is down at 99.68 to yield 2.286% (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares likely to end in positive territory, underpinned by gains in other Asian bourses, firmer U.S. stock futures. Still, prices not expected to head much higher given absence of catalysts. STI +0.5% at 3112.36, with resistance at year-to-date high of 3125. Gainers still outnumbering decliners by 2 to 1 in broad market. After recent gains, Singapore market "not inexpensive", according to CLSA, as it trades at 14.3X forward earnings, or 1 standard deviation above its 5-year mean; "thus, we continue to recommend a selective stock picking strategy. The investment themes that will work best are those exposed to tourism, the trend of rising corporate relocation to Singapore, and dividend yields." Flags UOB (U11.SG), \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG), Golden Agri (E5H.SG), ComfortDelgro (C52.SG), Genting Singapore (G13.SG), \uc2 CapitaMall Trust\uc2  (C38U.SG), \uc2 Singapore Airlines\uc2  (C6L.SG) as favorites.(frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD falls to intraday low at 1.3619 as investors take profits after pair rose slightly through Asian hours, says senior dealer at major Japan investment funds. Notes EUR/USD "has continued to renew highs recently. There seems to be a sense that is overheating." Tips technical support at 1.3550 vs 1.3646 last traded. EUR/JPY falls in tandem, touches intraday low at 113.76, last at 113.84. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: BOJ expected to expand liquidity-provision measures, including potential increase in supply of 6-month loans or more purchases of short-term government bills, at two-day policy board meeting starting Monday, according to 9 out of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones. Decision due Tuesday afternoon. Poll tips BOJ to keep policy target rate at 0.1%, unchanged since December 2008. Pressure to ease further is increasing, as BOJ's own tankan business sentiment survey indicated deepening concerns about corporate outlook and JPY's worrisome uptrend shows no signs of abating. See: =BOJ WATCH: More Monetary Easing Expected As Economic Outlook Darkens (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 10-01-10 0314ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020101001e6a100047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
