\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Up 0.3%; Volume Low Before Key U.S. Data\b0\par\par\uc2 1,616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 06:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei +0.3% at 9393.50 after briefly falling into negative territory, as lingering concerns about firmer yen outweigh positive China official September PMI figures (53.8 vs 51.7 in August); trading volume relatively low on caution ahead of U.S. ISM manufacturing index data for September due later in global trading day. Toshihiko Matsuno, senior strategist at SMBC Friend Securities, puts Nikkei range for next week at 9100-9700. "We can expect a surprise impact (on stocks) if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government carries out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with BOJ monetary easing (anticipated at next week's policy board meeting)," he says. But adds stocks unlikely to respond if BOJ easing in line with previous media reports. Cites U.S. September jobs data (due Friday of next week) as possible downward risk. 14/33 Topix subindexes higher; but Topix still 0.1% lower. Fanuc (6954.TO) up 1.1% at Y10,750, \uc2 Hitachi Construction Machinery\uc2  (6305.TO) up 1.1% at Y1,824 after China data. \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) off 2.1% at Y2,526. \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) off 1.1% at Y2,966 on Nikkei report of possible 40% fall in September domestic dealership orders. Real estate largest sectoral gainer, up 1.6%. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds rise despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision yesterday to raise policy rates 12.5 bps. "Concerns about a rate increase in December are relatively low, and any increase will be moderate, so yields have continued to drop," says local trader. Adds, inflows from foreign investors driving yields down. 10-year yield last at 1.1870% vs 1.1947% yesterday, eyed in 1.1500%-1.2100% next week; 5-year yield last at 0.8680% vs 0.8785% yesterday, eyed in 0.8400%-0.8800% yield next week.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0428 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  likely to hold policy rate steady at 1.75% at October's meeting as THB's rapid raise is more concerning issue than inflation, says \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  Manager of Macroeconomic analysis Pornthep Jubandhu. "The (stronger) baht is the pressure now. The Monetary Policy Committee is likely to pause policy rate hikes in the upcoming meeting if it (the baht) continues to become stronger," he says; add whether \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise rate at December's meeting unpredictable now as depends on economic momentum at that time. CPI in September rises 3.0% on year, core CPI +1.1% on year -- down from +3.3% for CPI, +1.2% for core CPI in August. Policy rate meeting slated for Oct. 20. \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  maintains 2010 policy rate forecast in range of 1.75%-2.0% by year-end. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0426 GMT [Dow Jones] After Korea's consumer inflation shoots to 17-month high in September, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  economist Prakriti Sofat says, "with the economy operating close to capacity, policymakers' concerns about demand-pull pressures likely were exacerbated, given that core inflation rose sequentially for the sixth consecutive month." Core inflation 1.9% on year, 0.3% on month. Sofat tips BOK to hike rates 25 bps this month, but adds resurgence in global financial market turbulence or further signs of softness in information technology cycle could see BOK hold off again this time. "The BOK continues to face a difficult balancing act -- weighing the risks of more external uncertainties against a strong domestic economy that has started to cool. We believe there is room for one more rate hike by the end of this year." September inflation at 17-month high of 3.6% on year, 90-month high of 1.1% on month. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds down as weekly food inflation continues to rise raising concerns of another round of rate hikes by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at yield of 7.88% (99.49 in price terms) vs 7.85% (99.68 in price terms) at Wednesday's close. Markets were shut Thursday for banks' half-yearly book closing. "The cut-offs in RBI's INR110 billion auction (later today) will decide the course for the later part of the session. For now, it is inflation concerns," says the dealer. Food articles index +0.8% at 179.9 for week ended Sept. 18, a fifth straight week of rise. Dealer tips 2020 bond yields in 7.85%-7.90% range in session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR steady at 8,920 despite capital inflows, USD weakness vs regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as Bank Indonesia suspected of buying earlier, say dealers. "We are a bit reluctant to push the dollar (down) because Bank Indonesia has been supporting the pair," dealer says. Still, thinks pair may test 8,915 support if capital inflows continue, European players sell later today. Adds, Lower-than-expected September inflation supporting local stocks, bonds, thus should be positive for IDR. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea's faster-than-expected inflation rate in September likely to prompt BOK to hike rates by 25 bps at October 14 meeting, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ; "The sharp rise is likely to reinforce concerns among many policy makers that the policy rate, currently at 2.25%, is too low...today's inflation data has sharply raised the odds of a 25-bp rate hike and the continuation of a hawkish stance," says Goldman economist Goohoon Kwon. Adds, "on this trend and without any policy actions, inflation could easily exceed 4% year on year in coming months." September inflation at 17-month high of 3.6% on year, 90-month high of 1.1% on month. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] China's stronger PMI reading - up at 53.8 in September vs 51.7 in August - good news for Asia, as shows China's anticipated economic slowdown in 3Q, 4Q won't be as bad as previously expected, says Tim Condon, ING Bank strategist; China Federation of Logistics and Purchasing issued PMI data ealier today. Condon adds "the softening of Chinese activity in the second quarter is over," property tightening measures were probably to blame for declining trend. Reckons China's PMI, coupled with Korea's solid September exports data, hints at continuation of Asia's positive growth story. Also notes Korea's inflation spike indicates food prices remain biggest risk to price stability, but one that shouldn't fundamentally change benign inflation picture in region. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.4% at 452.73 as investors discouraged by government meeting's outcome (ended late yesterday); dealers say government failed to come up with measures to solve tight liquidity conditions and support economy in 4Q. "Investors are disappointed because they think the authorities are basically ignoring local companies that are having difficulties raising capital due to high (borrowing) costs," says fund manager analyst. Notes, borrowing rates range between 15%, 18%. Volume thin with 28 million shares valued at VND740 billion traded so far. Among decliners, \uc2 Bao Viet Holdings\uc2  (BVH.VH) down 1.8% at VND54,500, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) down 2.5% at VND20,000, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) down 0.6% at VND36,000. Tips immediate support at 450. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR falls to near 5-month low as local stocks open higher (Sensex last +0.7%), major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stay strong against USD, says dealer with state-run bank; pair last at 44.75 vs 44.94 in late Wednesday trade. Markets were shut Thursday for bank's half-yearly book closing. "Dollar selling is likely to continue on the back of higher local stock markets," says dealer. Tips pair may find support first at 44.72 followed by 44.57, which if breached, could push pair to 44.48. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian government bond prices higher on lower-than-expected September inflation data, but room for further rallies seems limited, dealers say. "We actually predict that yields will start to creep higher in October on profit-taking as well as in anticipation of rising inflationary pressures next year," a dealer with a local bank says. 5-year yield down at 7.20% vs 7.23% yesterday; 10-year down at 7.63% vs 7.75%; and 15-year down at 8.12% vs 8.17%; official statistics agency says inflation 5.80% on-year in September vs 5.92% market forecast, 6.44% in August. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals making gains in thin trading with copper hitting highest level since August 2008, now at $8,080/ton, up $70 with 363 lots done. "The Chinese have been the dominant sellers this week but with them gone (on holiday) consumers' nibbling has sent us up," says Seoul-based trader. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,355/ton, up $4 with 371 lots done though long way of April high of $2,494. Harbor Intelligence says smelter cuts in China shifting market into monthly deficit. Adds, current drivers are accelerating demand in China, stronger seasonality, weakening USD, confidence in bullish territory, falling aluminum inventories; expects its year-end price target of $2,400 to be reached. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 10-01-10 0037ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM000