\par\par \b \uc2 Tokyo Shares End Up On China Data, Higher Crude Prices, Easing Hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brad Frischkorn   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2010\par 08:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks rose Friday, as solid China economic data and hopes for monetary easing by the Bank of Japan lifted the broader indexes, while higher crude prices helped oil-related shares like Inpex.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average rose 34.88 points, or 0.4%, to 9404.23. The Topix index of all the Tokyo Stock Exchange First Section issues rose 0.46 point, or 0.1%, to 829.97.\par \par \uc2 Trading volume was initially somewhat light, but ended at a robust 2.09 billion shares, the second time in as many days it passed the 2 billion mark.\par \par \uc2 The market began in positive territory, but was lackluster amid lingering concerns about the strong yen and caution ahead of U.S. manufacturing-index data for September later in the global day.\par \par \uc2 The dollar traded mostly around the mid-Y83.50 level.\par \par \uc2 Purchasing Managers Index (PMI) data from China's manufacturing sector showed a 2.1 percentage-point rise from August, acting as mild salve for the market in the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 "We can expect a surprise impact (on stocks) if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government carries out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with BOJ monetary easing (expected from next week's policy board meeting)," said Toshihiko Matsuno, senior strategist at SMBC Friend Securities. But he said stocks aren't likely to respond if BOJ steps are merely in line with media reports.\par \par \uc2 Matsuno put the Nikkei's range for next week at 9100-9700, citing U.S. September jobs data due next Friday as a downside risk.\par \par \uc2 Oil-related shares fared well from the start on the back of an overnight jump in crude prices. Inpex added 2.5% to Y403,000, while \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  closed up 1.7% at Y492. JX, along with \uc2 Showa Shell Sekiyu\uc2 , which rose 0.2% to Y639, also benefited from a local media report that Exxon Mobile plans to gradually pull out of gas station \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Japan due to weak demand for petroleum products.\par \par \uc2 Real estate stocks closed up strongly after government data showed that Japan August housing starts jumped 20.5% on year to 71,972 units, handily beating pessimistic expectations and rising for a third straight month. \uc2 Mitsui Fudosan\uc2  ended up 3.7% at Y1,460, and Mitsubishi Estate added 3.2% to 1,402.\par \par \uc2 Major Japanese banks suffered from rekindled European sovereign-debt concerns after Moody's Investors Service downgraded Spain's credit rating and Ireland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  outlined the costs of rescuing its troubled banking sector.\par \par \uc2 Speculation that global regulators may further beef up capital requirements included in the recent Basel III agreement weighed on the sector, helping push \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group\uc2  and \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\uc2  to fresh 2010 lows.\par \par \uc2 Mizuho ended down 2.5% at Y119, while SMFG rebounded on bargain-hunting to end up 0.7% at Y2,449.\par \par \uc2 December Nikkei 225 futures added 0.3% or 30 points, to 9390.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Nikkei lost 0.7% and remains down 11% for the year.\par \par \uc2 -By Brad Frischkorn, Dow Jones Newswires, 813-6269-2779, bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com [ 01-10-10 0657GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4159 5011 55117   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020101001e6a10000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
