\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro heading for best quarter vs dollar in 8 years\b0\par\par\uc2 1,125 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 02:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Euro retreats from 5-month high vs dollar\par \par \uc2 * Euro on track for biggest quarterly gain in 8 years\par \par \uc2 * Dollar index hits 8-month low; month-end flows dominate\par \par \uc2 * Better-than-expected U.S. data caps USD losses (Adds details, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Vivianne Rodrigues\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The euro hit a five-month high against the dollar and was on track for its best quarterly gains in eight years on Thursday as data showed euro zone banks are relying less on funds from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The news reinforced expectations Europe may gradually remove policy stimulus before the United States and Britain, helping offset concerns about Ireland's fiscal and banking problems.\par \par \uc2 The yen rose broadly on reported exporter demand for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the last day of Japan's fiscal half-year. This sparked talk Japan may step in again to curb yen gains.\par \par \uc2 The euro's rally, however, seemed to be running out of steam in the short-term, analysts said. It reached the 55-week moving average around $1.3613 but failed to take out the previous high of $1.3692 hit last April 12.\par \par \uc2 "The euro rally has gone a bit too far and it was time to take profits. So the dollar as a result is benefiting," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said in a note that a close this week in euro/dollar above the 55-week moving average would be an important break and suggest a medium term rally towards $1.40, $1.47 and possibly $1.51 again.\par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trading in New York, the euro was little changed on the day at $1.3632 <EUR=EBS>, after trading as high as $1.3684 earlier.\par \par \uc2 "The lower level of emergency borrowing, combined with the expiration of previous ECB loans reduces the amount of liquidity in Europe's banking system, puts upward pressure on money market rates and signals a gradual normalization in conditions," said Omer Esiner, chief market analyst at \uc2 Commonwealth \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Inc\uc2  in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The dollar recouped some of its losses after a stronger-than-expected reading of business activity in the U.S. Midwest. (For Chicago PMI, click on [ID:nN30285357]; for jobless claims, click on [ID:nN30285244])\par \par \uc2 Data showing a rise in U.S. Midwest business activity in September "was a solid number and certainly bodes well for the manufacturing sector. The positive data we've seen earlier in the day also contributes to the stabilization of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Joe Manimbo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Travelex Global Business Payments in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "But despite the good numbers, the reports are not solid enough to prevent the Fed from adding more stimulus to the economy."\par \par \uc2 OPTIONS BARRIERS\par \par \uc2 In a day of heavy month- and quarter-end flows, the euro seesawed after Ireland disclosed a worst-case price tag of more than 50 billion euros to bail out its troubled banks. [ID:nLDE68T045]\par \par \uc2 The euro pushed through reported options barriers at $1.3650 and $1.3675 to hit a five-month high of $1.3684 on the EBS trading platform. Traders said there is another barrier at $1.3700 and every 25-point interval all the way to $1.38.\par \par \uc2 The Irish news was outweighed by data on Thursday showing banks borrowed 29.4 billion euros of six-day ECB funds, ensuring a far bigger-than-expected drop in excess liquidity as 225 billion euros of ECB loans expire. That signaled a healthier outlook for money markets. [ID:nLDE68T13O].\par \par \uc2 The euro has risen almost 12 percent versus the dollar over the course of this quarter, its best quarterly performance since June 2002. On the month, the euro was up about 7.5 percent, its best monthly gains since December 2008.\par \par \uc2 Euro gains helped push the dollar index to an eight-month low against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar index <.DXY> fell earlier to an eight-month low of 78.414 before rebounding to 78.747. It has fallen about 8 percent over the quarter, leaving it on track for its biggest quarterly fall since the second quarter of 2002.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar fell 0.3 percent to 83.44 yen <JPY=EBS>, not far from a 15-year low of 82.87 hit on trading platform EBS earlier this month before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Japanese authorities sold 2.1249 trillion yen ($25.37 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the latest month to Sept. 28. [ID:nTOE68T08I]\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss and Wanfeng Zhou; Editing by Kenneth Barry) [20100930 004712 GMT]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 3:39 p.m. EDT (1939 GMT). All data taken from Reuters calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m.(2030 GMT) in the previous New York session.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Last  US Close    Pct   YTD Pct   2009\par                            Sept. 29  Change  Change    Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr   <EUR=>      1.3630   1.3628   +0.01   -4.79   1.4316\par Dlr/yen    <JPY=>      83.500   83.680   -0.22  -10.12   92.900\par Euro/yen   <EURJPY=