\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:EUR/JPY Falls On \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  News;113 Support\b0\par\par\uc2 1,565 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 08:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY falls, last 113.01, as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Ireland says State-owned Anglo Irish Bank Corp., Ireland's most troubled financial institution, will need total capital of EUR29.3 billion or additional EUR5 billion in "stress scenario," says senior dealer at major bank in Tokyo. Adds, USD/JPY also falls, last 83.29, investors executed small amount of stop-loss selling orders at 83.40. For outlook, focus on "whether Japan steps into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again," but for now that's unlikely, he says. Tips USD/JPY support at 83.00, EUR/JPY at 113.00 vs session high of 114.23. EUR/USD falling in tandem; last at 1.3571 vs session high of 1.3638. Separately, another trader at Tokyo bank says EUR/JPY stop-loss sell orders triggered around 113.50; also tips support around 113.00 amid caution government may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell JPY. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com, tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Even as Aussie bond curve rallies today on weak Australian building approvals and credit growth data, Aussie bond prices are going to decline further longer-term, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Strategist Roland Randall. "We may well see the short-end rally and take some of that yield expectation off the table, but I still think yields on the short-end will be higher over the next year," says Randall. Short-end bond prices down significantly in past month as market prices in increasing likelihood RBA will hike at its next meeting, with today's move doing little to erase broader move in bonds. Aussie 3-year up 8 ticks at 95.18 in recent trade, also hurt by comments from \uc2 HSBC\uc2  Chief Economist and former RBA economist Paul Bloxham that he expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hold on rates until November. 10-year up 7.5 ticks at 95.005. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's all-household spending may rise 1.4% on year in August after July's +1.1%, according to Dow Jones, Nikkei poll of economists. Analysts say there was rush demand for cars in month as government's powerful incentive program to buy fuel-efficient cars ended in early September. Japan's unusually hot weather should also have contributed to good sales of air conditioners, other summer-related goods, they say. Data due 2330 GMT.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.5% at 974.13 at midday pause, driven by persistent capital inflows; profit-taking, weakness in regional bourses cap upside, however. "The SET index is likely to test 1,000 points next month if fund flows continue," says Phillip Securities assistant VP Teerada Chanyingyong. Resistance tipped at 977-984 zone, support at 963-956 area. Among top actives, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.4% at THB720, PTT Aromatics & Refining (PTTAR.TH) +0.9% at THB27.75, \uc2 PTT Exploration & Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) +0.7% at THB153, \uc2 Thanachart Capital\uc2  (TCAP.TH) +3.1% at THB41.25, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +0.5% at THB104.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0543 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Japan's jobless rate expected to improve to 5.1% in August from 5.2% in July, according to Dow Jones, Nikkei poll of economists. Dai-ichi Life Research Institute economist Yonosuke Iwata says moderate recovery to continue as job postings, though most of them for part-time positions, on the rise recently. Adds full-time opportunities may not increase at fast pace as recent BOJ tankan showed firms in general still have excess in labor. Data due 2330 GMT.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains marginal losses in afternoon trade at 1,141.0 vs 1,142.0 late yesterday on persistent exporter USD selling. But pair little moved from morning levels amid lack of momentum to push pair further down, while euro weakens in Asian trade, failing to give local unit extra boost, traders say. "There's quite a lot of dollar selling from exporters, but given the likelihood of authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to buy dollars) at levels below KRW1,140, the pair will likely hover around current levels for rest of the session," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,139-1,143 range. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0535 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares by midday +0.3% at 3055.011 in light volume, off earlier low of 3489.812, led by buying in select resources, automotive stocks on hopes for strong 3Q earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 3520. "I think the main index is set to close at another fresh record high in a generally cautious trend," says fund manager at state-owned pension fund. Among gainers: tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) +5.3% at IDR3,000, gold, nickel miner Antam (ANTM.JK) +3.3% at IDR2,350. Profit-taking hits toll road builder, operator \uc2 Jasa Marga\uc2  (JSMR.JK), down 3.0% at IDR3,200. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0510 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi turns lower, down 0.2% at 1862.91 as investors taking breather before 3Q earnings season; "foreigners are buying KRW220 billion worth of domestic stocks and it's keeping the main index from falling further," says Lee Kyoung-min at \uc2 Woori Investment & Securities\uc2 . Says if major companies come up with strong 3Q results next month, Kospi may get hefty boost to test 1900 level; 1855-1870 band tipped for today. Stocks helping to limit Kospi's fall include technology, shipbuilding, transport shares. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.7% at KRW777,000, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) +1.1% at KRW22,100, Hyundai Mipo Dockyard (010620.SE) +1.9% at KRW188,500, \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) +1.8% at KRW77,400. Some shares falling after recent gains: \uc2 Hyundai Engineering & Construction\uc2  (000720.SE) down 2.5% at KRW71,800, SK Energy (096770.SE) off 2.3% at KRW148,500, \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) down 1.7% at KRW327,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] China likely to continue using targeted measures such as Wednesday's property curbs to rein in excesses in certain parts of the economy, rather than turning to broad-based tightening such as interest rate hikes; Wednesday's measures only the latest example of this strong policy preference. Consumer prices rose 3.5% in August, but mostly on one-off factors such as higher food prices, and economists almost universally expect inflation to ease in coming months. Inflation rates were also much higher during last round of interest rate hikes, over 6% in 2H 2007 and as high as 8.7% in February of 2008. See: =MONEY TALKS: China To Focus On Property, Not Broad Tightening. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0501 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 1.3% at 9440, extending morning losses on concerns about firmer yen (USD/JPY at 83.52 and EUR/JPY at 113.59). "In addition to the stronger yen, the decline in banking shares to fresh year-to-date lows is souring overall sentiment," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. MUFG (8306.TO) down 4.0% at Y388 and SMFG (8316.TO) off 3.9% at Y2,432; both hit fresh 2010 lows on concerns over consumer lender affiliates and renewed European debt worries. Puts successive futures index near-term support levels at 9400, then 9200. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1,464.08 midday in range-bound morning session as investors grope for leads from global markets amid dearth of local cues; index caught in tight 6 point range through morning session; Dealer says markets likely to continue to consolidate through rest of session; tips 1,460-1,467 range for rest of session. Immediate resistance at recent high of 1,479.59, with support at Friday low of 1,445.33. Market breadth negative with 348 decliners vs 253 advancers. Advancers led by property, construction stocks with \uc2 Kimlun Corp\uc2 . (5171.KU) +1.9% at MYR1.09 on MYR70 million contract win from \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU), which +1.4% at MYR2.19; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +2.0% at MYR3.00. Decliners led by Unisem (5005.KU) down 2.0% at MYR1.98, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) down 1.1% at MYR5.49. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY briefly falls below 83.50, lowest level since Sept. 15, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, amid persistent speculation Fed likely to ease monetary policy further soon, says Yuzo Sakai, senior dealer at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow; adds, selling by Japan's exporters also pushing pair lower. But notes "if the dollar extends falls against the yen, expectations could grow that the government will step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to sell the yen, which could discourage investors from actively buying" JPY. Tips support at 83.20 for now; last 83.51 vs session high of 83.81. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-30-10 0204ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100930e69u0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
