 at KRW148,500, \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) down 1.7% at KRW327,000. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0502 GMT [Dow Jones] China likely to continue using targeted measures such as Wednesday's property curbs to rein in excesses in certain parts of the economy, rather than turning to broad-based tightening such as interest rate hikes; Wednesday's measures only the latest example of this strong policy preference. Consumer prices rose 3.5% in August, but mostly on one-off factors such as higher food prices, and economists almost universally expect inflation to ease in coming months. Inflation rates were also much higher during last round of interest rate hikes, over 6% in 2H 2007 and as high as 8.7% in February of 2008. See: =MONEY TALKS: China To Focus On Property, Not Broad Tightening. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0501 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures down 1.3% at 9440, extending morning losses on concerns about firmer yen (USD/JPY at 83.52 and EUR/JPY at 113.59). "In addition to the stronger yen, the decline in banking shares to fresh year-to-date lows is souring overall sentiment," says Hideki Horikawa, chief analyst at Himawari Securities. MUFG (8306.TO) down 4.0% at Y388 and SMFG (8316.TO) off 3.9% at Y2,432; both hit fresh 2010 lows on concerns over consumer lender affiliates and renewed European debt worries. Puts successive futures index near-term support levels at 9400, then 9200. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0447 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1,464.08 midday in range-bound morning session as investors grope for leads from global markets amid dearth of local cues; index caught in tight 6 point range through morning session; Dealer says markets likely to continue to consolidate through rest of session; tips 1,460-1,467 range for rest of session. Immediate resistance at recent high of 1,479.59, with support at Friday low of 1,445.33. Market breadth negative with 348 decliners vs 253 advancers. Advancers led by property, construction stocks with \uc2 Kimlun Corp\uc2 . (5171.KU) +1.9% at MYR1.09 on MYR70 million contract win from \uc2 Malaysian Resources\uc2  (1651.KU), which +1.4% at MYR2.19; \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2 . (2194.KU) +2.0% at MYR3.00. Decliners led by Unisem (5005.KU) down 2.0% at MYR1.98, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) down 1.1% at MYR5.49. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY briefly falls below 83.50, lowest level since Sept. 15, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, amid persistent speculation Fed likely to ease monetary policy further soon, says Yuzo Sakai, senior dealer at Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  & Ueda Harlow; adds, selling by Japan's exporters also pushing pair lower. But notes "if the dollar extends falls against the yen, expectations could grow that the government will step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to sell the yen, which could discourage investors from actively buying" JPY. Tips support at 83.20 for now; last 83.51 vs session high of 83.81. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Action in Singapore stock market notably subdued as investors generally staying on sidelines after digesting solid gains from past weeks. STI +0.3% at 3095.66 midday, but +5.3% since start of September, with support at last week's 3069 low. "The index won't come off too much because everyone knows this year is a good year for the Singapore economy," says dealer at foreign brokerage; "we have had a good month in September. It's time to take a break, have a Kit Kat, as it's the end of the month." Overall market volume light at 894.4 million shares worth S$1.24 billion, with good mix of large, small, mid caps among most active, including Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 1.1% at S$1.88, Innovalues (591.SG), flat at S$0.17, \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  (S21.SG), off 2.3% at US$0.43, \uc2 Transcu Group\uc2  (E15.SG), flat at S$0.125, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), off 1.0% at S$3.14. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 1.1% at 9450.77 after hitting new intraday low of 9436.59 in afternoon session. "It's futures-led selling; it's not like new selling factors are hitting the cash market," says Japanese brokerage manager, adding Europeans appear to be selling on slightly weaker USD/JPY (now 83.53 vs 83.80 earlier); December Nikkei futures off 1.2% at 9450, in line with cash market. Adds, Nikkei may be supported at 9400. All 33 Topix subindexes in negative territory, with heavily-weighted banks down 3.1% as a group; MUFG (8306.TO) down 2.5% at Y390 on concerns about rising credit default swap levels for consumer lender affiliate Acom (8572.TO). Nintendo (7974.OK) continues fall, now off 9.5% at Y20,820 after slashing FY forecast. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) stabilizing somewhat, off 2.4% at Y2,055 (vs midday close of Y2,031) after announcing details of massive equity capital increase plan. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] HK market's resilience, with HSI ending midday only down 0.3% at 22,316.98, despite ICBC (1398.HK) stake cut by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , China's further property tightening, should be taken as sign of market's underlying strength. Market volume strong at HK$46.97 billion at interval, doesn't appear index would slide more; week's lowest of 22,109 likely won't troubled. ICBC down 3.2% at HK$5.78, China Overseas Land (0688.HK) tumbles 3.7% to HK$16.20, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) off 4.5% at HK$15.76, but weakness in part offset by rally in Hutchison (0013.HK), big HK developers Cheung Kong (0001.HK), \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK). Overall, given HSI's about 9% gain in September, "we believe the market will enter a period of consolidation over the next few trading sessions," says \uc2 Celestial Securities\uc2 . (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong August retail sales growth likely remained in double digits, but likely slowed vs July on fading base factor. By value, tipped +16.0% on-year in August vs July's +18.9%; by volume +13.2% in August vs July's +16.0%, according to Dow Jones poll of 5 economists. "Domestic demand remains buoyant with the fall in the jobless rate and tourist arrivals continue to be resilient, but the higher base of comparison last year also weighs on the headline figure," Core Pacific-Yamaichi analyst Keith Lo says. \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  economist Paul Tang says retail sales growth likely fell to "high single digits" in 4Q due to higher base of comparison, as city's retail sales returned to growth since September last year. Data due 0830 GMT Monday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] While central banks of South Korea, Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets overnight, following confirmed moves by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month, don't expect \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  to do any such thing, say traders and strategists. With surging mining sector, robust banking system, inflation a very real concern, RBA is looking to cool the economy any way it can, with gains in AUD helping \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "If the Aussie dollar wasn't as high as it is, we could see more than one rate hike by the end of the year as it's doing some heavy lifting for the RBA. A high Aussie dollar curbs importers and inflation, creating a dampening affect that is exactly what the RBA wants," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Economist Helen Kevans. AUD/USD last at 0.9670 after hitting 26-month high around 0.9730 overnight. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.3% at 454.52, with buying led by foreigners on 3Q earnings hopes, also ahead of key government meeting later this week, though concerns on high interest rates remain. "Institutions continue to buy with faith that the government will have to work out new measures to support the economy in 4Q, amid rising worries that high interest rates will hurt company profitability and also discourage borrowing," says Hanoi-based fund management analyst. Volume average at 40.6 million shares valued at VND1.21 trillion. Among most active, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 1.9% at VND31,600, \uc2 Thuan Thao Corp\uc2 . (GTT.VH) ends +4.5% at VND11,500, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends +1.2% at VND16,700. Tips stronger sentiment tomorrow; key index to move in 455-460 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 09-30-10 0135ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100930e69u0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
