ight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to sell the yen, which could discourage investors from actively buying" JPY. Tips support at 83.20 for now; last 83.51 vs session high of 83.81. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Action in Singapore stock market notably subdued as investors generally staying on sidelines after digesting solid gains from past weeks. STI +0.3% at 3095.66 midday, but +5.3% since start of September, with support at last week's 3069 low. "The index won't come off too much because everyone knows this year is a good year for the Singapore economy," says dealer at foreign brokerage; "we have had a good month in September. It's time to take a break, have a Kit Kat, as it's the end of the month." Overall market volume light at 894.4 million shares worth S$1.24 billion, with good mix of large, small, mid caps among most active, including Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 1.1% at S$1.88, Innovalues (591.SG), flat at S$0.17, \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  (S21.SG), off 2.3% at US$0.43, \uc2 Transcu Group\uc2  (E15.SG), flat at S$0.125, \uc2 SingTel\uc2  (Z74.SG), off 1.0% at S$3.14. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 1.1% at 9450.77 after hitting new intraday low of 9436.59 in afternoon session. "It's futures-led selling; it's not like new selling factors are hitting the cash market," says Japanese brokerage manager, adding Europeans appear to be selling on slightly weaker USD/JPY (now 83.53 vs 83.80 earlier); December Nikkei futures off 1.2% at 9450, in line with cash market. Adds, Nikkei may be supported at 9400. All 33 Topix subindexes in negative territory, with heavily-weighted banks down 3.1% as a group; MUFG (8306.TO) down 2.5% at Y390 on concerns about rising credit default swap levels for consumer lender affiliate Acom (8572.TO). Nintendo (7974.OK) continues fall, now off 9.5% at Y20,820 after slashing FY forecast. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) stabilizing somewhat, off 2.4% at Y2,055 (vs midday close of Y2,031) after announcing details of massive equity capital increase plan. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [Dow Jones] HK market's resilience, with HSI ending midday only down 0.3% at 22,316.98, despite ICBC (1398.HK) stake cut by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , China's further property tightening, should be taken as sign of market's underlying strength. Market volume strong at HK$46.97 billion at interval, doesn't appear index would slide more; week's lowest of 22,109 likely won't troubled. ICBC down 3.2% at HK$5.78, China Overseas Land (0688.HK) tumbles 3.7% to HK$16.20, \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) off 4.5% at HK$15.76, but weakness in part offset by rally in Hutchison (0013.HK), big HK developers Cheung Kong (0001.HK), \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK). Overall, given HSI's about 9% gain in September, "we believe the market will enter a period of consolidation over the next few trading sessions," says \uc2 Celestial Securities\uc2 . (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0429 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong August retail sales growth likely remained in double digits, but likely slowed vs July on fading base factor. By value, tipped +16.0% on-year in August vs July's +18.9%; by volume +13.2% in August vs July's +16.0%, according to Dow Jones poll of 5 economists. "Domestic demand remains buoyant with the fall in the jobless rate and tourist arrivals continue to be resilient, but the higher base of comparison last year also weighs on the headline figure," Core Pacific-Yamaichi analyst Keith Lo says. \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  economist Paul Tang says retail sales growth likely fell to "high single digits" in 4Q due to higher base of comparison, as city's retail sales returned to growth since September last year. Data due 0830 GMT Monday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0424 GMT [Dow Jones] While central banks of South Korea, Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets overnight, following confirmed moves by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month, don't expect \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  to do any such thing, say traders and strategists. With surging mining sector, robust banking system, inflation a very real concern, RBA is looking to cool the economy any way it can, with gains in AUD helping \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "If the Aussie dollar wasn't as high as it is, we could see more than one rate hike by the end of the year as it's doing some heavy lifting for the RBA. A high Aussie dollar curbs importers and inflation, creating a dampening affect that is exactly what the RBA wants," says \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Economist Helen Kevans. AUD/USD last at 0.9670 after hitting 26-month high around 0.9730 overnight. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +0.3% at 454.52, with buying led by foreigners on 3Q earnings hopes, also ahead of key government meeting later this week, though concerns on high interest rates remain. "Institutions continue to buy with faith that the government will have to work out new measures to support the economy in 4Q, amid rising worries that high interest rates will hurt company profitability and also discourage borrowing," says Hanoi-based fund management analyst. Volume average at 40.6 million shares valued at VND1.21 trillion. Among most active, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends down 1.9% at VND31,600, \uc2 Thuan Thao Corp\uc2 . (GTT.VH) ends +4.5% at VND11,500, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends +1.2% at VND16,700. Tips stronger sentiment tomorrow; key index to move in 455-460 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Nomura Securities says BOJ September quarterly tankan result, in which large manufacturers' diffusion index (DI) difference (between current survey and previous survey forecast) declined from +9 in June to +5, is consistent with revision index of analysts' earnings forecasts for manufacturers in Nomura 400 index, which remained positive but contracted between June and September. "Against this backdrop, we think upward revisions to analyst forecasts or company guidance are unlikely to provide support for share prices," writes analyst Ryota Sakagami in report. However, says forecast DIs are below current business conditions DIs for almost all sectors, setting the stage for possible positive surprises ahead. Adds, sectors likely to post positive earnings surprises relative to guidance include electrical machinery, motor vehicles, chemicals, ceramics makers, and pulp and paper makers. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Nikkei/Dow Jones] Office and medical equipment leasing firm Ricoh Leasing (8566.TO) up 2.8% at Y2,088, off high of Y2,120, as company revises up net profit outlook. Shares now up more than 4.0% from 4-week low of Y1,997 on Tuesday when they went ex-dividend for 1H ending September 30. Firm now expects 22% rise in this FY net profit outlook to Y8.1 billion vs previously estimated Y6.9 billion as fall in corporate bankruptcies lead to lower loan provisions, interest rate falls reduce fund-raising costs. Shares now up 13% year-to-date. (kazuhiro.shimamura@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD weaker after soft building consents data, business confidence survey but still rangebound, held up by weaker USD, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Bank Senior Market Strategist Imre Speizer. Pair last 0.7359 vs 0.7385 early in NZ; "The flavor of the day has been more poor data. Confidence is dribbling off, building activity is weaker than most economists expected." But says as long as USD weakness remains dominant theme, "the Kiwi probably won't fall as much as it probably should." Tips Kiwi to remain 0.7320-0.7340 range. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0412 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex off 0.1% at 19,948.16 as traders tread cautiously before September derivatives expiry later today. "Trading is subdued. There is some short-covering in select bluechips, mainly financials after yesterday's decline," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Adds, volume tepid, Sensex expected in 19,900-20,160 range. Says, trading activity could pick up towards the close. HDFC (500010.BY), +1.8% at INR719, top gainer, while \uc2 Reliance Communications (532712.BY) \uc2 off 0.8% at INR168.40. \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  (500696.BY) +0.8% at INR308.20, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +0.3% at INR1,104.50. Mahindra (500520.BY) down 0.8% at INR693.15, \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) down 0.6% at INR993.50. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com) [ 09-30-10 0047ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100930e69u00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
