\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Unchanged In Intricate Market\b0\par\par\uc2 1,416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 05:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0340 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options unchanged in Tokyo at 10.65%/11.35% from New York Wednesday. Options dealer at major Japan bank says some players buy short-term hedges as they expect rapid moves in spot after Japan's half-year ends Thursday; interest in both upside, downside hedges. Meanwhile, others exit contracts as spot moving little; "It's an intricate market with various expectations." Market now focused on U.S. 2Q GDP 3rd estimate due 1230 GMT. Says if data worse than expected, U.S. Treasury yields decline, USD may weaken, which will likely drive vols up. Says player buys 2-week USD-put/JPY-call contracts at strike price of 81.00 with implied vols of 12.30% for unknown amount. USD/JPY last at 83.57. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] China Commerce Ministry says anti-subsidy investigations on exchange rate grounds breach WTO rules, reacting to legislation passed by U.S. House, Xinhua reports. Also reiterates China has never undervalued yuan to gain competitive edge, says U.S.-China trade deficit shouldn't be excuse for protectionism. Mention of WTO vague but suggests possible threat of countermeasures; other statements reiterate Chinese stance, follow consistent defense of yuan policies against U.S. pressure, after PBOC yesterday also repeats pledge to increase flexibility of exchange rate. (owen.fletcher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0324 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds drift up in active trade as both offshore, local players continue buying. "Capital inflows are the key driver for the bond market now and it's highly likely yields will fall further in the near future," says bond dealer. Notes, MOF yesterday said THB1.27 trillion, of which 75% invested in bonds, flowed to Thailand over past 3 months. Yields expected to trade rangebound rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.52%/2.41% vs 2.55%/2.50% late yesterday, November 2016 2.74%/2.65% vs 2.75%/2.71, June 2019 2.86%/2.84% vs 2.89%/2.87%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares last up 0.1% at 3497.682 in light volume, off earlier low at 3489.812, helped by firmer IDR, hopes for strong 3Q corporate earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 3520. "The main index has been moving in a very limited range to reflect caution that profit-taking may occur all of a sudden," says trader at Erdhika Securities; index up 38% since start of 2010. Among gainers, tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) up 3.5% at IDR2,950, gold, nickel miner Antam (ANTM.JK) up 2.2% at IDR2,325. But profit taking hits \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), down 0.7% at IDR6,950. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0317 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open tad lower, tracking losses in most regional markets, while traders tread cautiously before monthly derivatives expiry, dealers say. "The index should open about 20-30 points down on subdued global markets. Funds are unlikely to deploy fresh money at these levels - Sensex is up about 25% from May 25 low," says local fund manager. Adds, expensive valuations after recent run-up could further but Sensex could find support at 19,800. Says, strong liquidity on sustained foreign inflows expected to lend support. Foreign funds were net buyers of local shares worth provisional INR7.56 billion yesterday vs INR7.51 billion Tuesday. Tips Sensex in 19,800-20,100 range vs 19,956.34 (down 0.7%) last close. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 1.1% at 3188.80 as market following Australia's lead, weighed further by weakness in heavyweight Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ), down 3.7% at NZ$8.04, unlikely to reverse losses prior to close, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  broker Humphrey Sherratt; "Fletcher Building is weak on both data out here in New Zealand and Australia today." Stats NZ says residential building permits issued in August fell seasonally adjusted 17.8% from July. \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  down 3.5% at NZ$0.56 on weak market sentiment, Sherratt adds. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP up slightly as it moves in tight 43.90-43.97 band, with banks wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if pair slides below 43.88 - level apparently being supported by authorities. Pair last at 43.93 in light volume after settling at 43.88 late yesterday, with support pegged at 43.80, resistance at 44.00. Traders say market fairly quiet, with banks taking USD/PHP rise as opportunity to sell pair. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY falls to intraday low at 113.70 due to Japan exporters' JPY purchases after daily fixing around 0100 GMT as they face end of financial half year, says senior dealer at major Japan investment fund. Tips immediate technical support around current levels, but says if cross extends falls on continued pressure from exporters, it may drop to key 113.00 level. EUR/JPY last 113.70. EUR/USD falls in tandem to intraday low at 1.3596; dealer tips support for pair at 1.3570 vs 1.3603 last.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper futures on Shanghai Futures Exchange lower, with December contract briefly hitting 7% limit-down at opening; uncertain why contract fell sharply at open; trader, 2 analysts speculate sharp drop in opening minutes could be due to trading error, but another analyst notes such speculation common when there are sharp price moves. Adds pre-holiday profit taking also contributing to price decline, but copper may recoup losses over course of day, although "the downside tone is set for today"; tips nearby support for benchmark January contract at CNY58,500/ton on chart-based cues. December contract last down 0.8% at CNY60,140/ton, benchmark January contract last down 0.8% at CNY60,170/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW marginally lower at 1,141.0 vs 1,142.0 late yesterday in Seoul, edging slowly towards key 1,140 support on exporters' end-of-month USD selling, traders say. "Today, there's more dollar selling from exporters than from offshore players...the market is entirely dependent on the (corporate) dollar demand and supply situation, which will surely help the pair continually aim for a test below the 1,140 mark," says local bank trader. But another local trader notes \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively than before near 1,140 on view that a breach could lead pair to a speedy fall towards low 1,100s. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,307.30/oz, down $2.60 since NY close, attracting a little bit of selling in Asia after easing from new record high overnight. Serious correction though seems improbable at this stage with few willing sellers and solid support around $1,275 level, says Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney. "I still think the support is there for gold. It's hard to see anything substantial in terms of a sell-off right now," he says. Adds, overall weak trend in U.S. data supportive, but as soon as any consistent upturn in outlook for U.S. economy then gold could come under pressure. Tips $1,345 as realistic target in coming weeks with gold, bonds, equities all expected to rise in tandem for now. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY falls to intraday low just above 83.50 after marking high of 83.81 around daily fixing at 0100 GMT as short-term speculators squeeze long positions accumulated as market expected strong Japan importer bids, says senior dealer at major Japanese bank. Adds short-terms had also viewed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure strong fixing and sold when there was no sign of government JPY selling. Overall dollar weakness now expected to weigh on pair, dealer eyes 83.20-84.00 range in global day vs last 83.55. EUR/JPY falling after hitting intraday high at 114.23, last at 113.74. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 2339ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100930e69u0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
