\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/JPY Marks Intraday Low As Exporters Sell\b0\par\par\uc2 1,496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 05:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0301 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY falls to intraday low at 113.70 due to Japan exporters' JPY purchases after daily fixing around 0100 GMT as they face end of financial half year, says senior dealer at major Japan investment fund. Tips immediate technical support around current levels, but says if cross extends falls on continued pressure from exporters, it may drop to key 113.00 level. EUR/JPY last 113.70. EUR/USD falls in tandem to intraday low at 1.3596; dealer tips support for pair at 1.3570 vs 1.3603 last.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper futures on Shanghai Futures Exchange lower, with December contract briefly hitting 7% limit-down at opening; uncertain why contract fell sharply at open; trader, 2 analysts speculate sharp drop in opening minutes could be due to trading error, but another analyst notes such speculation common when there are sharp price moves. Adds pre-holiday profit taking also contributing to price decline, but copper may recoup losses over course of day, although "the downside tone is set for today"; tips nearby support for benchmark January contract at CNY58,500/ton on chart-based cues. December contract last down 0.8% at CNY60,140/ton, benchmark January contract last down 0.8% at CNY60,170/ton. (yue.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW marginally lower at 1,141.0 vs 1,142.0 late yesterday in Seoul, edging slowly towards key 1,140 support on exporters' end-of-month USD selling, traders say. "Today, there's more dollar selling from exporters than from offshore players...the market is entirely dependent on the (corporate) dollar demand and supply situation, which will surely help the pair continually aim for a test below the 1,140 mark," says local bank trader. But another local trader notes \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively than before near 1,140 on view that a breach could lead pair to a speedy fall towards low 1,100s. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,307.30/oz, down $2.60 since NY close, attracting a little bit of selling in Asia after easing from new record high overnight. Serious correction though seems improbable at this stage with few willing sellers and solid support around $1,275 level, says Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney. "I still think the support is there for gold. It's hard to see anything substantial in terms of a sell-off right now," he says. Adds, overall weak trend in U.S. data supportive, but as soon as any consistent upturn in outlook for U.S. economy then gold could come under pressure. Tips $1,345 as realistic target in coming weeks with gold, bonds, equities all expected to rise in tandem for now. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY falls to intraday low just above 83.50 after marking high of 83.81 around daily fixing at 0100 GMT as short-term speculators squeeze long positions accumulated as market expected strong Japan importer bids, says senior dealer at major Japanese bank. Adds short-terms had also viewed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ensure strong fixing and sold when there was no sign of government JPY selling. Overall dollar weakness now expected to weigh on pair, dealer eyes 83.20-84.00 range in global day vs last 83.55. EUR/JPY falling after hitting intraday high at 114.23, last at 113.74. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] India money markets including government bond, call market, also \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets shut Thursday due to half yearly account closing (ending September 30) by banks. However, equity markets will be open. When bond market resumes trading Friday, will watch for weekly inflation data for food, primary articles for week ended Sept 18, also INR110 billion auction for further cues. Benchmark 10-year bond yield ended at 7.85% yesterday. USD/INR at 44.94 in late trade Wednesday. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed in tight range, thin volumes, as traders mostly on sidelines ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly policy meeting later today, says local brokerage trader. Notes market widely expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike policy rate by 12.5 bps; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision is not likely to surprise the market." 10-year yield last at 1.2028% vs 1.2014% yesterday, eyed in 1.1900%-1.2100% band for day; 5-year yield last at 0.8750% vs 0.8850% yesterday, eyed in 0.8700%-0.8850% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly after PBOC reiterated yesterday it will maintain its moderately loose monetary policy, remarks soothed worries over possible interest rate hike to curb inflation, says Shenzhen-based analyst with local fund. "I expect Beijing won't hike interest rates in the coming six to eight months, as the country's economic growth will likely slow down, partly due to the tightening measures in property sector." Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.60 vs 126.58 last close, tipped to consolidate around 126.6 rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.7% at 9494.67 midday, just off intraday low of 9482.43, as banks fall (Topix banking subindex off 2.2%); MUFG (8306.TO) off 3.2% at Y391 and SMFG (8316.TO) off 3.0% at Y2,455 as their consumer lenders affiliates' credit default swap (CDS) levels rise; Nintendo (7974.OK) down 9.2% at Y20,890 after hitting fresh 2010 low on weak earnings concerns. "There are many reasons for stocks to fall," says market analyst at Japanese asset management firm, citing banks' weakness, Tepco capital raising, strong yen concerns, but adds market may still see support on hopes for government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (USD/JPY now 83.62). Nikkei may hover around 9500-line for remainder of day, he says. Tepco (9501.TO) off 3.5% at Y2,031 after announcing details of its massive capital raising plan; shares lost 7.8% Wednesday (now down 13% over last 3 sessions). Inpex (1605.TO) off 3.3% at Y394,500 after reports it has decided to withdraw from Azadegan oil field development project. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.6% at 22,255.42, modest profit-taking vs 9.0% rise month-to-yesterday, as downside limited by window-dressing activities ahead of end-3Q today. "We believe investors should prepare for a possible pull-back, given the inadequate consolidation after the sizeable advance of more than 1,800 points in the index so far this month," Taifook says in report. Notes, eye-catching performance of newly-listed stocks, which may stimulate further fund-raising activity in IPO sector, may drain some market liquidity. Adds, quality laggards should be a safe bet, with China Mobile (0941.HK) trading at undemanding valuation and seeking cross-media development opportunities, remains on house's recommendation list. China Mobile down 0.1% at HK$79.70, while ICBC (1398.HK) down 3.2% at HK$5.78 after stake cut by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS). Volume brisk at HK$9.88 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares flat at 