\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Down; Speculators Squeeze Longs After Fix\b0\par\par\uc2 1,506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 04:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY falls to intraday low just above 83.50 after marking high of 83.81 around daily fixing at 0100 GMT as short-term speculators squeeze long positions accumulated as market expected strong Japan importer bids, says senior dealer at major Japanese bank. Adds short-terms had also viewed that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  government might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ensure strong fixing and sold when there was no sign of government JPY selling. Overall dollar weakness now expected to weigh on pair, dealer eyes 83.20-84.00 range in global day vs last 83.55. EUR/JPY falling after hitting intraday high at 114.23, last at 113.74. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] India money markets including government bond, call market, also \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets shut Thursday due to half yearly account closing (ending September 30) by banks. However, equity markets will be open. When bond market resumes trading Friday, will watch for weekly inflation data for food, primary articles for week ended Sept 18, also INR110 billion auction for further cues. Benchmark 10-year bond yield ended at 7.85% yesterday. USD/INR at 44.94 in late trade Wednesday. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed in tight range, thin volumes, as traders mostly on sidelines ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly policy meeting later today, says local brokerage trader. Notes market widely expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike policy rate by 12.5 bps; "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision is not likely to surprise the market." 10-year yield last at 1.2028% vs 1.2014% yesterday, eyed in 1.1900%-1.2100% band for day; 5-year yield last at 0.8750% vs 0.8850% yesterday, eyed in 0.8700%-0.8850% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly after PBOC reiterated yesterday it will maintain its moderately loose monetary policy, remarks soothed worries over possible interest rate hike to curb inflation, says Shenzhen-based analyst with local fund. "I expect Beijing won't hike interest rates in the coming six to eight months, as the country's economic growth will likely slow down, partly due to the tightening measures in property sector." Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.60 vs 126.58 last close, tipped to consolidate around 126.6 rest of session. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.7% at 9494.67 midday, just off intraday low of 9482.43, as banks fall (Topix banking subindex off 2.2%); MUFG (8306.TO) off 3.2% at Y391 and SMFG (8316.TO) off 3.0% at Y2,455 as their consumer lenders affiliates' credit default swap (CDS) levels rise; Nintendo (7974.OK) down 9.2% at Y20,890 after hitting fresh 2010 low on weak earnings concerns. "There are many reasons for stocks to fall," says market analyst at Japanese asset management firm, citing banks' weakness, Tepco capital raising, strong yen concerns, but adds market may still see support on hopes for government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (USD/JPY now 83.62). Nikkei may hover around 9500-line for remainder of day, he says. Tepco (9501.TO) off 3.5% at Y2,031 after announcing details of its massive capital raising plan; shares lost 7.8% Wednesday (now down 13% over last 3 sessions). Inpex (1605.TO) off 3.3% at Y394,500 after reports it has decided to withdraw from Azadegan oil field development project. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0219 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.6% at 22,255.42, modest profit-taking vs 9.0% rise month-to-yesterday, as downside limited by window-dressing activities ahead of end-3Q today. "We believe investors should prepare for a possible pull-back, given the inadequate consolidation after the sizeable advance of more than 1,800 points in the index so far this month," Taifook says in report. Notes, eye-catching performance of newly-listed stocks, which may stimulate further fund-raising activity in IPO sector, may drain some market liquidity. Adds, quality laggards should be a safe bet, with China Mobile (0941.HK) trading at undemanding valuation and seeking cross-media development opportunities, remains on house's recommendation list. China Mobile down 0.1% at HK$79.70, while ICBC (1398.HK) down 3.2% at HK$5.78 after stake cut by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  (GS). Volume brisk at HK$9.88 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares flat at 452.92, with investors buying slowly on 3Q earnings hopes ahead of key government meeting later this week at which government expected to announce new financial measures to boost economy in 4Q. "Though optimism on 3Q earnings earnings remain, many players have decided to stay on the sidelines to wait for clear signals on how the government will act to support companies which are generally facing interest rates of above 15%," says Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Tips key index in 450-455 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to extend gains as portfolio inflows continue. Big-cap banking plays expected to be buoyant on optimism over 3Q earnings due to be released in 3rd week of October. "The stronger baht indicates that funds remain flowing into the Thai market," says Globlex Securities head of investment strategist Chakkrit Charoenmethachai. USD/THB last down to 30.41-30.43 early vs 30.47 late yesterday. Profit taking may emerge after recent gains. Eyes on environment activists' plan later today to stage protest against lower number of Cabinet-approved activities deemed harmful to environment and health - list down from 18 activities originally to 11. Resistance tipped at 980, support at 958. SET index ends +1.1% at fresh 14-year high 969.65 yesterday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] China's interest in North American shale gas set to intensify as plans for 2 liquefied natural gas export terminals in U.S., Canada move ahead. China lagging European companies such as \uc2 Total S.A\uc2 . (TOT), Asian peers such as \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) in making U.S. shale gas investments; likely cautious due to political fire storm that accompanied \uc2 China National Offshore Oil Corp\uc2 .'s 2005 $18.5 billion bid for Unocal Corp. China's overseas deals predicated on guaranteeing energy security so North American export projects make investments attractive. Deals would also give China's state companies much-needed access to shale gas drilling technology, expertise. See: =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Export Options Spur US Shale Gas Interest (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly lower, as falls in several Asian markets such as Japan, Singapore, South Korea will inspire profit taking after recent sharp gains, traders say; tip index in 3460-3520 range after shares yesterday end +0.7% at record high of 3495.464. But, trader at Lautandhana Securities, says "correction may be limited due to healthy inflows which have driven the rupiah to its 38-month high." Firmer IDR could help reduce local companies' foreign debts, imported raw material costs, analysts say. Coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) may continue to fall amid concerns new bonds issue to increase debt burdens, while \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) may drop on profit taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY up moderately, rebounding after 12 consecutive session of declines, after PBOC set fixing surprisingly high (6.7011 vs 6.6936 yesterday) despite USD weakness overnight; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6931 vs 6.6868 last close. Shanghai-based trader at local bank says U.S. House of Representatives' passage of bill to penalize China's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy may be factor in PBOC's guiding CNY weaker today. But mainly, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want people to speculate easily that it will let the yuan continue appreciating," trader says, adding there were signs of PBOC-guided flows over past two days supporting USD. "We think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely intends to have the dollar-yuan consolidate around 6.70 in the near term," he says. USD/CNY tipped in 6.6900-6.7000 for session. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.5420/6.5460 vs 6.5500/6.5550 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0202 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.235 vs 31.380 yesterday, weighed by exporters' month-end selling, but losses limited by bargain-hunting after pair hit 2-year low yesterday, says local bank trader. "Many people think the pair is attractive at current price levels," he says. Tips pair in 31.150-31.300 band for day. Warns \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow pair's drop if it weakens sharply. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 2236ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100930e69u0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
