\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Test 83.00; Real Money Sales Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 1,438 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 03:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may test 83.00 if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities don't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and if U.S. indicators such as weekly jobless claims, Chicago PMI due later in global day, turn out to be worse than expected, says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Japan for Barclays Bank. Worse-than-expected U.S. data would boost speculation that Fed may soon take additional quantitative easing measures, putting pressure on USD. Citing house analysis of global real money investors, notes broad USD sales expected vs JPY, GBP, AUD, CAD as they rebalance at end-month after outperformance in U.S. shares. USD/JPY last 83.61. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0116 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian property sector clearly undergoing a period of much-needed consolidation with prices down 0.2% in August, says Craig James, chief economist at Commonwealth Securities. Notes cumulative rate hikes, substantial boost in construction activity and double-digit growth in property prices over past year have given potential home buyers a valid reason to be more circumspect about future purchases. Adds housing sector cooling but not about to collapse. Adds property prices still up 8% on a year ago. Says population growth strong, vacancy rates low and unemployment sliding - all conducive for a more optimistic longer-term view of domestic housing market. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan August industrial output data, showing drop of 0.3% from previous month, indicate Japan may enter another recession, says HSBC Securities chief economist Seiji Shiraishi. "This is an extremely disappointing result." Median forecast of economists polled by Dow Jones was for 1.1% on-month rise. Shiraishi notes market expected slight rise due to rush in demand for cars as Japan government's eco-car subsidises were approaching end-September expiration. "We have not seen such a negative surprise for some time." Companies surveyed by trade ministry on average expect industrial output to fall 0.1% in September, and 2.9% in October. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea manufacturers' business outlook for October at 8-month low of 99 vs 104 in September, first fall below key 100-point level since February, reflecting escalating concerns over strength of global economic recovery. "A growing number of economic data show growth momentum is losing steam. The local economy may not be as brisk as before, at least until the second quarter of next year," says NH Investment & Securities economist Kim Jong-su. Adds, BOK unlikely to resume tightening cycle until end-this year, given spreading global economic uncertainties. (In-Soo.Nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may open lower after DJIA's modest falls, but index expected to remain mostly in 22,200-22,400 range, as profit-taking expected to be offset by window-dressing activities ahead of end-3Q today, says Conita Hung, head of research at Delta Asia; "(HSI) is likely to continue range-trading, I don't expect any strong downside in the index for now." Mainland developers expected to fall after China's State Council announces series of stricter measures to curb rising property prices. ICBC (1398.HK) set to fall after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  sold 2.75 billion shares at indicative range of HK$5.70-HK$5.79, or 3.0%-4.5% discount to ICBC's Wednesday close of HK$5.97, according to person familiar with deal. HSI ended +1.2% at 22,378.67 Wednesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0105 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to slide more on continuous inflows, test immediate floor 31.200, local trader says. Pair yesterday hit 31.211 -- lowest intraday since August 2008 -- before ending at 31.380, after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward end of session. "Apart from the general trend that funds are flowing to Asia, if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises interest rate today as expected, there will probably be more inflows." \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will review interest rate at quarterly meeting today, statement expected 0800 GMT; market expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike benchmark discount rate 12.5 bps to 1.5%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped in tight range, with still-weak sentiment for USD likely to be offset by possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; pair may slide to initial support at 43.80 after settling at 43.88 late yesterday, with resistance at 44.00. "We've seen the dollar touch PHP43.88 several times this year and it continues to hold because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We'll see if it will allow the peso to appreciate some more," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to fall further, extending continuous decline over past 12 sessions, on USD weakness overnight; USD/CNY fixing expected to set new low around 6.6900 vs 6.6936 yesterday; pair tipped in 6.6800-6.6950 vs 6.6868 last close, fresh settlement low under current system. Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank says USD/CNY downside maybe limited as traders may accumulate USD before week-long holiday starting tomorrow. "Also, we are watching whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step in to support the dollar," he says. Traders say there have been suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -guided flows over past 2 sessions, supporting USD. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to edge lower, following tepid U.S. stocks overnight; Taiex yesterday ends +0.6% ar 8240.89. KGI Securities analyst Randy Chang says near-term uptrend remains intact, tips Taiex has strong floor at 8200. Any drop likely lead by property, construction stocks, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tipped to raise policy rate by 12.5 bps after market close. But adds declines will probably be partly offset by gains in techs, supported by TSMC's (2330.TW) latest approval to upgrade a plant in China, while upbeat earnings guidance from \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  (HPQ) will also buoy PC stocks. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.5% at 4621.9, with financials, consumer staples and telecoms dragging on the market. In financials, all 4 heavyweight lenders lower, with ANZ (ANZ.AU) down 1.2%, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  down 0.8%, National Australian Bank (NAB.AU) off 0.5% and Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU) down 0.5%. "Concern over European banks and Ireland could be weighing on the market," says Peter Copeland, senior institutional trader at BBY in Sydney. Strikes have ground much of Spain to halt as workers in Europe crank up protests against austerity measures. Investors in Europe worried whether Ireland can fix problems at state-owned Anglo Irish Bank Corp. On the positive side, BHP (BHP.AU) gains slightly, up 3 cents at A$39.40, \uc2 Fortescue Metals\uc2  (FMG.AU) gains 1.2%. Copeland adds that school holidays in New South Wales and Victoria states mean light trading volumes. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0053 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely flat in absence of fresh cues, attention focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, trader at international bank says; tips yield curve to flatten on month-end extensions. "We don't expect much action in the bond markets. U.S. Treasurys sold overnight on mixed signals regarding another round of bond purchases to support the economy, but that's unlikely to have much impact on local bonds," trader says. Yesterday, yield on 2-year government bond unchanged at 0.41%, 10-year up 2 bps to 2.04%. (gaurav.raghuvnahi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may fall after Beijing launches long-anticipated second round of tightening measures to rein in housing prices. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to test support at 60-day moving average of 2,560, after ending flat at 2610.68 Wednesday. "The market will likely see a sharp drop in the morning as the new measures are harsher than expected, especially the cancellation of certain tax breaks," says Zhuo Xiangyu at China Securities Information. However, says clarity of policy may bring some relief to shares later in day. Beijing late Wednesday orders banks nationwide to halt lending for third, subsequent home purchases, raise minimum down payment for first-time home buyers, cancels certain tax breaks for individual first-home buyers if family members already own one or more homes. Shenzhen index down 1.2% at 1149.69. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 2122ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100930e69u00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
