\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Bounces; QE Fears Driving Dollar Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 11:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro recouped most of its earlier losses against the dollar Thursday as the latest twists in the euro zone debt crisis proved no worse than expected.\par \par \uc2 Instead, investors preferred to focus on positive labor market news from Germany and on the continued speculation that the U.S. will have to introduce more quantitative easing before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 This speculation will likely increase if the latest purchasing managers' index from the Chicago Fed declines as expected.\par \par \uc2 The euro started the day off on the slide, falling as low as $1.3562 at one stage as the news on Spain and Ireland rolled in.\par \par \uc2 However, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fortunes gradually changed after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  announced that it was downgrading Spain's credit rating to Aa1. The move had been widely expected and proved no worse than forecast. If anything, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  decision to leave the rating stable was seen as positive.\par \par \uc2 Ireland, meanwhile, announced an additional EUR29 billion of extra funding for \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  taking the total of its bail out up to EUR34 billion. This was at the top end of market expectations but, again, proved no worse than the market expected.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  got additional impetus from news that the number of jobless in Germany fell by 40,000 this month, or double the 20,000 decline that had been expected. Also from data showing that euro-zone inflation had risen back to 1.8% in September, its highest level since November 2008, from 1.6% in August\par \par \uc2 See how the euro bounced against the dollar:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4654"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4654}}}\par \par \uc2 However, further euro gains could be limited ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's refunding exercise later in the day. The market is looking to see if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to continue pumping as much liquidity into the money markets.\par \par \uc2 Despite all these euro-zone issues, analysts suggested that the euro's performance against the dollar is still being driven largely by uncertainty over U.S. monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 A recent deterioration in U.S. economic data suggests that the Fed may yet have to resort to further quantitative easing to prevent a double dip recession. However, Fed officials themselves remain uncertain whether this will be needed with speeches by three different Fed governors on Wednesday expressing three different views on the subject.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also found itself under pressure against the yen, despite data from Japan showing that both retail sales and industrial production are softer than the market had hoped.\par \par \uc2 However, investors are reluctant to push the dollar much lower because of fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls under Y83.00. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to about Y82.95.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the dollar was down at Y83.26 from Y83.70 late on Wednesday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 After its early fall, the euro bounced back to trade at $1.3632, hardly changed from $1.3630 late in New York.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell to Y113.50 from Y114.07 while the dollar eased back a little to CHF0.9765 from CHF0.9778.\par \par \uc2 The pound rose to $1.5890 from $1.5787 after Nationwide reported that house prices rose 0.1% this month.\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires, 44 20 7842 9493;\par \par \uc2 nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 30-09-10 0916GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100930e69u000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
