\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ INTERVIEW: Japan Economy Minister Says "Decisive"  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps Possible, But Short Term\b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 916 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 13:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's economy minister said Wednesday the government remains ready to take "decisive" action to stop the sharp appreciation of the yen--which has resumed its climb since Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks ago--but that such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a "temporary measure."\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires, Economic and Fiscal Policy Minister Banri Kaieda also stressed that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to float on the open market, in contrast with the Chinese yuan.\par \par \uc2 While Kaieda isn't in charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, which is controlled by Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, his remarks suggest that while Tokyo maintains its resolve to fight unduly sharp yen gains, it may not be as aggressive as some had thought in resisting the general upward trend.\par \par \uc2 Noda has repeatedly said Japan would take "decisive steps," but has declined to discuss how long any market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may last.\par \par \uc2 Kaieda's comments came after the dollar briefly fell to Y83.50 in Asian trading, the lowest since Sept. 15 when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in more than six years to stem the yen's rise. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stubborn strength threatens the nation's export-driven economic recovery and reduces corporate profits when overseas earnings are repatriated.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's latest tankan survey of business sentiment released earlier in the day showed that big manufacturers remained optimistic for the July-September period, but were much more guarded for the next quarter due the surging yen and uncertainty about the global economy. Before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen rose to a 15-year high, with the dollar falling to Y82.87.\par \par \uc2 In addition to their overall negativity going forward, companies also said they had based their business plans on a dollar rate of Y89.66 for this financial year, much higher than the current level.\par \par \uc2 Firms' worsening outlook is "partly due to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Kaieda said. "If the yen stays at current levels or if it strengthens a bit more, the situation will become more serious."\par \par \uc2 The yen has strengthened partly because of concerns over the economic slowdown in the U.S., feeding speculation that this may prompt the Federal Reserve to take aggressive measures to add funds to the financial system.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the yen's rise is in line with economic conditions, making it difficult to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . IMF Managing Director Dominique Strauss-Kahn said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other countries "have nothing to gain" by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets, Kyodo News reported.\par \par \uc2 Kaieda, however, said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been a useful tool.\par \par \uc2 Before the action, the yen's gains were "very rapid, and I believed inflows of speculative money were behind it," he said.\par \par \uc2 He also suggested the global economy wouldn't benefit from severe problems in Japan.\par \par \uc2 "If Japan's economy regains stability and steadily recovers, that will not necessarily be negative for the world's economy. It's important for us to clearly explain that point to other countries and gain their understanding."\par \par \uc2 Kaieda drew a distinction between Japan's short-term steps and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, under which the yuan is held within a tight band.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "was an emergency escape, a temporary measure," Kaieda said. "It doesn't mean that we intend to artificially skew the market through continued (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't undermine its legitimacy in urging Beijing to let the yuan trade more freely, he said.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the economic impact of the dispute with China following a collision of ships in the East China Sea, Kaieda said a disruption of imports of rare earth minerals used in making solar panels and hybrid vehicles has reminded Japan of the importance of diversifying resource imports.\par \par \uc2 He noted that while China never formally suspended rare earth exports in retaliation for the detention of the ship's captain, actual shipments for Japan have been held up. Japan is still studying the situation and has yet to confirm Japanese media reports that shipments have resumed. Kaieda said it would be "good news" if confirmed.\par \par \uc2 Relying exclusively on China for rare earth supplies isn't ideal from the standpoint of resource national security," he said. Japan should diversify sources by accelerating the development of alternatives materials, finding sources outside China, and acquiring mines overseas.\par \par \uc2 Kaieda, a 61-year-old economic commentator-turned politician, also took a strong line on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should do more to stimulate the economy.\par \par \uc2 "I think there remain several steps" the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can take, he said, declining to elaborate. "I'm hoping the BOJ will act from the monetary side to provide steady support for the government to achieve their stated goal of pulling the economy out of deflation."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan will hold a policy meeting Oct. 4-5 to discuss possible additional easing measures. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long said that while it is open to further action, many of the proposals from politicians and private economists are unwise for the long-term economic good. Among these, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rejected setting an inflation target or buying large amounts of government debt to pump cash into the economy.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Yuka Hayashi of The Wall Street Journal contributed to this article)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2010 07:28 ET (11:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100929e69t002g8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
