\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-BOJ tankan outlook worsens, points to policy easing\b0\par\par\uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 02:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Big manufacturers' index improves to +8 vs forecast +6\par \par \uc2 * 2010/11 capex plans +2.4 pct versus forecast +4.2 pct\par \par \uc2 * Big manufacturers glum about outlook\par \par \uc2 * BOJ has been divided over how much monetary easing needed (Adds details, economist's quote)\par \par \uc2 By Rie Ishiguro and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 29 (Reuters) - Japanese manufacturers turned more pessimistic about their outlook for the first time in almost two years this quarter in a sign that a strong yen could derail the economic recovery and spur the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease policy next week.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan tankan survey for the three months to September showed big firms planned to raise capital spending at slower pace than expected as a recovery in exports slows due to a strong yen and slackening overseas demand.\par \par \uc2 The headline sentiment index improved to 8 from 1 in June, exceeding the median market forecast of 6. But the index is seen sliding to minus 1 in December, showing firms expect conditions to worsen over the next three months and that pessimists outnumber optimists.\par \par \uc2 This was the first time big manufacturers expected the outlook to worsen since December 2008.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has struggled to agree whether it should further relax policy at the Oct. 4-5 meeting, but firms' cautious views and subdued spending plans could tip the balance in favour of those calling for more easing.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month to weaken the yen, and some economists say additional monetary easing is needed to ensure that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeds in shielding the economy from a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ was really focused on corporate sentiment and the yen strength is an obvious factor. They'll be under greater pressure to ease from the government ... at the meeting next week," said Naomi Fink, Japan strategist at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 PRESSURE TO EASE\par \par \uc2 The lead 10-year Japanese government bond futures rose to a seven-year high on increased prospects for BOJ easing, including a possible boost in bond purchases, while the Nikkei stock average climbed 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 The yen largely took the tankan in stride, however, trading at 83.87 yen to the dollar, little changed from just before the data's release.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold the yen in the market on Sept. 15 for the first time in more than six years after its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged to a 15-year high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The September tankan survey was conducted from Aug. 23 to Sept. 28, but it did not capture the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because 70 to 80 percent of firms responded by the preliminary deadline on Sept. 7, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Big manufacturers' sentiment gauge in September improved for a sixth straight quarter to its highest since March 2008, while big service-sector firms' confidence was the highest since June 2008.\par \par \uc2 However, big firms' plan to increase capital spending by 2.4 percent in the financial year to March 2011 came below the market's median forecast for a 4.2 percent rise and the survey did little to ease concerns about a strong yen.\par \par \uc2 Big manufacturers expect the dollar to average 89.66 yen in the fiscal year that started in April, which may be overly optimistic.\par \par \uc2 "The expected exchange rate is still above current levels, so \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  could pose downside risks to the corporate outlook," said Yasuo Yamamoto, senior economist at Mizuho Research Institute in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is likely to extend the duration of its funding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a return to zero interest rates is also possible."\par \par \uc2 Sources have told Reuters that the BOJ could loosen policy if the recovery is deemed by the board to be under threat, with an increase in government bond buying and a further boost to its cheap fund-supply tool emerging as the most likely options.\par \par \uc2 Still, some BOJ officials have indicated that they want to wait until there is further evidence that the strong yen and slowing overseas growth are hurting the economy.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ may also be reluctant to pursue aggressive easing because its decision could end up having limited impact if the Federal Reserve eases policy at its next rate review on Nov. 2-3, causing the yen to appreciate further against the dollar. (Additional reporting by Kaori Kaneko; Writing by Stanley White; Editing by Edmund Klamann and Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100929e69t0000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
