\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Falls Vs Euro; Eyes On Fed's Next Move\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew J. Johnson   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 21:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell against most of its major rivals Wednesday on the assumption that Federal Reserve stimulus will create cheap dollars faster than any other country's issues can dominate the conversation.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fell Wednesday to its lowest level since January, though it recouped some of its losses by afternoon trading.\par \par \uc2 The dollar stands at its lowest level against the euro since April, and has solidly breached the $1.36 mark.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen every day this week, and has declined roughly 7.5% since Sept. 13, said Brian Dolan, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\par \par \uc2 The dollar hit Y83.50 and is trading at its lowest levels since the Japanese government's massive Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen against the Swiss franc to levels last seen in March 2008, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 "A short-term viewpoint is dominating the market now," said Marc Chandler, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday afternoon, the euro was at $1.3643 from $1.3590 from late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.62 from Y83.89, while the euro was at Y114.09 from Y114.00. The U.K. pound was at $1.5791 from $1.5802. The dollar was at CHF0.9767 from CHF0.9760.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index was at 78.691 from 78.904.\par \par \uc2 Investors understand the euro zone remains under pressure from periphery countries like Portugal and Ireland, but those nations seem small when compared to what the euro zone has already confronted with Greece, so those issues have been mostly cast aside for now, he said.\par \par \uc2 Right now, there are almost no other issues investors care about besides the possibility of an increasingly cheap dollar, he said.\par \par \uc2 It isn't only the euro that will continue to gain from more risk taking caused by a cheap dollar, said Beat Siegenthaler, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in Zurich.\par \par \uc2 "The Swiss franc will be a major beneficiary of any additional QE measures," he said, referring to the asset purchases known as quantitative easing. "Together with gold, it is the ultimate safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a world that fears \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debasement and competitive devaluation."\par \par \uc2 The euro had earlier ticked town against the dollar on hands-off signals from European leaders.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't set its policies solely for the sake of its vulnerable member countries, a top official signaled, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bases decisions on the 16-member bloc as a whole, not those on the extremes.\par \par \uc2 "We at the ECB deal with the euro-zone averages," Bank of Slovenia Governor Marko Kranjec said in an interview at his office in the Slovenian capital.\par \par \uc2 European leaders appear optimistic, but some euro investors are nonetheless hedging their bets and feel they may be too optimistic, said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in London. This caution keeps the euro's upside limited, he said.\par \par \uc2 The greenback dropped Wednesday in Tokyo to its lowest levels since Japan's first foray into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in more than six years. The dollar recouped some of those losses, but investors remained on high alert for signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem yen strength.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy minister, Banri Kaieda, said Wednesday the government remains ready to take "decisive" action to stop the sharp appreciation of the yen, but that such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a "temporary measure."\par \par \uc2 Kaieda drew a distinction between \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term steps and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "was an emergency escape, a temporary measure," Kaieda said. "It doesn't mean that we intend to artificially skew the market through continued (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )."\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew J. Johnson, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-3092; andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Takashi Nakamichi in Tokyo and Brian Blackstone in Ljubljana, Slovenia contributed to this article.) [ 09-29-10 1527ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t000ct\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
