\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:China Wants Smart Grid,But Not Too Smart -WSJ BLOG\b0\par\par\uc2 1,289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 10:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] Whoever figures out how much electricity China uses stands to make lots of money. China in process of adopting smart electrical grid, including Advanced Metering Infrastructure, which includes equipment such as meters for residences, companies. China needs to update aging equipment, better link power producers, which tend to be in northwest, with users, who tend to be in coastal southeast. Offers major business opportunities for foreign equipment suppliers such as \uc2 General Electric\uc2  (GE), \uc2 Siemens\uc2  (SIE.XE), \uc2 Alcatel-Lucent\uc2  (ALU). GE estimated market at $60 billion over next decade. But as in other sectors, foreign companies may need to tailor products for China, lower prices, risk having proprietary technology stolen by local competitors. But foreign suppliers should not think too smart, or too pricey, according to one prominent Chinese industry researcher. Hu Xuehao, principal expert at China Electric Power Research Institute, says leading AMI technology being developed in U.S., Europe often much more advanced than China requires. See: =WSJ BLOG/China Real Time: China Wants Smart Grid, But Not Too Smart (james.areddy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0802 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/GBP managed a close just above its 200DMA at 0.8589 but to suggests that the current gains are sustainable it needs to climb above the channel high of 0.8630, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . That move would then have a target of 0.8730 or even 0.90, says the bank. On the downside, support at 0.8465 should contain any pullbacks, says BarCap. EUR/GBP now at 0.8605 from a fresh 4-month high of 0.8612. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0801 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD weakens further after disappointing US data increases talk of more monetary easing. Elsewhere, an improvement in Japan's latest Tankan survey and a rise in China's latest manufacturing PMI to a five-month high has lifted market sentiment and pushed the EUR over $1.36. Although the market's focus is very much on Fed policy, suggestions that the ECB will be able to start unwinding its unconventional liquidity could come under scrutiny too as several ECB officials give speeches. The USD is down at Y83.66 while the EUR is up at $1.3618. The GBP is up at $1.5827. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] MYR, Asia's best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year (up 11% vs USD), may rise further ahead of key milestones for Malaysia's ambitious economic reform plans -- as long as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sticks to policy of not curtailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. Malaysia's government expected to present 2011 annual budget on Oct. 15; also in October, Petronas Petrochemicals Group Bhd is slated to complete country's largest-ever IPO. That's a favorable environment for MYR, which hit its highest level since the Asian crisis Wednesday at MYR3.0800. BarCap last week raised ringgit forecast, now expects USD/MYR to trade at 2.9200 in a year's time vs 3.0500 previous forecast. Some anticipate faster gains, with USD/MYR possibly falling to 3.0000 as early as December. See: =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Malaysian Ringgit May Sail Past \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Threat (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR still near-five month lows, but witnesses slight recovery as demand for greenback kicks in from importers, says trader with private bank. Pair at 44.93 after slipping to 44.84 early session vs 45.16 late Tuesday. "The rupee is being supported by strength in the similarly risk-sensitive euro even though local stock markets are showing losses," trader says. Adds, pair not budging much from around 44.92 levels on equal buy-sell pressure from importers and custodial banks. Tips pair in 44.85-44.95 band in session. Sensex down 0.5%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] The current yield level of around 3.92% of the Italian government bond BTP 3.75% March 2021, which is on auction Wednesday with two other bonds, should be "quite appealing" to investors, especially insurance companies that have to guarantee minimum returns, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  strategist Chiara Cremonesi. Italy also auctions the BTP 2% June 2013, and with a new three-year BTP expected at the next auction in October, the BTP June 2013, also on auction Wednesday, "could thus benefit from this factor over the next few weeks," Cremonesi says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] General USD weakness will keep USD/JPY heading lower, especially after the Tankan survey. But, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  down towards 83.00 will frustrate any heavy USD/JPY sales. The pair is now at 83.65. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures +1.1% at 22,380, in line with cash market's 1.1% rise. Contract trading at consistent premium to cash throughout session, now around 30 points, continuing to flag bullish view on market's near-term direction. Volume modest at 57,130 lots. September HSI futures, expiring today, +1.2% at 22,381. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end tad higher as local stocks turn lower, reverse earlier gains. Shanghai exchange government bond index end at 126.58 vs 126.56 last close; cash-rich investors increasing bond positions for coupon earnings during week-long National Day holiday which starts Friday, says Shenzhen-based analyst with local insurer. Tips bond index in narrow 126.5-126.6 band tomorrow. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0748 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS up after \uc2 HSBC\uc2  September China PMI rises to 5-month high. 1-year +3 bps at 2.13%-2.23%, 5-year +3 bps at 2.87%-2.97%. "The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI eased concerns that China's policy to encourage energy saving and carbon emission reduction had hurt small and medium enterprises," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. But notes, not sure whether swaps have further upside as U.S. treasury yields are falling. HSBC China PMI up at 52.9 in September vs 51.9 in August. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is stronger as USD weakens against some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/TRY trades at 1.4615, from 1.4690 Tuesday. Bonds are also stronger following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement Tuesday that it may cut borrowing rates again. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond trades at 8.08%, from 8.15% Tuesday. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.4590 and 1.5020, and the benchmark bond between 8.00% and 8.20%, in the short term. (sezin.bala@iha.com.tr)\par \par \uc2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF may prefer to wait for even slight respite in USD's current broad-based slide before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on JPY, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Japanese bank. That may explain why MOF has so far not ordered up USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, despite USD/JPY dropping to 2-week low at 83.50, lowest since Tokyo launched its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than 6 years on Sept. 15. "For the moment it may make more sense for Japan to forcefully reiterate its resolve to respond to any sharp yen gains," trader says. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now in the middle of this broad dollar weakness, it may not have the desired effect." USD/JPY last 83.58. USD Index earlier fell to 78.616, lowest since January 28.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0403ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
