\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: MYR May Sail Past  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Threat -  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,221 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 09:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0757 GMT [Dow Jones] MYR, Asia's best-performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year (up 11% vs USD), may rise further ahead of key milestones for Malaysia's ambitious economic reform plans -- as long as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sticks to policy of not curtailing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength. Malaysia's government expected to present 2011 annual budget on Oct. 15; also in October, Petronas Petrochemicals Group Bhd is slated to complete country's largest-ever IPO. That's a favorable environment for MYR, which hit its highest level since the Asian crisis Wednesday at MYR3.0800. BarCap last week raised ringgit forecast, now expects USD/MYR to trade at 2.9200 in a year's time vs 3.0500 previous forecast. Some anticipate faster gains, with USD/MYR possibly falling to 3.0000 as early as December. See: =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ASIA: Malaysian Ringgit May Sail Past \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Threat (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0756 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR still near-five month lows, but witnesses slight recovery as demand for greenback kicks in from importers, says trader with private bank. Pair at 44.93 after slipping to 44.84 early session vs 45.16 late Tuesday. "The rupee is being supported by strength in the similarly risk-sensitive euro even though local stock markets are showing losses," trader says. Adds, pair not budging much from around 44.92 levels on equal buy-sell pressure from importers and custodial banks. Tips pair in 44.85-44.95 band in session. Sensex down 0.5%. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] The current yield level of around 3.92% of the Italian government bond BTP 3.75% March 2021, which is on auction Wednesday with two other bonds, should be "quite appealing" to investors, especially insurance companies that have to guarantee minimum returns, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  strategist Chiara Cremonesi. Italy also auctions the BTP 2% June 2013, and with a new three-year BTP expected at the next auction in October, the BTP June 2013, also on auction Wednesday, "could thus benefit from this factor over the next few weeks," Cremonesi says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] General USD weakness will keep USD/JPY heading lower, especially after the Tankan survey. But, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  down towards 83.00 will frustrate any heavy USD/JPY sales. The pair is now at 83.65. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] October HSI futures +1.1% at 22,380, in line with cash market's 1.1% rise. Contract trading at consistent premium to cash throughout session, now around 30 points, continuing to flag bullish view on market's near-term direction. Volume modest at 57,130 lots. September HSI futures, expiring today, +1.2% at 22,381. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end tad higher as local stocks turn lower, reverse earlier gains. Shanghai exchange government bond index end at 126.58 vs 126.56 last close; cash-rich investors increasing bond positions for coupon earnings during week-long National Day holiday which starts Friday, says Shenzhen-based analyst with local insurer. Tips bond index in narrow 126.5-126.6 band tomorrow. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0748 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS up after \uc2 HSBC\uc2  September China PMI rises to 5-month high. 1-year +3 bps at 2.13%-2.23%, 5-year +3 bps at 2.87%-2.97%. "The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI eased concerns that China's policy to encourage energy saving and carbon emission reduction had hurt small and medium enterprises," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. But notes, not sure whether swaps have further upside as U.S. treasury yields are falling. HSBC China PMI up at 52.9 in September vs 51.9 in August. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is stronger as USD weakens against some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/TRY trades at 1.4615, from 1.4690 Tuesday. Bonds are also stronger following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement Tuesday that it may cut borrowing rates again. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond trades at 8.08%, from 8.15% Tuesday. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.4590 and 1.5020, and the benchmark bond between 8.00% and 8.20%, in the short term. (sezin.bala@iha.com.tr)\par \par \uc2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF may prefer to wait for even slight respite in USD's current broad-based slide before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on JPY, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Japanese bank. That may explain why MOF has so far not ordered up USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, despite USD/JPY dropping to 2-week low at 83.50, lowest since Tokyo launched its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than 6 years on Sept. 15. "For the moment it may make more sense for Japan to forcefully reiterate its resolve to respond to any sharp yen gains," trader says. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now in the middle of this broad dollar weakness, it may not have the desired effect." USD/JPY last 83.58. USD Index earlier fell to 78.616, lowest since January 28.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is strong across the board Wednesday with the threat of more QE from the U.S. and UK helping the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its way. EUR/USD printed a fresh 5 month high of 1.3639, EUR/GBP a 4 month high of 0.8612, EUR/CHF a one week high of 1.3310 and EUR/JPY a near 2 month high of 114.20. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones] Falling U.S. yields are keeping pressure on USD/JPY notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . However the bank says with the spot now at 83.50 further weakness is likely to be laboured as it approaches \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. The recent low for USD/JPY was 82.87 on September 15 when the BOJ stepped in and triggered a near 300sen rally. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 0.5% at 19,998.24 in volatile trade as traders tread cautiously before monthly derivatives expiry tomorrow. Sensex off 20,234.05 early high, 20,009.12 low. 17 of 30 index issues lower. "Barring some action in news driven stocks, the overall mood is cautious before the expiry," says local trader. Adds, recent sharp rally (over 11% so far this month), expensive valuations at over 19X FY11 earnings leaves limited headroom for further gains. Says, July-September quarter results starting middle of next month, global economic newsflow to be watched for direction medium term. For day tips Sensex in 19,940-20,120 range. ITC (500875.BY) down 2.2% at INR174.55, ONGC (500112.BY) off 1.5% at INR1,433.85. HDFC (500010.BY) down 1.1% at INR706, Larsen (500510.By) off 0.7% at INR2,032. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.8% at INR1,098.45, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1% at INR658.30. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0357ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par