\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Broad USD Moves Key To Any MOF JPY Steps - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,239 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 09:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF may prefer to wait for even slight respite in USD's current broad-based slide before \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again on JPY, says senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at Japanese bank. That may explain why MOF has so far not ordered up USD-buying, JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday, despite USD/JPY dropping to 2-week low at 83.50, lowest since Tokyo launched its first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in more than 6 years on Sept. 15. "For the moment it may make more sense for Japan to forcefully reiterate its resolve to respond to any sharp yen gains," trader says. "If \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now in the middle of this broad dollar weakness, it may not have the desired effect." USD/JPY last 83.58. USD Index earlier fell to 78.616, lowest since January 28.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro is strong across the board Wednesday with the threat of more QE from the U.S. and UK helping the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its way. EUR/USD printed a fresh 5 month high of 1.3639, EUR/GBP a 4 month high of 0.8612, EUR/CHF a one week high of 1.3310 and EUR/JPY a near 2 month high of 114.20. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0740 GMT [Dow Jones] Falling U.S. yields are keeping pressure on USD/JPY notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . However the bank says with the spot now at 83.50 further weakness is likely to be laboured as it approaches \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. The recent low for USD/JPY was 82.87 on September 15 when the BOJ stepped in and triggered a near 300sen rally. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex down 0.5% at 19,998.24 in volatile trade as traders tread cautiously before monthly derivatives expiry tomorrow. Sensex off 20,234.05 early high, 20,009.12 low. 17 of 30 index issues lower. "Barring some action in news driven stocks, the overall mood is cautious before the expiry," says local trader. Adds, recent sharp rally (over 11% so far this month), expensive valuations at over 19X FY11 earnings leaves limited headroom for further gains. Says, July-September quarter results starting middle of next month, global economic newsflow to be watched for direction medium term. For day tips Sensex in 19,940-20,120 range. ITC (500875.BY) down 2.2% at INR174.55, ONGC (500112.BY) off 1.5% at INR1,433.85. HDFC (500010.BY) down 1.1% at INR706, Larsen (500510.By) off 0.7% at INR2,032. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.8% at INR1,098.45, \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +1% at INR658.30. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0738 GMT [Dow Jones] The market won't only be looking to see what Fed members Kocherlakota, Plosser and Rosengren have to say about QE policy when they speak later today, but it will also be keeping a keen eye on whether ECB members are all so optimistic about their \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  being able to wind down its own unconventional liquidity. Bini-Smaghi, Constancio, Weber and Gonzalez-Paramo are all slated to talk Wednesday, too. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] Low bidding at the ECB's 3-month longer term refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday could push Eonia and other short term rates higher, suggesting lower demand by banks or a shift into the 6-day tender. "Both of them would facilitate the ECB resuming its exit strategy by allowing it to drop the full allotment over time at the 3M LTRO," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] Lack of flows in the Italian government bond BTP market and the recently completed 15-year BTP syndicated deal--which was priced Sept. 22--may limit demand at Italy's EUR5 billion to EUR8 billion auction of three bonds Wednesday, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  analyst Elaine Lin. She notes there has not been much of a concession built in the paper on offer. Italy sells BTP 2% June 2013, BTP 3.75% March 2021 and floating rate CCT December 2015, results are due at 0900 GMT. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] The rand is eyeing 6.90 to a softer dollar in early trade, tracking the euro. USD/ZAR at 6.9499 from a close of 6.9340. "The dollar is being lashed again. There might however come a point where we say the rand is stretched, but for now the target is 6.90 against the dollar and is dependent on whether the euro breaks through 1.37" against the dollar, a trader says. (I-Net Bridge)\par \par \uc2 0730 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may be rising as "the only game in town is the debate on the likelihood of further monetary easing by the Fed," says Credit Agricole. But, the bank reckons, this shouldn't last through the November FOMC especially as there are plenty of local reasons to sell the EUR. "Our bullishness on the EUR is an opportunistic one, not one built on any lasting assessment of its relative merits vis a vis the USD," the bank says. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0728 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR lower late at 3.0830 vs 3.0915 late Tuesday, after hitting fresh 13-year low of 3.0810 intraday, on broad USD weakness. Two dealers say Bank Negara spotted buying dollars at 3.0830 in small doses to steady MYR's rise. Dealer expects pair to continue downtrend Thursday; albeit at slower pace as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. Tips pair to trade between 3.0800-3.0900 Thursday. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts fall Wednesday, reversing some of the gains made in the previous session as stocks open higher. Comments arguing for more quantitative easing by the BOE's Posen lifted gilts on Tuesday and analysts said a 3-way split in the MPC was looking increasingly likely. Recent economic data in the UK have largely fallen short of estimates, and the BOE's mortgage and consumer lending figures due at 0830 GMT will be closely eyed. December gilts are down 0.12 at 124.37 and the 10-year gilt is down at 114.891 to yield 2.93%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0725 GMT [Dow Jones]Malaysia government bonds higher late, with short-term paper dominating market volume amid rising MYR, says dealer with local bank; USD/MYR last at 3.0830 vs 3.0915 late Tuesday. "GIIs (or Islamic bonds) also attracted interest after the new 3-year benchmark was priced at a average yield of 3.199%, slightly below the range of 3.200%-3.230% the market had expected." Tips yields to remain rangebound Thursday with downside bias. Yields on August 2015 MGS down 1 bp at 3.24%, September 2017 MGS down 1 bp at 3.51% and November 2019 MGS flat at 3.62%. Among Islamic bonds, July 2013 GII down 2 bps at 3.23%, February 2014 GII down 4 bps at 3.28% and June 2020 GII down 1 bp at 3.38%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0344ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t0005c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
